  ('b885a8ab-996f-5b28-9e88-d9965dd460a5', 'P001', 'person', 'cleopatra-vii', 'Cleopatra VII', 'Last active ruler of the Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt.', 'draft', 'Where was the capital city of this famous Egyptian queen?', 31.2001, 29.9187, 'Alexandria, Egypt', 'This is Alexandria, Egypt, the capital city of Queen Cleopatra VII. She was the last ruler from a Greek family that had ruled Egypt for almost 300 years.', 'Death / Battle of Actium aftermath', 'When did this powerful queen of Egypt die?', '10 August 30 BC', -30, 'day', 'Cleopatra died on August 10, 30 BC. Her family had ruled Egypt for centuries, but she was the first of them to learn to speak Egyptian.', '4 life events span 52-30 BC (22 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match, not Order.', 'Ptolemaic Egypt', 'Cleopatra was succeeded by her young son Caesarion (Ptolemy XV) as nominal pharaoh for only a few weeks before Octavian had him executed and formally annexed Egypt as a Roman province.', 'Cleopatra belonged to the Macedonian Greek Ptolemaic dynasty and is traditionally described as the first of her line, after nearly three centuries of rule, to learn to speak the Egyptian language.', 'She had children with both Julius Caesar (a son, Caesarion) and Mark Antony (the twins Alexander Helios and Cleopatra Selene II, plus a younger son, Ptolemy Philadelphus).', 'Ptolemaic Kingdom of Egypt (Egypt)', 'Africa', null, 'Wikipedia: Cleopatra (birth ~69/70 BC Alexandria; death 10/12 Aug 30 BC Alexandria); History.com (Actium 2 Sep 31 BC, death 10 Aug 30 BC).'),
  ('6eae1c24-aeb8-54a3-8e02-7ea6d7cd2b6a', 'P002', 'person', 'albert-einstein', 'Albert Einstein', 'Theoretical physicist who developed the theory of relativity.', 'draft', 'Where was this famous scientist born?', 48.3974, 9.9934, 'Ulm, Germany', 'This is Ulm, Germany, the birthplace of Albert Einstein. He grew up to change how we understand space and time.', 'Annus Mirabilis (special relativity published)', 'In what year did this scientist publish his ideas about relativity?', '1905', 1905, 'year', 'In 1905, Einstein shared his ideas about relativity with the world. That same year, he also explained the photoelectric effect, and this work later won him a Nobel Prize.', '4 career milestones span 1902-1921 (19 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match, not Order.', '20th Century', 'Einstein''s general theory of relativity, published in 1915, superseded Isaac Newton''s law of universal gravitation as the accepted framework for describing gravity.', 'Einstein won the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics not for relativity but ''for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect.''', 'As a teenager Einstein renounced his German citizenship and was stateless for about five years before becoming a Swiss citizen in 1901; he later also took U.S. citizenship in 1940.', 'German Empire (Germany)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia: Albert Einstein (born 14 Mar 1879 Ulm; died 18 Apr 1955 Princeton; 1902 Bern patent office; 1905 special relativity; 1915 general relativity Berlin; 1921 Nobel Prize).'),
  ('39c544e3-657a-5e17-a4c7-da35624e1f63', 'P003', 'person', 'christopher-columbus', 'Christopher Columbus', 'Genoese explorer whose 1492 voyage opened sustained European contact with the Americas.', 'draft', 'Where did this bold explorer come from?', 44.4056, 8.9463, 'Genoa, Republic of Genoa (Italy)', 'This is Genoa, in what is now Italy, the hometown of Christopher Columbus. He later sailed west across the ocean and reached the Americas.', 'Landfall in the Americas', 'When did this explorer first reach land in the Americas?', '12 October 1492', 1492, 'day', 'Columbus landed in the Americas on October 12, 1492. He made four trips across the ocean, but he always believed he had found islands near Asia.', '4 life events span 1479-1506 (27 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match, not Order.', 'Age of Exploration', 'Columbus was stripped of his governorship of the Indies when Francisco de Bobadilla arrived to replace him in 1500, and after Columbus''s death in 1506 his titles and legal claims passed to his son Diego Columbus.', 'Columbus made four voyages across the Atlantic between 1492 and 1504, but he died still believing he had reached islands off the coast of Asia rather than a previously unknown continent.', 'Columbus died in Valladolid, Spain in 1506 in relative obscurity and financial dispute with the Spanish crown, still petitioning for the wealth and titles he believed he was owed under his original contract.', 'Republic of Genoa (Italy)', 'Europe', 'Review - colonization content; treat aftermath neutrally per sensitivity guidelines.', 'Wikipedia: Christopher Columbus (born between 25 Aug-31 Oct 1451 Genoa; died 20 May 1506 Valladolid; departed Palos 3 Aug 1492; landfall Bahamas 12 Oct 1492; arrested 1500).'),
  ('d9ce3fde-129e-557d-a9c1-e36576048335', 'P004', 'person', 'leonardo-da-vinci', 'Leonardo da Vinci', 'Renaissance painter, engineer, and polymath.', 'draft', 'In what town was this Renaissance genius born?', 43.7833, 10.9333, 'Vinci, Republic of Florence (Italy)', 'This is the small town of Vinci in Italy, the birthplace of Leonardo da Vinci. He grew up to become a famous painter and inventor.', 'Began painting the Mona Lisa', 'In what year did this artist start painting his most famous portrait?', '1503', 1503, 'year', 'Leonardo began painting the Mona Lisa in 1503. Later, the King of France gave him a home near the royal castle to live in.', '4 life events span 1466-1519 (53 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match, not Order.', 'Italian Renaissance', 'Following Leonardo''s death in 1519, his notebooks and drawings passed to his pupil and heir Francesco Melzi, while leadership of Italian High Renaissance painting passed to younger contemporaries such as Raphael and Michelangelo.', 'Leonardo spent his final years in France as ''First Painter, Engineer, and Architect to the King'' under Francis I, who gave him the manor house of Clos Lucé near the royal château at Amboise to live in.', 'Leonardo filled thousands of notebook pages, largely written in distinctive mirror-image (right-to-left) handwriting, covering anatomy, flight, hydraulics, and engineering alongside his art.', 'Republic of Florence (Italy)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia: Leonardo da Vinci (born 15 Apr 1452 Vinci; died 2 May 1519 Amboise; 1466-72 Florence apprenticeship w/ Verrocchio; 1482 Milan under Ludovico Sforza; 1503 began Mona Lisa; 1516-19 France under Francis I).'),
  ('d996a564-d806-58e3-a0e7-70b806229f73', 'P005', 'person', 'marie-curie', 'Marie Curie', 'Physicist and chemist who pioneered research on radioactivity.', 'draft', 'In what city was this trailblazing scientist born?', 52.2297, 21.0122, 'Warsaw, Congress Poland (Russian Empire)', 'This is Warsaw, in what is now Poland, the birthplace of Marie Curie. She grew up to become a groundbreaking scientist who studied radioactivity.', 'First Nobel Prize (Physics)', 'In what year did this scientist win her first Nobel Prize?', '1903', 1903, 'year', 'Marie Curie won her first Nobel Prize, in Physics, in 1903. She is still the only person ever to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.', '4 life events span 1891-1911 (20 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match, not Order.', 'Belle Époque', 'Marie Curie was succeeded in her professorship at the Sorbonne by her daughter Irène Joliot-Curie, who together with her husband Frédéric won the 1935 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their own research on induced radioactivity.', 'Curie remains the only person in history to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences: Physics in 1903 (shared with Pierre Curie and Henri Becquerel) and Chemistry in 1911.', 'She died in 1934 of aplastic anemia almost certainly caused by prolonged radiation exposure; her papers and notebooks, including a personal cookbook, remain radioactive and are stored in lead-lined boxes today.', 'Congress Poland, Russian Empire (Poland)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia: Marie Curie (born 7 Nov 1867 Warsaw; died 4 Jul 1934 Passy, Haute-Savoie, of aplastic anaemia; 1891 moved to Paris; 1898 discovered polonium & radium; 1903 Nobel Physics; 1911 Nobel Chemistry).'),
  ('8d778e57-001e-5a15-81e3-57ce13099060', 'E001', 'event', 'battle-of-culloden', 'Battle of Culloden', 'Final pitched battle of the Jacobite rising of 1745, fought on Culloden Moor.', 'draft', 'Where did this battle take place?', 57.47722, -4.0925, 'Culloden Moor, near Inverness, Scotland', 'This is Culloden Moor, near Inverness, Scotland, where the Battle of Culloden was fought. It was the last major battle fought on British soil, and it lasted less than an hour.', 'Battle date', 'When was this famous battle fought?', '16 April 1746', 1746, 'day', 'The Battle of Culloden was fought on April 16, 1746. Even though it lasted less than an hour, it changed British history.', '4 sub-events span Jul 1745-Apr 1746 (<1 yr, well under 15-yr threshold) -> Order.', '18th Century Britain', 'Culloden ended the Jacobite risings for good; in its aftermath the British government passed the Act of Proscription of 1746, banning traditional Highland dress and weapons and hastening the breakdown of the clan system.', 'The battle itself lasted less than an hour, making it one of the shortest major pitched battles in British history despite its lasting significance.', 'The victorious government forces were commanded by Prince William, Duke of Cumberland, whose harsh treatment of defeated Jacobites and Highland civilians in the following weeks earned him the nickname ''Butcher Cumberland.''', 'Kingdom of Great Britain (Scotland)', 'Europe', 'Review - battlefield casualties; keep tone factual/educational per Event sensitivity guidelines.', 'Wikipedia: Battle of Culloden (16 Apr 1746, Culloden nr Inverness, 57.47722N 4.09250W; Jacobites ~1,500-2,000 casualties vs govt 50 dead/259 wounded; Charles Edward Stuart vs Duke of Cumberland; battle lasted under an hour).'),
  ('c2360968-107b-5d1c-8e03-34fbed5a5880', 'E002', 'event', '1918-influenza-pandemic-spanish-flu', '1918 Influenza Pandemic ("Spanish Flu")', 'Global influenza pandemic, one of the deadliest in recorded history.', 'draft', 'Where was the first known case of this deadly sickness recorded?', 39.0517, -96.8095, 'Camp Funston, Fort Riley, Kansas, USA', 'This is Camp Funston, at Fort Riley in Kansas, USA. Doctors recorded the first known case of the 1918 flu pandemic here, and it became one of the worst pandemics in history.', 'First documented case', 'When was the first case of this global sickness recorded?', '4 March 1918', 1918, 'day', 'The first case of the 1918 flu pandemic was recorded on March 4, 1918. People called it the ''Spanish flu,'' but it likely did not start in Spain at all.', '4 pandemic waves span Mar 1918-1920 (2 yrs, under 15-yr threshold) -> Order.', 'World War I Era', 'The 1918 pandemic''s virus strain never fully disappeared; its descendant genes persisted in circulating seasonal flu and are believed to be ancestral to later influenza A pandemics, including the 1957 ''Asian flu'' and 1968 ''Hong Kong flu.''', 'Despite its nickname, the ''Spanish flu'' likely did not originate in Spain; Spain earned the name mainly because its neutral, uncensored wartime press reported freely on the outbreak while combatant nations suppressed such news for morale.', 'Unlike typical seasonal flu, the 1918 pandemic disproportionately killed healthy young adults aged roughly 20 to 40, producing an unusual ''W-shaped'' age-mortality curve.', 'United States (global pandemic)', 'North America', 'Review - mass-casualty disease event; present death-toll figures neutrally per Event sensitivity guidelines.', 'Wikipedia: Spanish flu (conventionally begins 4 Mar 1918, Camp Funston/Haskell County, Kansas; ~500M infected, ~25-50M deaths; 2nd wave Aug-Dec 1918 deadliest; 3rd wave 1919; 4th wave early 1920; considered over by mid-1920).'),
  ('26ae74e2-b9a6-58b3-aae1-0a85622b2084', 'E003', 'event', '1883-eruption-of-krakatoa', '1883 Eruption of Krakatoa', 'Catastrophic volcanic eruption in the Sunda Strait, Indonesia.', 'draft', 'Where did this huge volcano explode?', -6.102, 105.423, 'Krakatoa, Sunda Strait, Indonesia', 'This is Krakatoa, in the Sunda Strait in Indonesia, where a huge volcano exploded in 1883. The blast made one of the loudest sounds ever recorded, heard almost 3,000 miles away.', 'Climactic explosion', 'When did this famous volcano erupt?', '27 August 1883, 10:02 am local time', 1883, 'day', 'Krakatoa erupted on August 27, 1883. The explosion was so loud that people heard it far across the ocean.', '4 sub-events span May-Aug 1883 (<1 yr, well under 15-yr threshold) -> Order.', 'Dutch Colonial Era', 'The 1883 eruption largely destroyed the original island, but volcanic activity resumed in the same caldera in 1927, gradually building a new island, Anak Krakatau (''Child of Krakatoa''), which remains active today.', 'The climactic explosion produced what is often cited as the loudest sound in recorded history, reportedly heard clearly nearly 3,000 miles (about 4,800 km) away on Rodrigues Island near Mauritius.', 'Ash and aerosols thrown into the stratosphere lowered global average temperatures for several years afterward and produced vivid red sunsets worldwide, effects some art historians have linked to the fiery sky in Edvard Munch''s ''The Scream.''', 'Dutch East Indies (Indonesia)', 'Asia', 'Review - natural disaster with 36,000+ deaths; present neutrally per Event sensitivity guidelines.', 'Wikipedia: 1883 eruption of Krakatoa (climactic explosion 27 Aug 1883 10:02am; VEI 6; >=36,417 deaths, mostly from tsunamis up to 46m at Merak; heard ~4,800km away near Mauritius; coordinates 6.102S 105.423E).'),
  ('a2a87944-f1c0-54d0-8493-f9850cac3d26', 'E004', 'event', 'd-day-normandy-landings', 'D-Day (Normandy Landings)', 'Allied amphibious invasion of Nazi-occupied France that opened the Western Front.', 'draft', 'Where did this huge military invasion take place?', 49.34, -0.6, 'Normandy beaches, France', 'This is Normandy, France, where the D-Day landings took place. Almost 7,000 ships and about 156,000 soldiers took part, making it the largest sea invasion ever.', 'Invasion date', 'When did this massive invasion happen?', '6 June 1944', 1944, 'day', 'D-Day took place on June 6, 1944. Troops landed on five different beaches to push back Nazi forces in France.', '4 sub-events occur within a single day (6 Jun 1944), well under 15-yr threshold -> Order.', 'World War II', 'D-Day was followed by the Battle of Normandy and the liberation of Paris on 25 August 1944, opening a sustained Western Front push that, combined with the Soviet advance from the east, led to Nazi Germany''s surrender in May 1945.', 'D-Day remains the largest seaborne invasion in history, involving nearly 7,000 vessels and roughly 156,000 Allied troops landing across five beaches codenamed Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno, and Sword.', 'The invasion, originally planned for 5 June 1944, was postponed a day due to bad weather; that same poor forecast contributed to German commander Erwin Rommel being away from the front on leave in Germany when the landings began.', 'Nazi-occupied France (France)', 'Europe', 'Review - wartime casualties; keep tone factual/commemorative per Event sensitivity guidelines.', 'Wikipedia: Normandy landings (6 Jun 1944; beaches Utah/Omaha/Gold/Juno/Sword, general coords 49.34N 0.60W; ~156,000 troops landed day one; Allied casualties >=10,000 incl. 4,414 confirmed dead; German 4,000-9,000; Eisenhower Supreme Commander).'),
  ('87fca899-b7ca-52f2-bbd7-3579842dbf9c', 'E005', 'event', 'fall-of-the-berlin-wall', 'Fall of the Berlin Wall', 'The opening of the East German border and collapse of the Berlin Wall, symbolizing the end of the Cold War division of Europe.', 'draft', 'In which city did this famous wall come down?', 52.50444, 13.44111, 'Berlin, Germany', 'This is Berlin, Germany, where the Berlin Wall once divided the city. Its sudden fall showed the world the Cold War was ending.', 'Wall opened', 'When did this famous wall open up?', '9 November 1989', 1989, 'day', 'The Berlin Wall opened on November 9, 1989. A government spokesman accidentally said new travel rules would start right away, so crowds rushed to the checkpoints that night.', '4 sub-events span 1961-1989 (28 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match, not Order.', 'Cold War', 'The Wall''s fall set in motion German reunification, formally completed on 3 October 1990 when East Germany was dissolved and its territory joined the Federal Republic of Germany.', 'The Wall''s sudden opening on 9 November 1989 came about partly by accident, after East German spokesman Günter Schabowski misspoke at a press conference and announced new travel rules would take effect ''immediately,'' prompting crowds to rush the checkpoints that night.', 'The Berlin Wall had stood for just over 28 years, having been erected by East Germany starting 13 August 1961 to stop mass emigration of its citizens to the West.', 'Germany (East and West Berlin)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia: Berlin Wall / Fall of the Berlin Wall (construction began 13 Aug 1961; wall fell 9 Nov 1989 after Schabowski''s announcement; coords 52.50444N 13.44111E; ~155km total length; built to stop emigration to West).'),
  ('442afbca-8dc4-5632-a7e4-c200a0cd38cd', 'P006', 'person', 'julius-caesar', 'Julius Caesar', 'Roman general and statesman whose rise to sole power ended the Roman Republic.', 'draft', 'In which city did this powerful Roman leader rule?', 41.9028, 12.4964, 'Rome, Italy', 'This is Rome, in what is now Italy, where Julius Caesar ruled. His rise to power ended centuries of the Roman Republic.', 'Assassination of Julius Caesar (Ides of March)', 'When was this famous Roman leader killed?', '15 March 44 BC', -44, 'day', 'Julius Caesar was killed on March 15, 44 BC. In his will, he adopted his young great-nephew, who later became the first Roman emperor.', 'Span from first consulship (59 BC) to Pharsalus (48 BC) is 11 years, under 15, so chronological tap-to-order fits best.', 'Ancient Rome', 'Caesar''s assassination triggered a power struggle that produced the Second Triumvirate of Octavian, Mark Antony, and Lepidus; Caesar''s adopted heir Octavian ultimately prevailed and became Rome''s first emperor, Augustus.', 'In his will, Caesar posthumously adopted his 18-year-old great-nephew Gaius Octavius, naming him principal heir; Octavius took the name Octavian and later became the emperor Augustus.', 'Caesar''s reformed calendar, the Julian calendar introduced in 46 BC, remained the standard civil calendar across much of the Western world for over 1,500 years, until the Gregorian reform of 1582.', 'Roman Republic (Italy)', 'Europe', 'Review - assassination', 'Wikipedia ''Julius Caesar'' confirmed birth 12/13 July 100 BC in Suburra, Rome; Rubicon crossing ~10-11 Jan 49 BC; Battle of Pharsalus 9 Aug 48 BC; assassination 15 March 44 BC at Theatre of Pompey.'),
  ('f4676c79-9da4-54ad-933e-df1c524e1cf8', 'P007', 'person', 'alexander-the-great', 'Alexander the Great', 'King of Macedon who conquered the Persian Empire and built one of history''s largest ancient empires.', 'draft', 'Where was the capital of this young king who built a huge empire?', 40.8, 22.517, 'Pella, Macedon (Greece)', 'This is Pella, in Macedon, part of modern Greece, the capital of Alexander the Great. From there he set out to conquer the Persian Empire.', 'Death of Alexander the Great', 'When did this famous conqueror die?', '10 June 323 BC', -323, 'day', 'Alexander the Great died on June 10, 323 BC. He founded more than twenty cities named after himself, and the most famous one, Alexandria in Egypt, became a great center of learning.', 'Span from becoming king (336 BC) to Battle of the Hydaspes (326 BC) is 10 years, under 15.', 'Classical Antiquity', 'Alexander died without a clear adult heir, and his generals, known as the Diadochi (''successors''), fought decades of wars that fractured his empire into rival Hellenistic kingdoms, chiefly Ptolemaic Egypt, the Seleucid Empire, and Antigonid Macedon.', 'Alexander founded more than twenty cities bearing his name across his empire; the most famous, Alexandria in Egypt, went on to become one of the ancient world''s leading centers of learning.', 'As a boy, Alexander was tutored by the philosopher Aristotle, who is credited with fostering his lifelong interests in science, medicine, and philosophy alongside his military education.', 'Kingdom of Macedon (Greece)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Alexander the Great'' confirmed birth 356 BC Pella; became king Oct 336 BC; died 10/11 June 323 BC Babylon.'),
  ('fc5382f3-2eaa-5d83-894b-8a7770f4f81e', 'P008', 'person', 'napoleon-bonaparte', 'Napoleon Bonaparte', 'French military leader who rose to become Emperor of the French and reshaped Europe through conquest.', 'draft', 'On what island was this future emperor born?', 41.9267, 8.7369, 'Ajaccio, Corsica, France', 'This is Ajaccio, on the island of Corsica, France, the birthplace of Napoleon Bonaparte. He rose from there to become Emperor of the French.', 'Battle of Waterloo', 'When did this emperor lose his final major battle?', '18 June 1815', 1815, 'day', 'Napoleon lost the Battle of Waterloo on June 18, 1815. His set of laws from 1804 still shapes the legal systems of many countries today.', 'Span from coronation (1804) to exile to Elba (1814) is 10 years, under 15.', 'Napoleonic Era', 'After his defeat at Waterloo, Napoleon abdicated for a second time and was exiled by the British to the remote South Atlantic island of Saint Helena, where he died in 1821, while in France the restored Bourbon monarchy under King Louis XVIII resumed the throne.', 'Napoleon''s Napoleonic Code of 1804, which established principles like equality before the law and secular civil authority, still forms the basis of civil law codes in France and dozens of other countries today.', 'Napoleon was of roughly average height for his era, about 5 feet 6-7 inches (around 1.68-1.70 m); the enduring ''short'' myth arose partly from confusion between French and English inches and from British wartime propaganda caricatures.', 'France (Corsica)', 'Europe', 'Review - war content (Napoleonic Wars)', 'Wikipedia ''Napoleon'' confirmed birth 15 Aug 1769 Ajaccio; coronation 2 Dec 1804; Waterloo 18 June 1815; death 5 May 1821 Saint Helena.'),
  ('f8eed3b8-cbfc-5ed7-bc41-eda77832558c', 'P009', 'person', 'abraham-lincoln', 'Abraham Lincoln', '16th U.S. President who led the country through the Civil War and worked to abolish slavery.', 'draft', 'Near what town was this U.S. president born?', 37.5311, -85.7375, 'near Hodgenville, Kentucky, USA', 'This is near Hodgenville, Kentucky, USA, the birthplace of Abraham Lincoln. He grew up to lead the country through the Civil War.', 'Assassination of Abraham Lincoln', 'When was this U.S. president shot?', '14 April 1865', 1865, 'day', 'Lincoln was shot on April 14, 1865, and died the next morning. He was the first U.S. president ever to be assassinated.', 'Span from election (1860) to Gettysburg Address (1863) is 3 years, well under 15.', 'American Civil War Era', 'Lincoln was succeeded as president by his vice president, Andrew Johnson, who was sworn in within hours of Lincoln''s death and went on to oversee the early, contested years of Reconstruction.', 'Lincoln was the first U.S. president to be assassinated; he was shot by actor John Wilkes Booth at Ford''s Theatre in Washington, D.C. on the evening of 14 April 1865 and died the next morning, 15 April.', 'Lincoln had less than a year of formal schooling in total and was largely self-taught, working as a store clerk, postmaster, and surveyor before becoming a lawyer and entering politics.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - assassination/Civil War', 'Wikipedia ''Abraham Lincoln'' confirmed birth 12 Feb 1809 Kentucky; elected president 6 Nov 1860; shot at Ford''s Theatre 14 April 1865, died 15 April 1865.'),
  ('a8886fc2-8aa1-5099-80a6-18c0eb7f3015', 'P010', 'person', 'mahatma-gandhi', 'Mahatma Gandhi', 'Indian lawyer and activist who led India''s nonviolent independence movement against British rule.', 'draft', 'In what Indian town was this peaceful protest leader born?', 21.6417, 69.6293, 'Porbandar, India', 'This is Porbandar, India, the birthplace of Mahatma Gandhi. He later led India''s peaceful fight for freedom from British rule.', 'Assassination of Mahatma Gandhi', 'When was this peaceful protest leader killed?', '30 January 1948', 1948, 'day', 'Gandhi was killed on January 30, 1948. He trained as a lawyer in London and first used peaceful protest while working in South Africa.', 'Span from South Africa legal work (1893) to Quit India (1942) is 49 years, 15+; two items share the same place (Bombay), so year is stronger than place.', 'Indian Independence Movement', 'Gandhi''s assassination came just months after India''s 1947 independence and partition; leadership of independent India passed to Jawaharlal Nehru, its first prime minister, who continued to invoke Gandhi''s principles in shaping the new nation.', 'Gandhi trained as a barrister in London, and it was while working as a lawyer opposing discriminatory laws against Indians in South Africa from 1893 to 1914 that he first developed his philosophy and tactics of nonviolent civil resistance, or satyagraha.', 'Gandhi was assassinated by Nathuram Godse, a Hindu nationalist who opposed Gandhi''s tolerance toward Muslims and his support for India honoring its financial commitments to the new state of Pakistan; Godse was tried and executed in 1949.', 'British India (India)', 'Asia', 'Review - assassination', 'Wikipedia ''Mahatma Gandhi'' confirmed birth 2 Oct 1869 Porbandar; assassinated 30 Jan 1948 Birla House, New Delhi.'),
  ('93f00037-263b-5f6e-81b2-1bed40c0ff38', 'P011', 'person', 'nelson-mandela', 'Nelson Mandela', 'South African anti-apartheid leader who became the country''s first Black president after 27 years in prison.', 'draft', 'In what village was this anti-apartheid leader born?', -31.95, 28.516, 'Mvezo, South Africa', 'This is Mvezo, South Africa, the birthplace of Nelson Mandela. He spent 27 years in prison before becoming his country''s first Black president.', 'Release from prison', 'When did this famous leader walk free from prison?', '11 February 1990', 1990, 'day', 'Mandela was released from prison on February 11, 1990. In 1961, he had helped start an armed group after deciding peaceful protest alone was not stopping apartheid.', 'Span from joining ANC (1944) to becoming President (1994) is 50 years, 15+; events cluster in two cities, so year is stronger.', 'Apartheid Era', 'Mandela served a single term as president and was succeeded in 1999 by his deputy, Thabo Mbeki, continuing the African National Congress''s hold on power in post-apartheid South Africa.', 'In 1961, after concluding that strictly nonviolent protest alone could not defeat apartheid, Mandela helped found and lead Umkhonto we Sizwe (''Spear of the Nation''), the armed wing of the African National Congress.', 'Mandela shared the 1993 Nobel Peace Prize with South African president F. W. de Klerk for their joint efforts to dismantle apartheid and negotiate South Africa''s peaceful transition to multiracial democracy.', 'South Africa', 'Africa', 'Review - apartheid imprisonment', 'Wikipedia ''Nelson Mandela'' confirmed birth 18 July 1918 Mvezo; released 11 Feb 1990; President from 10 May 1994.'),
  ('07203e09-bcc6-562e-bb7a-69c767f01c04', 'P012', 'person', 'martin-luther-king-jr', 'Martin Luther King Jr.', 'American Baptist minister and civil rights leader who championed nonviolent resistance to racial injustice.', 'draft', 'In what U.S. city was this civil rights leader born?', 33.7552, -84.3712, 'Atlanta, Georgia, USA', 'This is Atlanta, Georgia, USA, the birthplace of Martin Luther King Jr. He grew up to lead the fight for equal rights using peaceful protest.', '"I Have a Dream" speech', 'When did this civil rights leader give his most famous speech?', '28 August 1963', 1963, 'day', 'King gave his ''I Have a Dream'' speech on August 28, 1963. The next year, at age 35, he became the youngest person ever to win the Nobel Peace Prize.', 'Span from Montgomery Bus Boycott (1955) to assassination (1968) is 13 years, under 15.', 'Civil Rights Movement (20th Century)', 'Following King''s assassination in April 1968, his colleague Ralph Abernathy succeeded him as president of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC).', 'In 1964, at age 35, King became the youngest person at the time to receive the Nobel Peace Prize.', 'King was assassinated on April 4, 1968, on the balcony of the Lorraine Motel in Memphis, Tennessee, by James Earl Ray.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - assassination/civil rights struggle', 'Wikipedia ''Martin Luther King Jr.'' confirmed birth 15 Jan 1929 Atlanta; ''I Have a Dream'' 28 Aug 1963; assassinated 4 April 1968 Memphis.'),
  ('8d4ea4bf-f129-5f91-b699-c833b10f2283', 'P013', 'person', 'queen-elizabeth-i', 'Queen Elizabeth I', 'Queen of England whose long reign is remembered for defeating the Spanish Armada and a flourishing of English culture.', 'draft', 'Where was this Tudor queen born?', 51.476, -0.004, 'Greenwich, England', 'This is Greenwich, England, the birthplace of Queen Elizabeth I. Her long rule saw English culture flourish and the defeat of the Spanish Armada.', 'Coronation of Elizabeth I', 'When was this queen crowned?', '15 January 1559', 1559, 'day', 'Elizabeth I was crowned on January 15, 1559. She never married, so people called her the ''Virgin Queen.''', 'Span from accession (1558) to death (1603) is 45 years, 15+; four distinct places, so place is stronger.', 'Elizabethan Era (Tudor England)', 'Elizabeth I died without an heir in March 1603 and was succeeded by her cousin James VI of Scotland, who became James I of England, uniting the two crowns.', 'She never married and was known as the ''Virgin Queen,'' a choice that became central to her political image and propaganda.', 'Her reign saw a flourishing of English drama and literature, including the early works of William Shakespeare and Christopher Marlowe.', 'Kingdom of England', 'Europe', 'Review - execution of Mary, Queen of Scots ordered', 'Wikipedia ''Elizabeth I'' confirmed birth 7 Sept 1533 Greenwich; crowned 15 Jan 1559; died 24 March 1603 Richmond Palace.'),
  ('e2e16166-d284-599c-9637-aa92ed9fb9ac', 'P014', 'person', 'queen-victoria', 'Queen Victoria', 'Queen of the United Kingdom whose long reign presided over Britain''s industrial growth and imperial expansion.', 'draft', 'Where was this British queen born?', 51.505, -0.1876, 'Kensington Palace, London, England', 'This is Kensington Palace in London, England, the birthplace of Queen Victoria. Her long reign saw Britain grow into a huge industrial and imperial power.', 'Coronation of Queen Victoria', 'When did this young queen have her coronation?', '28 June 1838', 1838, 'day', 'Victoria was crowned on June 28, 1838. She was the first British monarch to live at Buckingham Palace, moving in soon after taking the throne.', 'Span from accession (1837) to Diamond Jubilee (1897) is 60 years, 15+; distinct places (London vs Delhi), so place is stronger.', 'Victorian Era', 'Victoria died in January 1901 and was succeeded by her eldest son, who became King Edward VII.', 'She was the first British monarch to take up residence at Buckingham Palace, moving in shortly after her accession.', 'Through the marriages of her nine children into royal houses across the continent, she became known as the ''grandmother of Europe.''', 'United Kingdom', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Queen Victoria'' confirmed birth 24 May 1819 Kensington Palace; crowned 28 June 1838; died 22 Jan 1901 Osborne House.'),
  ('ef53c830-c4b8-51c5-867a-51682bbcdf1a', 'P015', 'person', 'genghis-khan', 'Genghis Khan', 'Mongol conqueror who unified the Mongolian tribes and founded the largest contiguous land empire in history.', 'draft', 'Where was this Mongol conqueror born?', 49.0124, 111.509, 'Dadal, Khentii Province, Mongolia (traditional birthplace site, Delüün Boldog)', 'This is near Dadal, in Mongolia''s Khentii Province, the traditional birthplace of Genghis Khan. He grew up to unite the Mongol tribes and build a huge empire.', 'Proclaimed Genghis Khan (Great Khan) at the Kurultai on the Onon River', 'In what year was this conqueror named ruler of the Mongols?', '1206', 1206, 'year', 'In 1206, Genghis Khan was named ruler of the Mongols. He was born with the name Temujin, and ''Genghis Khan'' was a title meaning something like ''universal ruler.''', 'Span 1211-1227 (16 yrs, >=15); four cities distinct, so place is stronger.', 'Mongol Empire', 'Genghis Khan died in 1227 and, per his own designation, was succeeded as Great Khan by his third son, Ögedei Khan, who continued the empire''s expansion.', 'He was born with the name Temüjin; ''Genghis Khan'' was a title meaning something like ''universal ruler'' that he adopted after being proclaimed leader of the Mongols in 1206.', 'In keeping with Mongol custom, his burial site was kept deliberately secret and has never been definitively located despite extensive searches.', 'Mongol Empire (Mongolia)', 'Asia', 'Review - large-scale warfare/conquest content', 'Wikipedia ''Genghis Khan'' confirmed proclamation 1206, campaign years 1209-1227.'),
  ('c2f2e503-9311-55fd-8287-bfaf3ae8cfe2', 'P016', 'person', 'william-shakespeare', 'William Shakespeare', 'English playwright and poet widely regarded as the greatest writer in the English language.', 'draft', 'Where did this famous English writer live and work?', 52.1939, -1.708, 'Stratford-upon-Avon, England', 'This is Stratford-upon-Avon, England, where William Shakespeare lived and worked. Today, people consider him the greatest writer in the English language.', 'Death of William Shakespeare', 'When did this famous playwright die?', '23 April 1616', 1616, 'day', 'Shakespeare died on April 23, 1616. Over his career, he wrote 37 plays and 154 poems called sonnets.', 'Span 1592-1606 = 14 years, under 15.', 'Elizabethan/Jacobean Era (English Renaissance)', 'After Shakespeare''s death in 1616, his fellow actors John Heminges and Henry Condell compiled his plays into the First Folio, published in 1623, which preserved roughly half his plays that had not previously appeared in print.', 'He is traditionally credited with 37 plays and 154 sonnets, written over a career spanning roughly two decades.', 'He was a shareholder and principal playwright of the Lord Chamberlain''s Men (later the King''s Men), the acting company that built and performed at the Globe Theatre.', 'Kingdom of England', 'Europe', null, 'Folger Shakespeare Library timeline (1592 first playwright reference, 1599 Globe built, death 1616).'),
  ('46b63fe5-f354-59de-9f02-e2016504fc3c', 'P017', 'person', 'wolfgang-amadeus-mozart', 'Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart', 'Austrian composer and child prodigy who wrote over 600 works during the Classical era.', 'draft', 'In what city was this child-prodigy composer born?', 47.8, 13.045, 'Salzburg, Austria', 'This is Salzburg, Austria, the birthplace of Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart. He started writing music at about age five and grew up to write over 600 pieces.', 'Death of Mozart', 'When did this famous composer die?', '5 December 1791', 1791, 'day', 'Mozart died on December 5, 1791. He began touring as a child piano prodigy with his sister Nannerl.', 'Span 1762-1787 = 25 years (>=15); four distinct cities.', 'Classical Era', 'Mozart left his final work, the Requiem, unfinished at his death in 1791; it was completed from his sketches by his student Franz Xaver Süssmayr.', 'He began composing music at around age five and toured European royal courts as a child prodigy performer with his sister, Nannerl.', 'He died in Vienna at age 35; the exact cause of his death remains debated by historians, with rheumatic fever among the leading theories.', 'Archbishopric of Salzburg (Austria)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart'' confirmed 1762 Munich, 1764 London, 1770 Milan, 1787 Prague, death 5 Dec 1791 Vienna.'),
  ('b05226c6-01fe-5d27-bf70-fb3bbe758b33', 'P018', 'person', 'ludwig-van-beethoven', 'Ludwig van Beethoven', 'German composer who bridged the Classical and Romantic eras despite progressive hearing loss.', 'draft', 'Where was this famous composer born?', 50.7344, 7.0955, 'Bonn, Germany', 'This composer was born in Bonn, Germany. He went on to write some of the world''s most loved music.', 'Premiere of Symphony No. 9', 'When did this composer''s Ninth Symphony first play for an audience?', '7 May 1824', 1824, 'day', 'The Ninth Symphony premiered on 7 May 1824. By then the composer was almost completely deaf, yet he still conducted the show.', 'Span 1792-1805 = 13 years, under 15.', 'Transition from Classical to Romantic Era', 'Beethoven''s expansion of musical form and expression paved the way for the Romantic era, directly influencing successors such as Franz Schubert and Johannes Brahms.', 'He began losing his hearing in his late twenties and was almost completely deaf by the time he conducted the premiere of his Ninth Symphony in 1824.', 'He moved to Vienna in 1792 to study composition with Joseph Haydn and remained based there for the rest of his life.', 'Electorate of Cologne, Holy Roman Empire (modern-day Germany); later based in Vienna, Austria', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Ludwig van Beethoven'' confirmed move to Vienna 1792, hearing loss ~1798, Heiligenstadt Testament 1802, Eroica premiere 1805.'),
  ('355b6989-6592-572f-a508-455d2a702979', 'P019', 'person', 'vincent-van-gogh', 'Vincent van Gogh', 'Dutch Post-Impressionist painter known for bold color and expressive brushwork in works like Sunflowers and The Starry Night.', 'draft', 'Where was this famous painter born?', 51.467, 4.667, 'Zundert, Netherlands', 'This painter was born in Zundert, in the Netherlands. He later moved to France, where he painted many of his best-known works.', 'Paints The Starry Night', 'When did this artist paint one of his most famous night skies?', 'June 1889', 1889, 'year', 'He painted The Starry Night in June 1889. During his whole life, he is only known to have sold one painting.', 'Span 1880-1890 = 10 years, under 15.', 'Post-Impressionism (19th Century)', 'After Van Gogh''s death in 1890, his sister-in-law Johanna van Gogh-Bonger inherited his paintings and letters and worked for decades to promote and preserve his artistic legacy, which posthumously established his fame.', 'He is only documented to have sold one painting, ''The Red Vineyard,'' during his lifetime.', 'Despite only becoming an artist in his late twenties, he produced an estimated 2,100 artworks in about a decade, with a large share created in his final two years.', 'Netherlands (worked primarily in France)', 'Europe', 'Review - mental health/self-harm biography; content kept focused on his art', 'Wikipedia ''Vincent van Gogh'' confirmed 1880 Cuesmes, 1886 Paris, 1888 Arles/Sunflowers, 1889 Starry Night, 1890 Auvers-sur-Oise.'),
  ('769cebd4-c29e-57d0-93ee-8db71c1f8555', 'P020', 'person', 'pablo-picasso', 'Pablo Picasso', 'Spanish artist and co-founder of Cubism, one of the most influential figures in 20th-century art.', 'draft', 'Where was this artist born in Spain?', 36.7202, -4.4203, 'Málaga, Spain', 'This artist was born in Málaga, Spain. He later helped start a bold new art style called Cubism.', 'Paints Guernica', 'When did this artist paint his famous picture about the horrors of war?', '1937', 1937, 'year', 'He painted Guernica in 1937. Over his whole life, he made about 50,000 works of art.', 'Span 1900-1973 = 73 years (>=15); three of four places are Paris-adjacent, too repetitive, so year is chosen.', 'Modern Art (20th Century)', 'Picasso and Georges Braque''s development of Cubism in the years before World War I influenced a wave of later modern art movements, including Futurism, Surrealism, and abstract art.', 'He is estimated to have produced roughly 50,000 artworks over his lifetime, including paintings, sculptures, prints, and ceramics.', 'Guernica was painted in response to the April 1937 bombing of the Basque town of Guernica by German and Italian warplanes supporting Francisco Franco''s Nationalist forces during the Spanish Civil War.', 'Spain (worked primarily in France)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Pablo Picasso'' confirmed 1900 first Paris trip, 1901-04 Blue Period, 1907 Les Demoiselles, 1937 Guernica, died 1973 Mougins.'),
  ('53fdc17e-a9b8-5d71-9b8e-df6c57a5848c', 'P021', 'person', 'galileo-galilei', 'Galileo Galilei', 'Italian astronomer and physicist whose telescopic discoveries supported heliocentrism and led to conflict with the Catholic Church.', 'draft', 'Where was this astronomer born in Italy?', 43.717, 10.4, 'Pisa, Italy', 'This astronomer was born in Pisa, Italy. He used a telescope to study the planets and stars in the sky.', 'Discovers Jupiter''s four largest moons', 'When did this scientist first spot four moons circling Jupiter?', '7 January 1610', 1610, 'day', 'He found Jupiter''s four largest moons on 7 January 1610. He did not invent the telescope, but he made it much more powerful.', 'Span 1589-1633 = 44 years (>=15); four distinct cities.', 'Scientific Revolution (Renaissance Italy)', 'Following Galileo''s 1633 trial, telescopic astronomy and the case for heliocentrism advanced further through the work of later scientists, most notably Isaac Newton, whose 1687 Principia provided the physical laws underpinning it.', 'He significantly improved the design of the telescope, roughly tripling its magnifying power, though he did not invent the instrument himself.', 'In 1633 the Roman Inquisition found him ''vehemently suspect of heresy'' for defending heliocentrism and placed him under house arrest for the remainder of his life.', 'Grand Duchy of Tuscany (Italy)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Galileo Galilei'' confirmed 1589 Pisa, 1609 Padua telescope, 1632 Florence, 1633 Rome Inquisition trial.'),
  ('7010420e-7a76-5072-b49f-b7dc2692522d', 'P022', 'person', 'isaac-newton', 'Isaac Newton', 'English physicist and mathematician who formulated the laws of motion and universal gravitation.', 'draft', 'Where was this scientist born in England?', 52.8055, -0.625, 'Woolsthorpe-by-Colsterworth, Lincolnshire, England', 'This scientist was born in Woolsthorpe-by-Colsterworth, in Lincolnshire, England. He later explained the laws of motion and gravity.', 'Publishes Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica', 'When did this scientist publish his famous book about motion and gravity?', '1687', 1687, 'year', 'He published the Principia Mathematica in 1687. Later in life, he ran England''s mint and chased down counterfeiters.', 'Span 1661-1705 = 44 years (>=15); Cambridge repeats twice, so year is chosen.', 'Scientific Revolution', 'Newton''s laws of motion and universal gravitation remained the foundation of physics for over two centuries, until Albert Einstein''s theories of special and general relativity in the early 20th century refined them for extreme speeds and gravitational fields.', 'Later in life he served as Warden and then Master of the Royal Mint, overseeing England''s currency and pursuing counterfeiters.', 'He served as President of the Royal Society for over two decades, from 1703 until his death in 1727.', 'Kingdom of England', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Isaac Newton'' confirmed 1661 Trinity College, 1665-66 annus mirabilis, 1672 Royal Society, knighted 1705.'),
  ('4e84558a-ff01-59b0-9e3e-8815347f341b', 'P023', 'person', 'charles-darwin', 'Charles Darwin', 'English naturalist whose theory of evolution by natural selection transformed the study of biology.', 'draft', 'Where was this naturalist born in England?', 52.7101, -2.7521, 'Shrewsbury, England', 'This naturalist was born in Shrewsbury, England. His ideas changed how people understand living things.', 'Publishes On the Origin of Species', 'When did this scientist publish his famous book on evolution?', '24 November 1859', 1859, 'day', 'He published On the Origin of Species on 24 November 1859. Much of his evidence came from a five-year trip around the world on a ship called HMS Beagle.', 'Span 1831-1871 = 40 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Victorian Era', 'Darwin''s theory of natural selection was later combined with Gregor Mendel''s rediscovered genetics in the ''modern evolutionary synthesis'' of the 1930s and 1940s, which remains the basis of modern evolutionary biology.', 'Much of the observational evidence behind his theory came from his five-year voyage around the world aboard HMS Beagle, from 1831 to 1836.', 'When he died in 1882, he was given a state funeral and buried in Westminster Abbey, close to the grave of Isaac Newton.', 'United Kingdom (England)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Charles Darwin'' confirmed Beagle voyage 1831-1836, Galápagos, Origin of Species 1859, Descent of Man 1871.'),
  ('d52ef8a1-7fb0-5bab-b0bf-851451e1b173', 'P024', 'person', 'nikola-tesla', 'Nikola Tesla', 'Serbian-American inventor and electrical engineer who pioneered alternating current (AC) power systems.', 'draft', 'Where was this inventor born?', 44.567, 15.317, 'Smiljan, Croatia (birthplace)', 'This inventor was born in Smiljan, in modern-day Croatia. He helped bring alternating current power to the world.', 'AC Induction Motor Unveiled', 'When did this inventor show off his new electric motor for the first time?', '16 May 1888', 1888, 'day', 'He unveiled his AC induction motor on 16 May 1888. This invention helped send electric power across long distances.', 'Span 1875-1901 = 26 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Second Industrial Revolution (Age of Electricity)', 'Tesla''s AC induction motor and polyphase power system were rapidly adopted by industry, and his rivalry with Thomas Edison''s DC system was effectively settled when Westinghouse''s AC technology, licensing Tesla''s patents, won the contract to power the 1893 World''s Columbian Exposition and the first Niagara Falls power station.', 'He unveiled his AC induction motor to the American Institute of Electrical Engineers in May 1888, a breakthrough that helped make long-distance alternating-current power transmission practical.', 'Beyond AC power, he also pioneered early wireless transmission and remote-control technology, demonstrating a radio-controlled boat in 1898.', 'Austrian Empire (born in modern-day Croatia, ethnic Serb); naturalized United States citizen in 1891', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia (Nikola Tesla) confirmed birth 1856 Smiljan, 1875 Graz, 1884 emigration NYC, 1888 AC motor demo, 1893 Niagara Falls, 1901 Wardenclyffe.'),
  ('440ae22b-bdac-5a68-9290-df791fac7a0a', 'P025', 'person', 'thomas-edison', 'Thomas Edison', 'American inventor and businessman who developed the practical incandescent light bulb and the phonograph.', 'draft', 'Where was this inventor born in the United States?', 41.28806, -82.59944, 'Milan, Ohio, USA (birthplace)', 'This inventor was born in Milan, Ohio. He held more U.S. patents than any other inventor of his time.', 'Incandescent Lamp Breakthrough', 'When did this inventor make his big breakthrough with the light bulb?', '21 October 1879', 1879, 'day', 'His big lightbulb breakthrough came on 21 October 1879. Over his life, he earned 1,093 U.S. patents.', 'Span 1869-1931 = 62 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Gilded Age / Second Industrial Revolution', 'Edison''s electric companies were consolidated and, in 1892, merged with the Thomson-Houston Electric Company to form General Electric, which continued and expanded his work in electrical power.', 'He held 1,093 U.S. patents in his name, more than any other inventor of his era.', 'In 1876 he established an industrial research laboratory at Menlo Park, New Jersey, often considered a prototype for the modern industrial R&D lab.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia (Thomas Edison) confirmed birth 1847 Milan OH, 1869 Boston patent, 1877 Menlo Park phonograph, 1882 Pearl Street NYC, died 1931 West Orange NJ.'),
  ('a89713da-1033-599c-8ffb-fff7d2990250', 'P026', 'person', 'amelia-earhart', 'Amelia Earhart', 'Pioneering American aviator, the first woman to fly solo nonstop across the Atlantic Ocean.', 'draft', 'Where was this pilot born in the United States?', 39.5625, -95.12833, 'Atchison, Kansas, USA (birthplace)', 'This pilot was born in Atchison, Kansas. She became the first woman to fly alone across the Atlantic Ocean.', 'Disappearance Over the Pacific', 'When did this famous pilot vanish during a flight over the ocean?', '2 July 1937', 1937, 'day', 'She disappeared over the Pacific Ocean on 2 July 1937. In 1929, she had helped start a club for women pilots called The Ninety-Nines.', 'Span 1928-1937 = 9 years, under 15.', 'Golden Age of Aviation (Early 20th Century)', 'After an extensive U.S. Navy and Coast Guard search failed to find Earhart or her plane, she was formally declared legally dead on January 5, 1939, and her disappearance remains one of aviation''s most enduring unsolved mysteries.', 'In 1929 she helped found The Ninety-Nines, an organization for female pilots, and served as its first elected president.', 'She vanished on July 2, 1937, along with navigator Fred Noonan, while attempting to fly around the world and searching for the tiny mid-Pacific refueling stop of Howland Island.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - disappearance stated factually, no speculation', 'Wikipedia (Amelia Earhart) confirmed birth 1897 Atchison KS; 1928 transatlantic passenger flight; 1932 solo transatlantic; 1935 Hawaii-Oakland; 1937 disappearance near Howland Island.'),
  ('018d892e-353a-5814-b41a-179aff72b56e', 'P027', 'person', 'neil-armstrong', 'Neil Armstrong', 'American astronaut and the first person to walk on the Moon during the Apollo 11 mission.', 'draft', 'Where was this astronaut born in the United States?', 40.56556, -84.19111, 'Wapakoneta, Ohio, USA (birthplace)', 'This astronaut was born in Wapakoneta, Ohio. He later became the first person ever to walk on the Moon.', 'First Steps on the Moon', 'When did this astronaut take the first steps on the Moon?', '20 July 1969', 1969, 'day', 'He walked on the Moon on 20 July 1969. Before he became an astronaut, he flew combat missions as a Navy pilot.', 'Span 1951-2012 = 61 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Space Race (Cold War Era)', 'Armstrong was followed onto the lunar surface minutes later by fellow Apollo 11 astronaut Buzz Aldrin; the next crewed Moon landing was Apollo 12 in November 1969, commanded by Pete Conrad.', 'Before joining NASA, he served as a U.S. Navy fighter pilot and flew 78 combat missions during the Korean War.', 'After leaving NASA, he became a professor of aerospace engineering at the University of Cincinnati, teaching there through much of the 1970s.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia (Neil Armstrong) confirmed birth 1930 Wapakoneta OH, died 2012; 1951 Korea combat mission; 1962 NASA selection; 1966 Gemini 8; 1969 Apollo 11 landing.'),
  ('35ef0fff-a0fc-5333-87aa-a0d3c9cf756f', 'P028', 'person', 'ferdinand-magellan', 'Ferdinand Magellan', 'Portuguese explorer whose 1519-1522 expedition achieved the first circumnavigation of the globe, though he died before its completion.', 'draft', 'Where did this explorer''s ships set sail from to circle the globe?', 36.7781, -6.3515, 'Sanlúcar de Barrameda, Spain (departure point of the circumnavigation fleet)', 'His fleet set sail from Sanlúcar de Barrameda, in Spain. The trip became the first voyage to sail all the way around the world.', 'Death at the Battle of Mactan', 'When did this explorer die during a battle far from home?', '27 April 1521', 1521, 'day', 'He died at the Battle of Mactan on 27 April 1521. He never finished the voyage himself, but he had led it most of the way there.', 'Span 1505-1521 = 16 years (>=15); four highly varied locations.', 'Age of Exploration', 'Following Magellan''s death at the Battle of Mactan, Juan Sebastián Elcano took command of the expedition and completed the first circumnavigation of the globe, returning to Spain in September 1522.', 'Magellan relied heavily on Enrique of Malacca, an enslaved interpreter he brought along, whose language skills were crucial for communicating with Southeast Asian and Philippine leaders during the voyage.', 'During the brutal three-month Pacific crossing, when roughly 30 crew members died of scurvy, Magellan himself reportedly stayed healthy, in part due to a personal supply of preserved quince.', 'Kingdom of Portugal, sailing in the service of the Crown of Castile (Spain)', 'Europe', 'Review - death in armed battle; birthplace historically disputed', 'Wikipedia (Ferdinand Magellan) confirmed birth c.1480 (birthplace disputed, Sabrosa claim is an 18th-c. forgery); death 27 Apr 1521 Mactan; departure fleet from Sanlúcar de Barrameda, Sept 1519.'),
  ('134442ac-8e25-50ac-a1df-d68a87961516', 'P029', 'person', 'marco-polo', 'Marco Polo', 'Venetian merchant and explorer famed for his 24-year journey along the Silk Road to the court of Kublai Khan.', 'draft', 'Where was this merchant and explorer born in Italy?', 45.4375, 12.33583, 'Venice, Italy (birthplace)', 'This explorer was born in Venice, Italy. He later traveled the Silk Road all the way to the court of a Mongol ruler in China.', 'Departure on the Silk Road Journey to Asia', 'When did this explorer set off on his long journey along the Silk Road?', '1271', 1271, 'year', 'He left on his journey to Asia in 1271. Years later, while in jail, he told the story of his travels to a writer, who turned it into a famous book.', 'Span 1275-1298 = 23 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Medieval Venice', 'Marco Polo''s account of his travels went on to inspire later explorers; most notably, Christopher Columbus owned and heavily annotated a copy of Polo''s Travels nearly two centuries later while planning his own westward voyage to Asia.', 'Polo dictated his travel account to a fellow prisoner, romance writer Rustichello da Pisa, while jailed in Genoa around 1298-1299; the resulting book, known as ''Il Milione,'' introduced Europeans to details of Chinese paper money, coal-burning, and the imperial postal relay system.', 'In his will, Polo freed a servant believed to have accompanied him back from Asia, a man identified only as ''Peter the Tartar,'' leaving him 100 lire of Venetian currency.', 'Republic of Venice (Italy)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia (Marco Polo) confirmed birth c.1254, death 1324; 1271 departure Venice; c.1275 Kublai Khan''s court; 1295 return Venice; 1298 imprisoned Genoa, dictated travels.'),
  ('12987ac1-c1f3-5360-95b0-c74da063ba5c', 'P030', 'person', 'vasco-da-gama', 'Vasco da Gama', 'Portuguese explorer who led the first European fleet to sail directly from Europe to India by sea.', 'draft', 'Where was this explorer born in Portugal?', 37.95472, -8.86444, 'Sines, Portugal (birthplace)', 'This explorer was born in Sines, Portugal. He was the first European to sail all the way from Europe to India by sea.', 'First Sea Route to India', 'When did this explorer reach India by sailing straight from Europe?', '20 May 1498', 1498, 'day', 'He reached India on 20 May 1498. His new sea route opened up direct trade between Europe and India.', 'Span 1497-1524 = 27 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Age of Exploration', 'Da Gama was succeeded as Governor of Portuguese India by Henrique de Menezes in 1524, after da Gama died of malaria in Cochin just months into his term as Viceroy.', 'Da Gama secured a remarkable commitment from King John III of Portugal that his descendants would hold the hereditary captaincy of Malacca, a strategically vital port on the Malay Peninsula.', 'Da Gama''s gravestone was accidentally swapped with that of his great-grandson during a 19th-century restoration, causing decades of confusion over the true location of his remains before they were correctly identified and returned to Portugal.', 'Kingdom of Portugal', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia (Vasco da Gama) confirmed birth c.1460, death 1524 Cochin; 1497 departure Lisbon; Nov 1497 Cape of Good Hope; 1498 Calicut; 1502 conflict Calicut; 1524 Viceroy.'),
  ('be6c2e62-29a3-516a-8222-d7e25feaf017', 'P031', 'person', 'zheng-he', 'Zheng He', 'Chinese admiral and diplomat of the Ming dynasty who led seven massive maritime treasure voyages across the Indian Ocean.', 'draft', 'Where was this Chinese admiral born?', 25.0464, 102.7094, 'Kunming, Yunnan, China (birthplace region)', 'This admiral was born in the Kunming area of Yunnan, China. He went on to lead huge fleets of ships across the Indian Ocean.', 'First Treasure Voyage Departs', 'When did this admiral''s first great fleet of ships set sail?', '11 July 1405', 1405, 'day', 'His first treasure voyage left port on 11 July 1405. He led seven of these huge voyages during the Ming Dynasty.', 'Span 1381-1433 = 52 years (>=15); four distinct locations.', 'Ming Dynasty', 'Following Zheng He''s death at sea around 1433-1435, the Ming court, under pressure from Confucian officials wary of the voyages'' cost, ended the treasure fleet program and shifted its focus to land-based defenses against Mongol incursions.', 'Zheng He was born into a Muslim Hui family in Yunnan and was captured and castrated as a boy during a Ming military campaign before rising to become a trusted eunuch official under the Yongle Emperor.', 'Despite his Muslim upbringing, Zheng He became a devoted follower of Tianfei, the Chinese goddess of seafarers, and built temples and shrines in her honor during his voyages.', 'Ming Dynasty China', 'Asia', null, 'Wikipedia/Britannica (Zheng He) confirmed birth c.1371 Yunnan; capture 1381; service Prince of Yan from c.1390; 1405 first voyage; 1421 sixth voyage East Africa; death 1433 near Calicut.'),
  ('dcac344a-7e37-591e-9ce5-29536a194299', 'P032', 'person', 'confucius', 'Confucius', 'Chinese philosopher and teacher of the Spring and Autumn period whose ideas on ethics and governance shaped East Asian thought for millennia.', 'draft', 'Where was this ancient Chinese teacher born?', 35.5819, 116.9862, 'Qufu, Shandong, China (associated birthplace region, ancient state of Lu)', 'This teacher was born near Qufu, in Shandong, China. His ideas about how to live and lead shaped China for thousands of years.', 'Death of Confucius', 'When did this ancient teacher die?', '479 BC', -479, 'year', 'He died in 479 BC. His birth name, Qiu, means ''hill,'' after a bump on his head as a baby.', 'Span 551-484 BC = 67 years (>=15); three of four events occur in same Lu/Qufu location, so year is chosen.', 'Spring and Autumn Period', 'After Confucius''s death, his teachings were carried on by his disciples and his grandson Zisi, and were later developed further by the philosophers Mencius and Xunzi, cementing Confucianism as a dominant school of Chinese thought.', 'Confucius''s given name, Qiu, meaning ''hill,'' is traditionally said to reference an unusual bump on the top of his head at birth.', 'None of Confucius''s own writings survive; his teachings are known chiefly through the Analects, a collection of sayings and ideas compiled by his students after his death.', 'State of Lu, Zhou Dynasty China', 'Asia', 'Review - philosophical/religious figure, kept strictly historical/biographical', 'Wikipedia/Britannica confirmed birth c.551 BC Zou (Lu state); death c.479 BC Lu; 501 BC govt appointment; departure c.497 BC; return c.484 BC.'),
  ('49b67d9a-c65e-52b0-a3cf-67e4976a685e', 'P033', 'person', 'sun-tzu', 'Sun Tzu', 'Ancient Chinese military strategist and general credited with writing The Art of War.', 'draft', 'In what old Chinese city did this military leader live?', 31.3193, 120.5918, 'Suzhou (ancient Gusu, capital of Wu), China', 'This military leader was tied to Suzhou, the old capital of the state of Wu. He is said to have written the famous book The Art of War.', 'Victory at the Battle of Boju', 'When did this military leader win a major battle for his home state?', '506 BCE', -506, 'year', 'He won the Battle of Boju in 506 BCE. Some historians even debate whether he was a real person, since his name shows up only in books written centuries later.', 'Span 544-496 BCE = 48 years (>=15); place chosen over year for clearer geographic arc.', 'Spring and Autumn Period', 'After his victory at Boju, Sun Tzu was appointed a general by King Helü of Wu; centuries later, scholars confused his identity with that of his descendant Sun Bin, a mix-up not resolved until Sun Bin''s own long-lost military treatise was rediscovered in 1972.', 'Some modern historians question whether Sun Tzu was a real individual, since his name does not appear in Chinese texts earlier than Sima Qian''s Records of the Grand Historian, written centuries after his supposed lifetime.', 'The Art of War, attributed to Sun Tzu, was not compiled into its familiar form until decades later, with military strategist Cao Cao producing the earliest surviving annotated edition around AD 200.', 'State of Wu, Zhou Dynasty China', 'Asia', null, 'Wikipedia ''Sun Tzu''/''Battle of Boju'' confirmed traditional dates 544-496 BCE, service to King Helü of Wu, 506 BCE Boju campaign.'),
  ('a69c63ec-a6e4-5806-a1df-dbec65705d57', 'P034', 'person', 'cyrus-the-great', 'Cyrus the Great', 'Founder of the Achaemenid Persian Empire, remembered for conquering Babylon and freeing captive peoples.', 'draft', 'Where was this Persian emperor born?', 30.007, 52.4047, 'Anshan, Persis (modern Fars Province, Iran)', 'This emperor came from Anshan, in the region of Persis, now part of Iran. He built one of the largest empires the world had seen.', 'Conquest of Babylon', 'When did this ruler conquer the great city of Babylon?', 'October 29, 539 BCE', -539, 'day', 'He conquered Babylon on 29 October 539 BCE. He also set up fast mail stations called chapar khaneh to carry messages across his empire.', 'Span 559-530 BCE = 29 years (>=15); precisely sequenced conquests, year chosen.', 'Achaemenid Empire', 'Cyrus the Great was succeeded by his son Cambyses II, who went on to conquer Egypt and further expand the Achaemenid Empire.', 'Cyrus established an early postal relay system across his empire, using way stations called chapar khaneh to speed the delivery of royal messages over vast distances.', 'The Cyrus Cylinder, a clay artifact from Babylon recording his policies, has been described by some as an early proclamation on the treatment of conquered peoples, though scholars debate how closely it reflects modern human-rights concepts.', 'Achaemenid Persian Empire (modern Iran)', 'Asia', null, 'Wikipedia/Britannica confirmed accession 559 BCE, defeat of Astyages 550 BCE, Lydia/Croesus 547 BCE, Babylon Oct 29 539 BCE, death c.530 BCE.'),
  ('a04000f1-cddc-5980-b517-fb984b29018b', 'P035', 'person', 'ramesses-ii', 'Ramesses II', 'Pharaoh of Egypt''s New Kingdom, renowned for his 66-year reign, monumental building, and the Battle of Kadesh.', 'draft', 'Where was the capital city of this famous pharaoh?', 30.7994, 31.8342, 'Pi-Ramesses (modern Qantir), Nile Delta, Egypt', 'This pharaoh''s capital city was Pi-Ramesses, in the Nile Delta of Egypt. Today the site is known as Qantir.', 'Battle of Kadesh', 'When did this pharaoh fight one of history''s biggest chariot battles?', 'c. May 1274 BCE', -1274, 'year', 'He fought the Battle of Kadesh around May 1274 BCE. During his long reign, he celebrated at least thirteen royal jubilees, more than any other pharaoh we know of.', 'Span 1303-1213 BCE = 90 years (>=15); long reign is standout fact, year chosen.', 'New Kingdom Egypt (19th Dynasty)', 'Ramesses II was succeeded by his thirteenth son, Merneptah, since most of his elder sons had predeceased him after his exceptionally long 66-year reign.', 'Ramesses II celebrated at least thirteen Sed festivals, royal jubilees marking the renewal of a pharaoh''s rule, more than any other known Egyptian king.', 'He fathered over 100 children with his many wives and consorts, including his chief queen Nefertari, for whom he built the celebrated temple at Abu Simbel.', 'New Kingdom Egypt', 'Africa', null, 'Wikipedia/Britannica confirmed birth c.1303 BCE, coronation 31 May 1279 BCE, Kadesh c.1274 BCE, peace treaty c.1259 BCE, death c.1213 BCE.'),
  ('2f79a5b0-1fed-5ed1-bf4b-5c0b77723149', 'P036', 'person', 'tutankhamun', 'Tutankhamun', 'Boy pharaoh of ancient Egypt whose nearly intact tomb, discovered in 1922, became one of archaeology''s greatest finds.', 'draft', 'Where was this boy pharaoh''s tomb found?', 25.7404, 32.6014, 'Tomb KV62, Valley of the Kings, Egypt', 'His tomb, called KV62, was found in the Valley of the Kings in Egypt. It was found nearly untouched in 1922, making it one of the greatest finds in history.', 'Accession as pharaoh', 'When did this young pharaoh take the throne of Egypt?', 'c. 1332 BCE (age ~9)', -1332, 'year', 'He became pharaoh around 1332 BCE, when he was only about nine years old. He brought back worship of Egypt''s old gods after his father had tried to change the religion.', 'Span from birth (c.1341 BCE) to tomb discovery (1922 CE) is over 3,000 years (>=15); year highlights the dramatic ancient-to-modern gap.', 'New Kingdom Egypt (18th Dynasty)', 'Tutankhamun was succeeded as pharaoh by his elderly vizier Ay, who may have strengthened his claim to the throne by marrying Tutankhamun''s widow, Ankhesenamun.', 'As a young pharaoh, Tutankhamun reversed his father Akhenaten''s radical religious revolution, restoring worship of the traditional Egyptian gods, including Amun, after Akhenaten had imposed near-exclusive worship of the sun disk Aten.', 'Tutankhamun suffered from significant health problems, including a club foot and repeated bouts of malaria, and DNA studies suggest his parents were siblings, likely Akhenaten and one of his sisters.', 'New Kingdom Egypt', 'Africa', null, 'Wikipedia ''Tomb of Tutankhamun'' gave tomb coords 25.7404N 32.6014E; discovery 4 Nov 1922 (Howard Carter); reign c.1334/1332-1323 BCE.'),
  ('a886f04c-024b-595f-9233-73719583adc0', 'P037', 'person', 'catherine-the-great', 'Catherine the Great', 'German-born empress who ruled Russia for 34 years, greatly expanding its territory and embracing Enlightenment ideas.', 'draft', 'Where was this future Russian empress born?', 53.4285, 14.5528, 'Stettin, Prussia (modern Szczecin, Poland)', 'This empress was born in Stettin, Prussia, a city now called Szczecin, in Poland. She was born German, not Russian, before she came to rule Russia.', 'Coronation as Empress of Russia', 'When was this empress crowned ruler of Russia?', 'September 22, 1762', 1762, 'day', 'She was crowned Empress of Russia on 22 September 1762. She had changed her name and her religion before marrying into the Russian royal family.', 'Span 1729-1796 = 67 years (>=15); cleanly sequenced timeline, year chosen.', 'Russian Enlightenment', 'Catherine the Great was succeeded by her son, Paul I, upon her death in November 1796.', 'Born a minor German princess in Stettin, Prussia, Catherine was not ethnically Russian; she converted from Lutheranism to Russian Orthodoxy and changed her name from Sophie to Catherine before marrying into the Romanov family.', 'In 1768, Catherine had herself publicly inoculated against smallpox by an English doctor to demonstrate the procedure''s safety, helping spark a wave of inoculation across the Russian Empire.', 'Russian Empire (Russia)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Catherine the Great'' confirmed birth 2 May 1729 Stettin; marriage 21 Aug 1745; coronation 22 Sept 1762; death 17 Nov 1796.'),
  ('c3df857d-fe85-5ebe-9e04-674ab18569d9', 'P038', 'person', 'peter-the-great', 'Peter the Great', 'Tsar who modernized and westernized Russia, founded Saint Petersburg, and transformed the country into an empire.', 'draft', 'In what Russian city was this tsar born?', 55.7558, 37.6173, 'Moscow, Russia', 'This tsar was born in Moscow, Russia. He later modernized Russia and founded the city of Saint Petersburg.', 'Victory at the Battle of Poltava', 'When did this tsar win a major battle that helped make Russia a great power?', 'June 27, 1709 (O.S.)', 1709, 'day', 'He won the Battle of Poltava on 27 June 1709. Years earlier, he had secretly worked as a shipbuilder in Europe to learn new skills for Russia.', 'Span 1672-1721 = 49 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated reign milestones.', 'Russian Empire (Early Modern)', 'Peter the Great was succeeded by his second wife, Catherine I, who became the first woman to rule Russia in her own right after his death in 1725.', 'Peter personally worked incognito as a shipwright in the Netherlands and England during his 1697-98 Grand Embassy, studying Western shipbuilding and technology firsthand before returning to modernize Russia.', 'He imposed a tax on beards in 1698 to pressure Russian nobles into adopting clean-shaven, Western European fashions as part of his broader cultural modernization campaign.', 'Tsardom of Russia / Russian Empire', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Peter the Great'' confirmed birth 9 June 1672 Moscow; co-tsar 1682; founded St. Petersburg 1703; Poltava 1709; Emperor 1721.'),
  ('cc2603c2-2418-54a3-89b1-ba6c91683ecb', 'P039', 'person', 'suleiman-the-magnificent', 'Suleiman the Magnificent', 'Longest-reigning Ottoman sultan, presiding over the empire''s political, military, and cultural golden age.', 'draft', 'Where was this Ottoman sultan born?', 41.0027, 39.7168, 'Trabzon, Ottoman Empire (modern Turkey)', 'This sultan was born in Trabzon, in what is now Turkey. He ruled longer than any other Ottoman sultan and was also a skilled poet.', 'Accession to the Ottoman throne', 'When did this sultan take the Ottoman throne?', 'September 30, 1520', 1520, 'day', 'He became sultan on 30 September 1520. He wrote poetry under the pen name Muhibbi during his long rule.', 'Span 1494-1566 = 72 years (>=15); year chosen given his precisely dated 46-year reign.', 'Ottoman Golden Age', 'Suleiman the Magnificent was succeeded by his son Selim II upon his death in 1566, marking the start of a period of slower Ottoman territorial expansion.', 'Beyond ruling as sultan, Suleiman was an accomplished poet who wrote under the pen name ''Muhibbi'' and produced a substantial body of Ottoman Turkish verse.', 'Suleiman''s reign saw large-scale cultivation and export of tulips from the Ottoman Empire to Europe, a trade that helped set the stage for the tulip craze that later swept the Dutch Republic.', 'Ottoman Empire (modern Turkey)', 'Asia', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 6 Nov 1494 Trabzon; accession 30 Sept 1520; Siege of Vienna 1529; died 6 Sept 1566 Szigetvár.'),
  ('d81bc9ad-4345-5a76-95f4-57087287933f', 'P040', 'person', 'joan-of-arc', 'Joan of Arc', 'French peasant girl who led the French army to key victories during the Hundred Years'' War before being captured and executed.', 'draft', 'Where was this farm girl born before she went on to lead an army into battle?', 48.4431, 5.6758, 'Domrémy-la-Pucelle, Duchy of Bar, France', 'This spot is Domrémy, a small village in France. It''s the birthplace of Joan of Arc, who left her farm to lead French troops during the Hundred Years'' War.', 'Execution at Rouen', 'When was this young military leader put to death?', 'May 30, 1431', 1431, 'day', 'Joan of Arc was executed on May 30, 1431. She could not read or write, so she had others write her letters for her.', 'Span 1412-1430 = 18 years (>=15); 1429 repeats twice, so place is stronger signal than year.', 'Hundred Years'' War', 'Following Joan''s execution, King Charles VII continued the military campaign she had helped reinvigorate, and France went on to win the Hundred Years'' War by 1453; in 1456 a posthumous retrial overturned her conviction, and she was canonized as a Catholic saint in 1920.', 'Joan could not read or write and dictated her correspondence to scribes, although she may have later learned to sign her own name.', 'The name ''Jeanne d''Arc'' was not standard during her lifetime; she referred to herself as ''Jeanne la Pucelle'' (''Joan the Maiden''), and the earliest written record using ''d''Arc'' dates to 1455, over two decades after her death.', 'Kingdom of France (France)', 'Europe', 'Review - execution by burning at the stake; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Joan of Arc'' confirmed birth c.1412 Domrémy; relief of Orléans May 1429; Charles VII coronation Reims 17 July 1429; captured Compiègne May 1430; executed 30 May 1431 Rouen.'),
  ('9bdf95c2-6333-5369-b9d7-1600446b7ac2', 'P041', 'person', 'simon-bolivar', 'Simón Bolívar', 'Venezuelan revolutionary leader known as ''El Libertador'' for winning independence for much of northern South America from Spain.', 'draft', 'Can you find the birthplace of this famous South American freedom fighter?', 10.4806, -66.9036, 'Caracas, Venezuela', 'This is Caracas, in Venezuela. It''s the birthplace of Simón Bolívar, who helped free much of northern South America from Spanish rule.', 'Victory at the Battle of Boyacá', 'When did this leader win a key battle that helped free his homeland from Spain?', 'August 7, 1819', 1819, 'day', 'On August 7, 1819, Bolívar won the Battle of Boyacá. Years earlier, he had promised not to rest until Spain''s rule over South America was broken.', 'Span 1783-1830 = 47 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated campaign timeline.', 'Spanish American Wars of Independence', 'Following his victory at Boyacá, Bolívar liberated New Granada and went on to found Gran Colombia, a union of Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, and Panama that fractured into separate republics shortly after his death in 1830.', 'In 1805, on Rome''s Monte Sacro, Bolívar swore an oath to his tutor Simón Rodríguez that he would not rest until he had broken the chains of Spanish rule over South America.', 'Bolívar narrowly escaped an assassination attempt while in exile in Jamaica in 1815, when a hired killer stabbed his paymaster, who was sleeping in Bolívar''s own hammock, mistaking him for Bolívar.', 'Gran Colombia (modern Venezuela)', 'South America', null, 'Wikipedia/Britannica confirmed birth 24 July 1783 Caracas; Admirable Campaign 1813; Boyacá 7 Aug 1819; Carabobo 24 June 1821; death 17 Dec 1830 Santa Marta.'),
  ('d926d090-39e6-58ef-b8eb-443148127816', 'P042', 'person', 'benjamin-franklin', 'Benjamin Franklin', 'American polymath — printer, scientist, inventor, and diplomat — and a Founding Father of the United States.', 'draft', 'In what city was this American inventor and Founding Father born?', 42.3601, -71.0589, 'Boston, Massachusetts, USA', 'This is Boston, Massachusetts. It''s the birthplace of Benjamin Franklin, who became a famous inventor, writer, and Founding Father of the United States.', 'Kite experiment demonstrating lightning is electricity', 'When did this scientist fly a kite to prove that lightning is electricity?', 'June 1752', 1752, 'month', 'This happened in June 1752. Franklin later used his science skills to map the Gulf Stream ocean current, using reports from whaling ships.', 'Span 1706-1790 = 84 years (>=15); year chosen given clean chronological life milestones.', 'American Enlightenment', 'Franklin was succeeded as United States Minister to France by Thomas Jefferson in 1785, continuing American diplomacy in Europe after Franklin returned home.', 'Franklin charted and named the Gulf Stream after gathering reports from Nantucket whalers, producing a 1770 map intended to speed transatlantic mail ships, though British sea captains largely ignored his findings at the time.', 'Franklin invented bifocal eyeglasses, a flexible urinary catheter, and the Franklin stove, but he deliberately declined to patent any of his inventions, believing they should freely benefit the public.', 'Province of Pennsylvania, British America (United States)', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''Benjamin Franklin'' confirmed birth 17 Jan 1706 Boston; kite experiment June 1752; Declaration signer 1776; death 17 Apr 1790 Philadelphia.'),
  ('74982420-d8aa-5aeb-8e98-eb737f112fc0', 'P043', 'person', 'george-washington', 'George Washington', 'Commander of the Continental Army and the first President of the United States.', 'draft', 'Where was America''s first president born?', 38.1751, -76.9219, 'Westmoreland County, Virginia, USA', 'This is Westmoreland County, Virginia. It''s the birthplace of George Washington, who led the Continental Army and later became the first U.S. President.', 'Inauguration as first U.S. President', 'When did this general become the very first president of his country?', 'April 30, 1789', 1789, 'day', 'Washington was sworn in as president on April 30, 1789. He also ran a secret spy network called the Culper Ring during the war.', 'Span 1732-1799 = 67 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated career milestones.', 'American Revolutionary Era', 'George Washington was succeeded as President of the United States by his own vice president, John Adams, after Washington voluntarily stepped down following two terms in 1797.', 'Washington built one of colonial America''s most extensive espionage networks, the Culper Ring, which supplied crucial intelligence to the Continental Army against the British during the Revolutionary War.', 'Washington was an early advocate of mule breeding in the United States, importing a prized Spanish jack donkey gifted by King Charles III in 1785 to improve American livestock and agriculture.', 'Colony of Virginia, British America (United States)', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 22 Feb 1732 Westmoreland Co.; Continental Army commander 1775; inauguration 30 Apr 1789 New York; death 14 Dec 1799 Mount Vernon.'),
  ('cbc4eba9-c9ab-51da-a4a4-a2498b1f9483', 'P044', 'person', 'thomas-jefferson', 'Thomas Jefferson', 'Principal author of the Declaration of Independence and third President of the United States.', 'draft', 'Where was the man who wrote most of the Declaration of Independence born?', 38.0293, -78.4517, 'Shadwell, Virginia, USA', 'This is Shadwell, Virginia. It''s the birthplace of Thomas Jefferson, who wrote most of the Declaration of Independence and later became the third U.S. President.', 'Drafting the Declaration of Independence', 'When did this founding father write the Declaration of Independence?', 'June-July 1776', 1776, 'month', 'Jefferson drafted the Declaration in June and July of 1776. He later said founding the University of Virginia was one of his proudest achievements.', 'Span 1743-1826 = 83 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated milestones.', 'American Revolutionary Era', 'Jefferson was succeeded as President in 1809 by James Madison, his close political ally, protege, and former Secretary of State.', 'Jefferson founded the University of Virginia in 1819, personally designing its campus (the ''Academical Village'') and curriculum, which he counted among his proudest achievements.', 'As President, Jefferson negotiated the 1803 Louisiana Purchase from Napoleonic France, roughly doubling the territory of the United States for about $15 million.', 'United States of America (Colony/Commonwealth of Virginia)', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 13 Apr 1743 Shadwell; Declaration drafted June-July 1776; presidency 1801-1809; Louisiana Purchase 1803; death 4 July 1826.'),
  ('6d9c3505-c357-5c5b-aa21-047b7c337551', 'P045', 'person', 'winston-churchill', 'Winston Churchill', 'British statesman who led the United Kingdom as Prime Minister through most of World War II.', 'draft', 'Do you know where this British wartime leader was born?', 51.8414, -1.3611, 'Blenheim Palace, Woodstock, England', 'This is Blenheim Palace in Woodstock, England. It''s the birthplace of Winston Churchill, who led Britain through most of World War II.', '''We shall fight on the beaches'' speech to Parliament', 'When did this prime minister give his famous ''fight on the beaches'' speech?', 'June 4, 1940', 1940, 'day', 'Churchill gave that speech on June 4, 1940. He later won the Nobel Prize in Literature for his writing, including his history of World War II.', 'Span 1874-1965 = 91 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated wartime milestones.', 'World War II Era', 'Despite leading Britain to victory in Europe, Churchill was voted out of office in the July 1945 general election and was succeeded as Prime Minister by Labour''s Clement Attlee.', 'Churchill was awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953, largely for his historical and biographical writing, including his multi-volume history of the Second World War.', 'Churchill served two separate terms as Prime Minister (1940-1945 and 1951-1955) and had earlier crossed the floor of Parliament twice, switching between the Conservative and Liberal parties.', 'United Kingdom', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 30 Nov 1874 Blenheim Palace; PM from 10 May 1940; ''fight on the beaches'' speech 4 June 1940 (corrected from earlier draft''s June 18); death 24 Jan 1965.'),
  ('1704b1a3-4673-5ce0-abd5-6deef2162907', 'P046', 'person', 'franklin-d-roosevelt', 'Franklin D. Roosevelt', '32nd U.S. President who led the nation through the Great Depression and most of World War II, serving an unprecedented four terms.', 'draft', 'Where was this four-term American president born?', 41.7909, -73.9327, 'Hyde Park, New York, USA', 'This is Hyde Park, New York. It''s the birthplace of Franklin D. Roosevelt, who led the United States through the Great Depression and most of World War II.', 'First inauguration as President', 'When did this leader first become president during the Great Depression?', 'March 4, 1933', 1933, 'day', 'Roosevelt was first sworn in as president on March 4, 1933. An illness had left him unable to walk since 1921, so he used a wheelchair for the rest of his life.', 'Span 1882-1945 = 63 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated presidency milestones.', 'Great Depression / World War II Era', 'Roosevelt died in office on April 12, 1945, and was succeeded as President by his Vice President, Harry S. Truman.', 'Roosevelt was paralyzed from the waist down in 1921 by an illness long believed to be polio (though some modern researchers argue it may have been Guillain-Barre syndrome), and used a wheelchair for the rest of his life, largely hidden from public view.', 'Roosevelt''s unprecedented four elected terms directly led to the ratification of the 22nd Amendment in 1951, which limits U.S. presidents to two terms in office.', 'United States of America', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 30 Jan 1882 Hyde Park NY; first inauguration 4 Mar 1933; Pearl Harbor/US entry into WWII Dec 1941; died 12 Apr 1945 Warm Springs GA.'),
  ('c6bbdd46-19a1-557e-92ea-946f5a9166a8', 'P047', 'person', 'susan-b-anthony', 'Susan B. Anthony', 'American women''s rights leader who campaigned for decades for women''s suffrage in the United States.', 'draft', 'Point to the birthplace of this women''s rights leader.', 42.6212, -73.1198, 'Adams, Massachusetts, USA', 'This is Adams, Massachusetts. It''s the birthplace of Susan B. Anthony, who spent decades fighting for women''s right to vote in the United States.', 'Arrested for illegally voting in a U.S. presidential election', 'When was this activist arrested for voting when women were not allowed to?', 'November 18, 1872', 1872, 'day', 'Anthony was arrested on November 18, 1872, for voting illegally. She refused to pay her fine, and the government never collected it.', 'Span 1820-1906 = 86 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated activism milestones.', 'Women''s Suffrage Movement', 'Anthony died in 1906, fourteen years before the Nineteenth Amendment guaranteeing women the right to vote was ratified in 1920.', 'In 1872, Anthony was arrested and later convicted for illegally voting in the presidential election in Rochester, New York; she refused to pay the $100 fine and the government never collected it.', 'In 1979, the U.S. Mint issued the Susan B. Anthony dollar coin, making her the first named woman depicted on U.S. circulating currency.', 'United States of America', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 15 Feb 1820 Adams MA; illegal voting arrest 18 Nov 1872 Rochester NY; co-founded NWSA 1869; died 13 Mar 1906, 14 years before the 19th Amendment (1920).'),
  ('79f4b6e4-8b52-5c46-8460-5debc7d792db', 'P048', 'person', 'rosa-parks', 'Rosa Parks', 'American civil rights activist whose refusal to give up her bus seat sparked the Montgomery Bus Boycott.', 'draft', 'Where was this civil rights hero born?', 32.4244, -85.7091, 'Tuskegee, Alabama, USA', 'This is Tuskegee, Alabama. It''s the birthplace of Rosa Parks, whose refusal to give up her bus seat helped spark the Montgomery Bus Boycott.', 'Refuses to give up her seat on a Montgomery bus', 'When did this woman refuse to give up her seat on a bus?', 'December 1, 1955', 1955, 'day', 'This happened on December 1, 1955, in Montgomery, Alabama. Parks had already spent over ten years working for the NAACP before that day.', 'Span 1913-2005 = 92 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated civil-rights milestones.', 'Civil Rights Movement', 'Parks''s arrest led directly to the 381-day Montgomery Bus Boycott, whose leadership propelled a young pastor named Martin Luther King Jr. to national prominence in the civil rights movement.', 'Before her famous arrest, Parks had served for over a decade as secretary of the Montgomery chapter of the NAACP, investigating cases of racial violence and assault.', 'Upon her death in 2005, Parks became the first woman in American history to lie in honor in the U.S. Capitol Rotunda.', 'United States of America', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 4 Feb 1913 Tuskegee AL; bus incident 1 Dec 1955 Montgomery AL; boycott lasted 381 days into 1956; died 24 Oct 2005.'),
  ('e7c926cc-2984-5d40-b386-06005db3d4eb', 'P049', 'person', 'florence-nightingale', 'Florence Nightingale', 'British nurse and social reformer who founded modern nursing during the Crimean War.', 'draft', 'Where was this pioneering nurse born?', 43.7696, 11.2558, 'Florence, Italy (birthplace, hence her name)', 'This is Florence, Italy. Nurse Florence Nightingale was named after this city, and she went on to found modern nursing during the Crimean War.', 'Arrives at Scutari to nurse Crimean War wounded', 'When did this nurse arrive to care for wounded soldiers during a war?', 'November 1854', 1854, 'month', 'She arrived at Scutari in November 1854. Nightingale later invented a new kind of chart to show that dirty conditions, not battle wounds, killed most soldiers.', 'Span 1820-1910 = 90 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated milestones.', 'Victorian Era', 'Nightingale''s Crimean War reforms led her to found the Nightingale Training School for Nurses at St Thomas'' Hospital in London in 1860, the world''s first secular nursing school, which set the template for modern professional nursing worldwide.', 'Nightingale was a pioneering statistician who invented the ''polar area diagram,'' an early form of pie chart, to show visually that poor sanitation, not battle wounds, caused most soldier deaths in the Crimean War.', 'In 1907, Nightingale became the first woman awarded Britain''s Order of Merit, conferred by King Edward VII.', 'United Kingdom', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 12 May 1820 Florence Italy; Scutari Nov 1854; founded Nightingale Training School 1860; died 13 Aug 1910 London.'),
  ('6cd33d3b-9eae-583a-ad39-83453e71f356', 'P050', 'person', 'louis-pasteur', 'Louis Pasteur', 'French chemist and microbiologist who developed pasteurization and pioneered the germ theory of disease.', 'draft', 'In what city was this disease-fighting scientist born?', 47.0975, 5.4897, 'Dole, France', 'This is Dole, France. It''s the birthplace of Louis Pasteur, who discovered how germs cause disease and invented the process of pasteurization.', 'First successful human rabies vaccination (Joseph Meister)', 'When did this scientist give the first successful rabies vaccine to a person?', 'July 6, 1885', 1885, 'day', 'On July 6, 1885, Pasteur successfully vaccinated a boy named Joseph Meister against rabies. Four years earlier, he had also proven his anthrax vaccine worked in a famous public test.', 'Span 1822-1895 = 73 years (>=15); year chosen given precisely dated scientific milestones.', '19th Century France', 'Pasteur''s work on germ theory and vaccination led to the founding of the Pasteur Institute in Paris in 1887, which he directed until his death and which remains a leading center for infectious-disease research today.', 'Pasteur developed a vaccine for anthrax in 1881, proving it in a famous public field trial at Pouilly-le-Fort, France, that convinced skeptical farmers and scientists of vaccination''s power.', 'Early in his career, Pasteur''s research into diseases affecting silkworms saved France''s struggling silk industry from collapse in the 1860s.', 'France', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia confirmed birth 27 Dec 1822 Dole France; pasteurization process 1864; rabies vaccine for Joseph Meister 6 July 1885; died 28 Sept 1895.'),
  ('8a2b5079-cda1-598b-8c39-6c13040f3032', 'E006', 'event', 'fall-of-constantinople', 'Fall of Constantinople', 'The Ottoman conquest of the Byzantine capital, ending the Byzantine Empire after over a thousand years.', 'draft', 'Can you find the ancient capital city that fell after this famous siege?', 41.0082, 28.9784, 'Constantinople (modern Istanbul, Turkey)', 'This is Constantinople, known today as Istanbul, Turkey. Ottoman forces captured this city, ending the Byzantine Empire after more than a thousand years.', 'The city falls to Ottoman forces', 'When did this ancient capital finally fall after a long siege?', 'May 29, 1453', 1453, 'day', 'The city fell on May 29, 1453. The Ottomans used giant bronze cannons to break through walls that had protected the city for a thousand years.', 'Span from siege start (April 6) to fall (May 29) 1453 is under 2 months (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Late Byzantine Empire', 'Constantinople became the new Ottoman capital, and Sultan Mehmed II, thereafter known as ''the Conqueror,'' converted the Hagia Sophia into a mosque and made the city the seat of Ottoman power for the next 470 years.', 'The 53-day siege saw the Ottomans deploy massive bronze cannons designed by the engineer Orban, among the largest siege weapons used up to that time, to breach the ancient Theodosian Walls that had protected the city for a thousand years.', 'The fall sent a wave of Greek scholars fleeing to Italy with ancient manuscripts, a migration many historians credit with helping fuel the Italian Renaissance.', 'Byzantine Empire / Ottoman Empire (modern Turkey)', 'Europe', 'Review - siege and conquest with loss of life; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Fall of Constantinople'' confirmed siege began 6 Apr 1453, city fell 29 May 1453, Sultan Mehmed II''s forces, Byzantine Emperor Constantine XI died in the battle.'),
  ('dec7cdff-252a-51f1-a00e-47f07e0f0864', 'E007', 'event', 'storming-of-the-bastille', 'Storming of the Bastille', 'Parisian revolutionaries stormed the Bastille fortress-prison, a flashpoint that ignited the French Revolution.', 'draft', 'Where did angry crowds storm a prison fortress to start a revolution?', 48.8531, 2.3691, 'Bastille, Paris, France', 'This happened at the Bastille in Paris, France. Crowds stormed this fortress-prison, and the event became a spark that lit the French Revolution.', 'The Bastille is stormed by revolutionaries', 'When did crowds storm a famous prison fortress in Paris?', 'July 14, 1789', 1789, 'day', 'The Bastille was stormed on July 14, 1789. Even though it was feared as a royal prison, only seven prisoners were actually inside that day.', 'Span of events within 1789 is under a year (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'French Revolution', 'The fall of the Bastille ignited the wider French Revolution, and within weeks the National Constituent Assembly abolished feudal privileges and adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen in August 1789.', 'Despite its fearsome reputation as a royal state prison, only seven prisoners were actually being held in the Bastille at the time it was stormed.', 'July 14 is now celebrated annually in France as Bastille Day (Fete Nationale), the country''s official national holiday.', 'Kingdom of France (modern France)', 'Europe', 'Review - violent uprising with casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Storming of the Bastille'' confirmed date 14 July 1789, Paris; sparked by fears of royal crackdown; became symbol of the French Revolution, now Bastille Day.'),
  ('10a315d9-2c0c-5434-87d6-9919acb68130', 'E008', 'event', 'signing-of-the-declaration-of-independence', 'Signing of the Declaration of Independence', 'Delegates of the Second Continental Congress approved the document declaring the American colonies'' independence from Britain.', 'draft', 'Where did leaders meet to approve a famous declaration of freedom?', 39.9489, -75.15, 'Pennsylvania State House (Independence Hall), Philadelphia, USA', 'This is Independence Hall in Philadelphia. Delegates met here to approve the Declaration of Independence.', 'Declaration formally adopted by Congress', 'When did American leaders formally approve the document declaring their freedom from Britain?', 'July 4, 1776', 1776, 'day', 'Congress adopted the Declaration on July 4, 1776. Most delegates did not actually sign the paper until August 2, and a few signed even later.', 'Span of drafting-to-signing events in 1776 is under a year (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'American Revolution', 'The Declaration''s adoption was followed by continued war with Britain, and following American victory it led to the 1783 Treaty of Paris, in which Britain formally recognized United States independence.', 'Although the engrossed document is dated July 4, 1776, most delegates did not actually sign it until August 2, 1776, and a few signers added their names even later.', 'John Hancock, president of the Continental Congress, signed his name first and largest of all the signers, giving rise to ''John Hancock'' as an American colloquialism for a signature.', 'United States of America (British American Colonies)', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''United States Declaration of Independence'' confirmed adoption 4 July 1776 at Independence Hall, Philadelphia; most delegates signed 2 Aug 1776.'),
  ('03d71848-39fa-50bd-877d-ab04644c43af', 'E009', 'event', 'attack-on-pearl-harbor', 'Attack on Pearl Harbor', 'A surprise Japanese military strike on the U.S. Pacific Fleet that brought the United States into World War II.', 'draft', 'Where did a surprise attack pull a whole country into a world war?', 21.3469, -157.9797, 'Pearl Harbor, Oahu, Hawaii, USA', 'This is Pearl Harbor in Hawaii. A surprise attack on the U.S. Navy base here pulled the United States into World War II.', 'Surprise attack on the U.S. naval base', 'When did a surprise attack hit a U.S. naval base and change a nation''s course?', 'December 7, 1941', 1941, 'day', 'The attack happened on December 7, 1941. Nearly half of all the deaths that day happened on one single ship, the USS Arizona.', 'Span of the attack''s timeline within one morning (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'World War II', 'The attack led directly to the United States declaring war on Japan on December 8, 1941, and, after Germany and Italy declared war on the U.S. days later, to full American entry into World War II.', 'Nearly half of all American deaths in the attack, 1,177 sailors and Marines, occurred aboard a single ship, the USS Arizona, after a bomb detonated its forward ammunition magazine.', 'The attack fueled anti-Japanese sentiment that led the U.S. government to forcibly intern over 110,000 Japanese Americans, most of them U.S. citizens, for the duration of the war.', 'United States of America (Territory of Hawaii)', 'North America', 'Review - military attack with significant loss of life; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Attack on Pearl Harbor'' confirmed date 7 Dec 1941, Oahu Hawaii; US declared war on Japan 8 Dec 1941; 2,403 Americans killed.'),
  ('308b32cc-4cbc-5be6-a9fc-e1533a82af5c', 'E010', 'event', 'assassination-of-archduke-franz-ferdinand', 'Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand', 'The assassination of the Austro-Hungarian heir in Sarajevo, the spark that triggered World War I.', 'draft', 'Where was a royal heir shot in an event that helped start a world war?', 43.8586, 18.4131, 'Sarajevo, Bosnia and Herzegovina', 'This is Sarajevo, in Bosnia and Herzegovina. A gunman shot Archduke Franz Ferdinand here, an act that helped spark World War I.', 'Franz Ferdinand and Sophie are shot', 'When was a royal heir shot in a moment that helped trigger a world war?', 'June 28, 1914', 1914, 'day', 'The shooting happened on June 28, 1914. The gunman only got his shot because the driver made a wrong turn and stopped right in front of him.', 'Span of the day''s events (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Pre-World War I Europe', 'The assassination triggered the diplomatic ''July Crisis,'' and exactly one month later, on July 28, 1914, Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia, setting off the alliance system that escalated into World War I.', 'The assassin, 19-year-old Gavrilo Princip, belonged to the Bosnian Serb nationalist group the Black Hand and got his fatal shot only after the archduke''s driver took a wrong turn and stopped the car directly in front of him.', 'Franz Ferdinand had already survived one assassination attempt earlier that same morning, when a bomb thrown at his motorcade bounced off his car and wounded occupants of the vehicle behind him.', 'Austria-Hungary (Sarajevo, modern Bosnia and Herzegovina)', 'Europe', 'Review - assassination; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand'' confirmed date 28 June 1914 Sarajevo; shot by Gavrilo Princip; triggered July Crisis leading to WWI (Aug 1914).'),
  ('2dd7b080-81e5-50a8-8512-fc19e74fd4af', 'E011', 'event', 'apollo-11-moon-landing', 'Apollo 11 Moon Landing', 'The first crewed spacecraft to land on the Moon, with Neil Armstrong becoming the first human to walk on its surface.', 'draft', 'Can you find the spot where humans first landed and walked outside Earth?', 28.5729, -80.649, 'Sea of Tranquility, the Moon (mission launched from Kennedy Space Center, Florida)', 'This is the Sea of Tranquility on the Moon. It''s where astronauts first landed and walked on a world beyond Earth.', 'Lunar landing and first moonwalk', 'When did humans first land on the Moon and walk on its surface?', 'July 20, 1969', 1969, 'day', 'The landing happened on July 20, 1969. One astronaut stayed in orbit and lost contact with Earth for 48 minutes on each pass behind the Moon.', 'Span of the mission (July 16-24, 1969) is days (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Cold War Space Race', 'Apollo 11 was followed by five more successful crewed lunar landings through Apollo 17 in 1972, after which NASA shifted its focus to the Skylab space station and later the Space Shuttle program.', 'While Armstrong and Aldrin walked on the Moon, astronaut Michael Collins orbited alone in the command module Columbia, losing radio contact with Earth for about 48 minutes on each orbit''s far side of the Moon.', 'The lunar module Eagle landed with only about 25 seconds of fuel remaining after Armstrong had to manually pilot it past a boulder field to avoid a rock-strewn crater.', 'United States of America', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''Apollo 11'' confirmed launch 16 July 1969 Kennedy Space Center; lunar landing 20 July 1969; Armstrong''s first step 20/21 July 1969 (UTC); splashdown 24 July 1969.'),
  ('517cb2bf-57bd-5c08-acbe-e40760bb482d', 'E012', 'event', 'chernobyl-nuclear-disaster', 'Chernobyl Nuclear Disaster', 'A catastrophic explosion and meltdown at a Soviet nuclear power plant, the worst nuclear accident in history.', 'draft', 'Where did the worst nuclear power plant accident in history happen?', 51.3894, 30.0997, 'Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant, Pripyat, Ukrainian SSR (modern Ukraine)', 'This is the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant near Pripyat, in what is now Ukraine. It was the site of the worst nuclear accident in history.', 'Reactor 4 explodes during a safety test', 'When did a nuclear reactor explode during a safety test?', 'April 26, 1986', 1986, 'day', 'The explosion happened on April 26, 1986. Soviet officials stayed silent for two days, until Sweden detected radiation far away and started asking questions.', 'Span of the explosion-to-evacuation timeline (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Late Soviet Union', 'The disaster forced the Soviet government toward greater transparency under Gorbachev''s glasnost policy, and is widely cited by historians, and by Gorbachev himself, as a major factor that accelerated the political and economic collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991.', 'The Soviet government did not publicly acknowledge the disaster until two days later, after Swedish monitoring stations roughly 1,100 kilometers away detected abnormally high radiation levels and demanded an explanation.', 'A massive steel structure called the New Safe Confinement was completed in 2016-2018 to seal the damaged reactor, replacing the original hastily built concrete ''sarcophagus'' from 1986.', 'Soviet Union (modern Ukraine)', 'Europe', 'Review - nuclear disaster with radiation exposure and casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Chernobyl disaster'' confirmed explosion 26 Apr 1986 01:23 local time; Pripyat evacuated 27 Apr 1986; exclusion zone established May 1986.'),
  ('2266c46e-ee61-5514-b99b-b4154c8a0471', 'E013', 'event', 'sinking-of-the-titanic', 'Sinking of the Titanic', 'The ''unsinkable'' RMS Titanic struck an iceberg on her maiden voyage and sank in the North Atlantic, killing over 1,500 people.', 'draft', 'Where did a famous ''unsinkable'' ship sink after hitting an iceberg?', 41.726, -49.9481, 'North Atlantic Ocean, approx. 370 miles south of Newfoundland (voyage departed Southampton, England)', 'This spot is in the North Atlantic Ocean, south of Newfoundland. The Titanic hit an iceberg here and sank, killing over 1,500 people.', 'Titanic strikes an iceberg and sinks', 'When did this famous ship hit an iceberg and sink on its very first trip?', 'April 14-15, 1912', 1912, 'day', 'The Titanic sank on the night of April 14-15, 1912. A nearby ship missed the distress signals because its radio operator had turned off the radio for the night.', 'Span of the voyage (April 10-15, 1912) is days (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Edwardian Era', 'The disaster''s death toll led directly to the creation of the International Ice Patrol and the first International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS) in 1914, reforms that still govern maritime safety today.', 'The nearby ship SS Californian was only a few miles away but missed Titanic''s distress rockets because its lone wireless operator had switched off the radio for the night about ten minutes before the collision.', 'The rescue ship RMS Carpathia raced roughly 58 miles through iceberg-strewn waters to reach the scene, arriving about an hour and a half after Titanic sank and rescuing all of the roughly 705 survivors.', 'United Kingdom (RMS Titanic, White Star Line, registered in Liverpool, England)', 'Europe', 'Review - maritime disaster with over 1,500 deaths; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''RMS Titanic'' confirmed departed Southampton 10 Apr 1912; struck iceberg 14 Apr 1912 23:40; sank 15 Apr 1912 02:20; over 1,500 died.'),
  ('be1b0692-739f-58af-9229-3a4a046cdcd2', 'E014', 'event', 'the-great-fire-of-london', 'The Great Fire of London', 'A massive fire swept through the medieval City of London, destroying most of the city over four days.', 'draft', 'Where did a huge fire burn down most of an old city over several days?', 51.5099, -0.0919, 'City of London, England', 'This is the City of London, England. A massive fire destroyed most of this old city over four days.', 'Fire breaks out and spreads across the city', 'When did a giant fire break out and burn through a historic city?', 'September 2-6, 1666', 1666, 'day', 'The fire burned from September 2 to 6, 1666. Only a few deaths were officially recorded, but many believe the real number was much higher.', 'Span of the fire''s four days (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Restoration England', 'The fire led to the Rebuilding of London Act 1666, and although Christopher Wren''s ambitious grid redesign for the city was rejected, he went on to design 51 new parish churches and the new St Paul''s Cathedral in the following decades.', 'Official records list only a handful of confirmed deaths, commonly cited as around six, though many historians believe the true toll was far higher since deaths among London''s poor went unrecorded.', 'An innocent French watchmaker named Robert Hubert falsely confessed to starting the fire and was hanged for it in 1666, even though evidence later suggested he was not even in London when the fire began.', 'Kingdom of England (modern United Kingdom)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Great Fire of London'' confirmed fire started 2 Sept 1666 in a Pudding Lane bakery, extinguished by 6 Sept 1666; destroyed ~13,200 houses; official death toll recorded as low, likely undercount.'),
  ('e0ca0b67-59c0-51ad-a13b-187300d600a2', 'E015', 'event', 'eruption-of-mount-vesuvius-destruction-of-pompeii', 'Eruption of Mount Vesuvius (Destruction of Pompeii)', 'A catastrophic volcanic eruption buried the Roman cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum in ash, preserving them for millennia.', 'draft', 'Can you find the Roman city that was buried by a volcano?', 40.7461, 14.4989, 'Pompeii, near Naples, Italy', 'This is Pompeii, near Naples, Italy. A huge volcanic eruption buried this Roman city in ash, keeping it frozen in time for centuries.', 'Vesuvius erupts, burying Pompeii', 'When did a volcano erupt and bury a Roman city in ash?', '79 CE (traditionally August 24; some evidence suggests October)', 79, 'year', 'This eruption happened in the year 79 CE. It released roughly 100,000 times more heat energy than the atomic bombs dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.', 'Span of the eruption''s two-day event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Ancient Rome (Flavian Dynasty)', 'The eruption prompted the newly enthroned Emperor Titus to organize one of history''s first documented disaster-relief efforts, funding aid for survivors and visiting the affected region in person.', 'The eruption released an estimated 100,000 times the thermal energy of the atomic bombs dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.', 'Nearby Herculaneum was spared the ash fall but was instead buried under roughly 23 meters of material from pyroclastic surges reaching 220-260°C, a different fate than Pompeii''s.', 'Roman Empire (modern Italy)', 'Europe', 'Review - natural disaster with mass casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD'' confirmed traditional date 24 Aug 79 CE per Pliny the Younger''s letters, though a charcoal inscription and other evidence suggest an autumn date (October) is more likely; exact date remains disputed among scholars.'),
  ('6ed41f43-c5b0-5f5e-b973-447817838801', 'E016', 'event', 'the-black-death', 'The Black Death', 'A devastating pandemic of bubonic plague that killed an estimated 25-50 million people across Europe, Asia, and North Africa.', 'draft', 'Where did a deadly plague first arrive in Europe, sailing in on ships?', 37.5079, 15.083, 'Europe (pandemic traced to origins in Central/East Asia; arrived in Europe via Sicily)', 'The plague arrived in Europe through Sicily. This deadly disease, called the Black Death, went on to kill tens of millions of people across Europe, Asia, and North Africa.', 'Plague arrives in Europe via Sicily', 'When did a deadly plague first reach Europe by ship?', 'October 1347', 1347, 'month', 'The plague reached Europe in October 1347. It was caused by bacteria spread mainly by fleas that lived on black rats.', 'Span of the pandemic''s main European wave (1347-1351) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Late Middle Ages', 'The resulting labor shortages helped erode feudalism across Europe, as surviving peasants gained bargaining power and higher wages in the decades that followed.', 'The pandemic was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis, spread primarily by fleas carried on black rats, with pneumonic cases also spreading via respiratory droplets.', 'Plague outbreaks recurred across Europe for centuries afterward, with the disease present somewhere on the continent in nearly every year between 1346 and 1671.', 'Europe (pandemic entered via the Kingdom of Sicily before spreading continent-wide)', 'Europe', 'Review - pandemic with massive death toll; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Black Death'' confirmed arrival in Sicily October 1347 via Genoese trading ships; spread across Europe 1347-1351; estimated 25-50 million deaths (30-60% of Europe''s population).'),
  ('95836cac-f283-5f2d-84f2-ae882b2dc9ba', 'E017', 'event', 'the-boston-tea-party', 'The Boston Tea Party', 'American colonists boarded British ships and dumped an entire shipment of tea into Boston Harbor to protest taxation without representation.', 'draft', 'Where did colonists dump a whole shipload of tea into the water?', 42.3554, -71.0509, 'Boston Harbor, Massachusetts, USA', 'This happened in Boston Harbor, in Massachusetts. Angry colonists dumped chests of tea into the water to protest a tax they thought was unfair.', 'Colonists dump tea into the harbor', 'When did colonists dump tea into the harbor to protest an unfair tax?', 'December 16, 1773', 1773, 'day', 'This happened on December 16, 1773. Protesters known as the Sons of Liberty dumped 342 chests of tea, worth a huge amount of money, into the sea.', 'Span of the single night''s event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'American Revolution (Colonial Era)', 'Britain retaliated with the Coercive Acts (the ''Intolerable Acts'') of 1774, which further united the colonies and helped set the stage for the American Revolution.', 'Participants organized by the Sons of Liberty destroyed 342 chests of tea, roughly 92,000 pounds, valued at about £18,000 at the time.', 'Many participants disguised themselves as Mohawk warriors, partly to symbolize a distinct American identity separate from Britain.', 'British America (Province of Massachusetts Bay; modern United States)', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''Boston Tea Party'' confirmed date 16 Dec 1773, Boston Harbor; Sons of Liberty destroyed 342 chests of tea; protest against the Tea Act.'),
  ('f5b98b52-c632-53c6-80cf-0acc9240c4da', 'E018', 'event', 'the-battle-of-hastings', 'The Battle of Hastings', 'William the Conqueror''s Norman army defeated King Harold II''s English forces, leading to the Norman Conquest of England.', 'draft', 'Where was this battle fought that changed who ruled England?', 50.9115, 0.4869, 'Battle, East Sussex, England (near Hastings)', 'This battle was fought near Hastings, in East Sussex, England. A Norman army beat the English army here and took control of the whole country.', 'The battle is fought and King Harold II is killed', 'When was this battle fought, the one that ended with an English king''s death?', 'October 14, 1066', 1066, 'day', 'This battle was fought on October 14, 1066. The English king, Harold II, had just marched his tired army 200 miles to reach the fight.', 'Span of the single-day battle and its immediate aftermath (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Norman Conquest (Medieval England)', 'William the Conqueror was crowned King of England on Christmas Day 1066, founding the Norman dynasty that replaced Anglo-Saxon rule.', 'Before facing William, Harold II had just marched his army roughly 200 miles south after defeating a Norwegian invasion at the Battle of Stamford Bridge only weeks earlier.', 'The battle and its lead-up are depicted in the Bayeux Tapestry, a nearly 70-meter embroidered narrative created within a few decades of the event.', 'Kingdom of England', 'Europe', 'Review - battle with significant casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Battle of Hastings'' confirmed date 14 Oct 1066; William Duke of Normandy defeated King Harold II; Harold killed in battle; William crowned King of England 25 Dec 1066.'),
  ('f1e8970e-a254-5079-9655-2b395508ff3e', 'E019', 'event', 'the-battle-of-waterloo', 'The Battle of Waterloo', 'The Duke of Wellington and Prussian forces under Blücher decisively defeated Napoleon Bonaparte, ending his rule of France.', 'draft', 'Where was this battle fought that finally ended one famous general''s rule?', 50.6803, 4.4009, 'Waterloo, near Brussels, Belgium', 'This battle happened near Waterloo, close to Brussels in Belgium. British and Prussian armies defeated Napoleon here and ended his rule over France for good.', 'Napoleon is decisively defeated', 'When was this battle fought that finally defeated Napoleon?', 'June 18, 1815', 1815, 'day', 'This battle happened on June 18, 1815. It ended Napoleon''s short comeback, called the Hundred Days, after he had escaped exile on the island of Elba.', 'Span of the single-day battle (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Napoleonic Wars', 'Napoleon abdicated for the second time four days after the defeat and was exiled by the British to the remote island of Saint Helena, where he died in 1821.', 'The battle ended the ''Hundred Days,'' Napoleon''s brief return to power after escaping exile on the island of Elba.', 'Roughly 50,000 soldiers were killed or wounded in a single day of fighting, making it one of the bloodiest single days of the Napoleonic Wars.', 'United Kingdom of the Netherlands (modern Belgium)', 'Europe', 'Review - battle with significant casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Battle of Waterloo'' confirmed date 18 June 1815, near Waterloo (modern Belgium); Duke of Wellington and Gebhard von Blücher defeated Napoleon; ended the Napoleonic Wars.'),
  ('ba30fc4e-ef81-54ec-9836-f45835046de5', 'E020', 'event', 'the-battle-of-gettysburg', 'The Battle of Gettysburg', 'The largest and bloodiest battle of the American Civil War, a turning point that halted the Confederate invasion of the North.', 'draft', 'Where was this huge battle fought during a civil war?', 39.8309, -77.2311, 'Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, USA', 'This battle was fought in Gettysburg, Pennsylvania. It was the biggest battle of the American Civil War and stopped an army from pushing further north.', 'The three-day battle concludes with Union victory', 'When did this three-day battle end with a big win for one side?', 'July 1-3, 1863', 1863, 'day', 'This battle ran from July 1 to July 3, 1863. Tens of thousands of soldiers were killed, hurt, or captured, making it the costliest battle of the war.', 'Span of the three-day battle (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'American Civil War', 'The Union victory, combined with the fall of Vicksburg the following day, marked the turning point of the war; four months later Lincoln delivered the Gettysburg Address at the battlefield''s dedication.', 'Combined casualties for both armies totaled an estimated 46,000-51,000 men killed, wounded, captured, or missing, making it the costliest battle of the entire war.', 'On the battle''s final day, roughly 12,500 Confederate soldiers attempted the doomed Pickett''s Charge against the Union center, suffering about 50 percent casualties.', 'United States (Union vs. Confederacy)', 'North America', 'Review - Civil War battle with massive casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Battle of Gettysburg'' confirmed dates 1-3 July 1863; Union Army under Meade defeated Confederates under Lee; ~51,000 combined casualties; Lincoln''s Gettysburg Address followed 19 Nov 1863.'),
  ('c4541a28-b817-59a9-bee1-a0c2b69bb6d0', 'E021', 'event', 'the-battle-of-trafalgar', 'The Battle of Trafalgar', 'A decisive British naval victory over the combined French and Spanish fleets during the Napoleonic Wars, securing British naval supremacy.', 'draft', 'Where did a British fleet win a huge victory at sea?', 36.1793, -6.0333, 'Cape Trafalgar, off the coast of Spain', 'This battle was fought off Cape Trafalgar, near the coast of Spain. The British navy won a huge victory here and controlled the seas for years after.', 'The British fleet under Nelson wins a decisive victory', 'When did the British navy win this famous sea battle?', 'October 21, 1805', 1805, 'day', 'This battle happened on October 21, 1805. Before the fight, Admiral Nelson sent his famous message: ''England expects that every man will do his duty.''', 'Span of the single-day battle (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Napoleonic Wars', 'The victory secured British naval supremacy for the following century, but Admiral Horatio Nelson was mortally wounded during the battle and died shortly after learning of the win.', 'Nelson signaled the fleet before battle with the now-famous message, ''England expects that every man will do his duty.''', 'The British fleet lost no ships in the battle itself, while the Franco-Spanish fleet had 18 vessels captured or destroyed.', 'Kingdom of Great Britain (battle fought off Cape Trafalgar, Spain)', 'Europe', 'Review - naval battle with casualties, including Nelson''s death; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Battle of Trafalgar'' confirmed date 21 Oct 1805, off Cape Trafalgar, Spain; Admiral Horatio Nelson led British fleet to victory but was mortally wounded and died during the battle.'),
  ('4eae7702-c2e2-524d-8710-e7c5c0bb9027', 'E022', 'event', 'the-battle-of-thermopylae', 'The Battle of Thermopylae', 'A small Greek force, famously including 300 Spartans, held off a massive Persian army at a narrow mountain pass for three days.', 'draft', 'Where did a small army hold off a much bigger army at a narrow mountain pass?', 38.7975, 22.5372, 'Thermopylae, Greece', 'This stand happened at Thermopylae, in Greece. A small Greek force, including famous Spartan soldiers, blocked a huge Persian army at this narrow pass.', 'The Greek stand at the pass', 'When did Greek soldiers make their famous stand at a mountain pass?', 'August or September, 480 BCE', -480, 'year', 'This happened around August or September of 480 BCE. About 7,000 Greek soldiers fought here in total, not just the famous 300 Spartans.', 'Span of the three-day battle (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Classical Greece (Greco-Persian Wars)', 'Despite the defeat, the delay allowed Athens time to evacuate before the Persians burned it, and later that same year the Greeks won a decisive naval victory at the Battle of Salamis, turning the tide of the war.', 'The Greek defenders numbered roughly 7,000 in total, including contingents of Thespians, Thebans, and other city-states, far more than just the famous 300 Spartans.', 'The Greek position was outflanked after a local resident named Ephialtes showed the Persians a hidden mountain path around the pass, betraying Leonidas''s force in exchange for a reward.', 'Greek city-states, led by Sparta (modern Greece)', 'Europe', 'Review - ancient battle with significant casualties; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Battle of Thermopylae'' confirmed year 480 BCE during Xerxes I''s invasion of Greece; exact month disputed between scholars (August most commonly cited, some argue September); led by King Leonidas I of Sparta.'),
  ('996069e4-d7fe-5bb2-aec7-018564052dff', 'E023', 'event', 'assassination-of-julius-caesar', 'Assassination of Julius Caesar', 'Roman senators, including Brutus and Cassius, stabbed Julius Caesar to death in the Senate, fearing his growing power would end the Republic.', 'draft', 'Where was this powerful Roman leader killed by his own senators?', 41.8955, 12.4767, 'Theatre of Pompey, Rome, Roman Republic', 'This happened at the Theatre of Pompey, in Rome. Roman senators, including Brutus and Cassius, stabbed Julius Caesar here because they feared he had too much power.', 'Caesar is assassinated by senators', 'When was this powerful Roman leader killed by senators who feared him?', 'March 15, 44 BCE (the Ides of March)', -44, 'day', 'This happened on March 15, 44 BCE, a day known as the Ides of March. As many as 60 senators took part, though only one stab wound was actually fatal.', 'Span of the assassination''s single-day event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Late Roman Republic', 'The assassination triggered years of civil war, from which Caesar''s adopted heir Octavian emerged victorious, becoming Rome''s first emperor as Augustus in 27 BCE.', 'As many as 60 senators took part in the conspiracy, and Caesar was stabbed 23 times, though ancient physicians determined only one wound was fatal.', 'Far from restoring the Republic as the conspirators intended, the assassination ultimately hastened its collapse and the rise of one-man imperial rule.', 'Roman Republic', 'Europe', 'Review - assassination; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Assassination of Julius Caesar'' confirmed date 15 March 44 BCE at the Theatre of Pompey, Rome; led by Marcus Brutus and Gaius Cassius Longinus; over 60 senators involved in the conspiracy.'),
  ('4a7fd2b8-22ec-5cff-ada6-35ada2de10e2', 'E024', 'event', 'assassination-of-abraham-lincoln', 'Assassination of Abraham Lincoln', 'President Abraham Lincoln was shot by John Wilkes Booth at Ford''s Theatre days after the Civil War''s end, dying the next morning.', 'draft', 'Where was this president shot while watching a play?', 38.8973, -77.0254, 'Ford''s Theatre, Washington, D.C., USA', 'This happened at Ford''s Theatre, in Washington, D.C. President Abraham Lincoln was shot here just days after the Civil War ended.', 'Lincoln is shot at Ford''s Theatre', 'When was this president shot while watching a play?', 'April 14, 1865 (died April 15)', 1865, 'day', 'He was shot on April 14, 1865, and died early the next morning. That same night, the plotters also stabbed and badly hurt the Secretary of State.', 'Span of the assassination''s overnight timeline (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'American Civil War / Reconstruction Era', 'Vice President Andrew Johnson was sworn in as president within hours of Lincoln''s death, inheriting the task of overseeing Reconstruction.', 'John Wilkes Booth''s conspiracy also targeted Secretary of State William Seward, who was stabbed and severely wounded that same night, while a would-be assassin sent after Vice President Johnson lost his nerve.', 'Booth was tracked down and killed by Union soldiers 12 days later on a Virginia farm.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - assassination; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Assassination of Abraham Lincoln'' confirmed shot 14 Apr 1865 at Ford''s Theatre by John Wilkes Booth; died 15 Apr 1865 at the Petersen House.'),
  ('b213a16d-8cca-5403-9a2c-2ce464be9f66', 'E025', 'event', 'assassination-of-john-f-kennedy', 'Assassination of John F. Kennedy', 'President John F. Kennedy was fatally shot while riding in a motorcade through Dallas, Texas, one of the most documented assassinations in history.', 'draft', 'Where was this president shot while riding through a city in a car?', 32.7799, -96.8083, 'Dealey Plaza, Dallas, Texas, USA', 'This happened at Dealey Plaza, in Dallas, Texas. President John F. Kennedy was shot here while riding through the city in his motorcade.', 'Kennedy is shot while riding in his motorcade', 'When was this president shot while riding through a city in his car?', 'November 22, 1963', 1963, 'day', 'He was shot on November 22, 1963. The man accused of the shooting was himself shot and killed two days later, live on national television.', 'Span of the single-day event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Cold War Era (20th Century)', 'Vice President Lyndon B. Johnson was sworn in as president aboard Air Force One at Dallas Love Field less than two hours after Kennedy''s death.', 'Alleged shooter Lee Harvey Oswald was himself shot and killed two days later by nightclub owner Jack Ruby while in police custody, live on national television.', 'The 1964 Warren Commission concluded Oswald acted alone, but a 1979 U.S. House committee found a probable conspiracy without identifying its scope, and the case remains debated.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - assassination; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Assassination of John F. Kennedy'' confirmed shot 22 Nov 1963 Dealey Plaza, Dallas TX; Lee Harvey Oswald identified as the shooter; Kennedy pronounced dead at Parkland Hospital.'),
  ('e4e0c036-3508-5732-bfda-b5518fca27ed', 'E026', 'event', 'the-wright-brothers-first-flight', 'The Wright Brothers'' First Flight', 'Orville and Wilbur Wright achieved the first powered, controlled flight of a heavier-than-air aircraft at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.', 'draft', 'Where did two brothers fly the first successful airplane?', 36.0158, -75.6683, 'Kill Devil Hills, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, USA', 'This flight happened at Kill Devil Hills, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina. Orville and Wilbur Wright flew the first powered airplane here.', 'First successful powered flight of the Wright Flyer', 'When did two brothers make the first successful powered flight?', 'December 17, 1903', 1903, 'day', 'This flight happened on December 17, 1903. The plane flew only about 120 feet, a shorter distance than the wings of a modern jumbo jet.', 'Span of the single-day set of flights (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Progressive Era (early 20th Century)', 'The breakthrough spurred rapid advances in aviation technology, and within just over a decade airplanes were being used for reconnaissance and combat in World War I.', 'The historic first flight covered only about 120 feet, a shorter distance than the wingspan of a modern Boeing 747.', 'Orville and Wilbur Wright ran a bicycle repair and sales shop in Dayton, Ohio, whose profits helped fund their aeronautical experiments.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''Wright Flyer'' confirmed first flight 17 Dec 1903, Kill Devil Hills NC; Orville piloted the first 12-second flight; four flights made that day.'),
  ('78e17164-350b-55e8-ac6b-fea08dc108fd', 'E027', 'event', 'gutenberg-s-printing-of-the-bible', 'Gutenberg''s Printing of the Bible', 'Johannes Gutenberg''s invention of the movable-type printing press culminated in the printing of the Gutenberg Bible, revolutionizing the spread of knowledge.', 'draft', 'Where was this famous book finished using a brand-new printing machine?', 49.9929, 8.2473, 'Mainz, Holy Roman Empire (modern Germany)', 'This book was finished in Mainz, in what is now Germany. Johannes Gutenberg printed it using his new movable-type press, which changed how books were made forever.', 'The Gutenberg Bible is completed', 'When was this famous book finished using a brand-new printing method?', 'c. 1455', 1455, 'year', 'This book was finished around 1455. Only about 49 copies are known to still exist today, out of the roughly 160 to 180 first printed.', 'Span of the printing project (c.1450-1455) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Renaissance / Early Modern Europe', 'Printing technology spread rapidly across Europe after Gutenberg''s Bible, and within about 50 years printing presses were operating in over 200 European cities.', 'An estimated 160-180 copies of the Gutenberg Bible were originally printed; only about 49 are known to survive today, with roughly half of those complete.', 'Gutenberg lost control of his printing workshop to his financial backer, Johann Fust, in a lawsuit before the Bible project was even finished.', 'Holy Roman Empire (modern Germany)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Gutenberg Bible'' confirmed printing began c.1450, completed by 1455, Mainz Germany; considered the first major book printed with movable metal type in the West.'),
  ('df4dcf34-5e2b-59ea-9722-a56e95ba8d7d', 'E028', 'event', 'discovery-of-penicillin', 'Discovery of Penicillin', 'Alexander Fleming''s accidental discovery of penicillin''s antibacterial properties launched the age of antibiotics, saving countless lives.', 'draft', 'Where did a scientist notice mold killing germs in a lab dish?', 51.5171, -0.173, 'St. Mary''s Hospital, London, England', 'This happened at St. Mary''s Hospital, in London, England. Alexander Fleming noticed mold killing bacteria here, a discovery that led to the first antibiotic medicine.', 'Fleming notices mold killing bacteria in a petri dish', 'When did a scientist notice mold killing germs in his lab?', 'September 1928', 1928, 'month', 'This happened in September 1928. Fleming later shared the 1945 Nobel Prize for turning this discovery into a medicine that has saved countless lives.', 'Span of the discovery-to-publication events (1928-1929) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Interwar Period (20th Century)', 'Penicillin was developed into a mass-produced drug during World War II by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain''s Oxford team, saving countless soldiers from infection.', 'Fleming shared the 1945 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Howard Florey and Ernst Chain for the discovery and development of penicillin.', 'Fleming''s discovery came about by accident: he noticed that mold contaminating a petri dish he had left in his lab while on vacation had killed the surrounding bacteria.', 'United Kingdom (England)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Penicillin''/''Alexander Fleming'' confirmed discovery Sept 1928 at St. Mary''s Hospital London; published findings 1929; mass production developed in the 1940s by Florey and Chain.'),
  ('b4c156cf-c178-5d2f-a196-561726bfae99', 'E029', 'event', 'edward-jenner-s-smallpox-vaccination', 'Edward Jenner''s Smallpox Vaccination', 'Edward Jenner tested the first vaccine by inoculating a boy with cowpox material, pioneering the science of immunization against smallpox.', 'draft', 'Where did a doctor test the world''s very first vaccine on a boy?', 51.69, -2.46, 'Berkeley, Gloucestershire, England', 'This happened in Berkeley, Gloucestershire, England. Edward Jenner gave a boy cowpox material here to test if it could protect him from smallpox.', 'Jenner inoculates James Phipps with cowpox material', 'When did a doctor test the world''s very first vaccine on a boy?', 'May 14, 1796', 1796, 'day', 'This happened on May 14, 1796. Jenner named his new method ''vaccine,'' from the Latin word for cow, since he used cowpox instead of smallpox itself.', 'Span of the trial-to-publication events (1796-1798) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Georgian Era (Age of Enlightenment)', 'Jenner''s method of vaccination gradually replaced the riskier practice of variolation, and his approach eventually enabled the global eradication of smallpox, declared by the WHO in 1980.', 'Jenner coined the term ''vaccine'' from ''vaccinae,'' the Latin word for cow, since he used cowpox material rather than smallpox itself.', 'His first test subject, eight-year-old James Phipps, was the son of Jenner''s gardener; Jenner later deliberately exposed the boy to smallpox to confirm his immunity had taken hold.', 'Kingdom of Great Britain', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Edward Jenner'' confirmed cowpox inoculation of James Phipps 14 May 1796, Berkeley England; published findings 1798; term ''vaccine'' derives from Latin ''vacca'' (cow).'),
  ('d70c0c00-031b-5063-9627-89b38602dca3', 'E030', 'event', 'sealing-of-the-magna-carta', 'Sealing of the Magna Carta', 'King John of England sealed the Magna Carta under pressure from rebel barons, establishing the principle that the monarch is subject to the law.', 'draft', 'Where did a king agree to follow new rules after pressure from angry nobles?', 51.4436, -0.5697, 'Runnymede, England', 'This happened at Runnymede, in England. King John sealed a famous document here that said even the king had to obey the law.', 'King John seals the Magna Carta', 'When did a king agree to follow new rules after pressure from angry nobles?', 'June 15, 1215', 1215, 'day', 'This happened on June 15, 1215. Only a few of its original rules are still law in England today, but its big idea still matters.', 'Span of the negotiation-to-sealing events in 1215 (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Medieval England (Plantagenet Era)', 'Following King John''s death in 1216, the regency government of his young son Henry III reissued a revised Magna Carta to win back rebel support, and it was reissued again in 1225 and 1297.', 'Only three of the original 1215 clauses remain in force under English law today, one of which protects the ancient liberties of the City of London.', 'Magna Carta later influenced foundational legal documents including the U.S. Constitution and Bill of Rights.', 'Kingdom of England', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Magna Carta'' confirmed sealed 15 June 1215 at Runnymede by King John under pressure from rebellious barons; reissued in later years.'),
  ('0b73e5d1-372a-58aa-8ff9-5ce38fb81944', 'E031', 'event', 'the-october-revolution', 'The October Revolution', 'The Bolsheviks, led by Vladimir Lenin, seized power in Russia, overthrowing the Provisional Government and establishing Soviet rule.', 'draft', 'Where did revolutionaries storm a palace and seize control of a government?', 59.9343, 30.3351, 'Petrograd (modern Saint Petersburg), Russia', 'This happened in Petrograd, now called Saint Petersburg, Russia. Bolshevik forces led by Lenin took over the Winter Palace here and seized control of the government.', 'Bolshevik forces seize the Winter Palace', 'When did revolutionaries storm a palace and seize control of a government?', 'November 7, 1917 (October 25, Old Style)', 1917, 'day', 'This happened on November 7, 1917. The takeover was almost bloodless, since most of the palace''s defenders surrendered instead of fighting.', 'Span of the uprising''s key events within 1917 (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Russian Revolution', 'Following the October Revolution, the Russian Civil War broke out between the Bolshevik Red Army and anti-communist White forces, lasting until 1922 and ending with Bolshevik control over most of the former Russian Empire.', 'The assault on the Winter Palace was almost bloodless: the roughly 3,000 cadets, officers, and members of the Women''s Battalion defending it largely surrendered to the far larger Bolshevik force rather than fighting.', 'Leon Trotsky, as chairman of the Petrograd Soviet, organized and directed the actual insurrection alongside Lenin, and the new Bolshevik government''s first decrees called for immediate peace and the redistribution of land to peasants.', 'Russian Republic (modern Russia)', 'Europe', 'Review - revolutionary uprising with subsequent civil war; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''October Revolution'' confirmed date 7 Nov 1917 (Gregorian) / 25 Oct 1917 (Julian/Old Style); Bolsheviks under Lenin stormed the Winter Palace in Petrograd, overthrowing the Provisional Government.'),
  ('d01bce78-e6e2-5598-ad37-b8b24d793592', 'E032', 'event', 'founding-of-the-united-nations', 'Founding of the United Nations', 'Representatives of 50 nations signed the UN Charter in San Francisco, establishing an international organization to maintain peace after WWII.', 'draft', 'Where did nations sign a charter to build a new peace organization?', 37.7749, -122.4194, 'San Francisco, California, USA', 'This happened in San Francisco, California. Representatives from 50 nations signed a charter here, creating a new organization to help keep the world at peace after World War II.', 'The UN Charter is signed', 'When did nations sign a charter to create a new organization for world peace?', 'June 26, 1945', 1945, 'day', 'This happened on June 26, 1945. Poland was one of the founding countries but could not attend, so it signed the charter later that year.', 'Span of the conference-to-ratification events in 1945 (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Post-World War II Era', 'Following the signing of the UN Charter, the organization formally came into existence on October 24, 1945, once it had been ratified by the five permanent Security Council members and a majority of the other signatory nations.', 'Poland, though recognized as one of the 51 original founding member states, was absent from the San Francisco Conference due to disputes over its postwar government and only signed the Charter later, on October 15, 1945.', 'The Charter established five permanent members of the Security Council — the United States, the Soviet Union, the United Kingdom, France, and China — each holding sole veto power over Council resolutions, a structure that still shapes UN decision-making today.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''United Nations'' confirmed Charter signed 26 June 1945 San Francisco by 50 founding nations; UN officially came into existence 24 Oct 1945 after ratification.'),
  ('7068498e-ec8b-50ed-baf8-ca8fec5ff776', 'E033', 'event', 'the-cuban-missile-crisis', 'The Cuban Missile Crisis', 'A 13-day Cold War confrontation between the U.S. and Soviet Union over Soviet nuclear missiles in Cuba, brought the world to the brink of nuclear war.', 'draft', 'Where were nuclear missiles found that pushed two superpowers to the edge of war?', 21.5218, -77.7812, 'Cuba (crisis centered on Washington, D.C. and Moscow diplomacy)', 'This crisis was centered on Cuba, where spy planes spotted Soviet nuclear missiles. The discovery pushed the United States and the Soviet Union to the edge of nuclear war.', 'U.S. discovers Soviet missile sites via spy plane photos', 'When did spy planes first spot nuclear missiles that scared the whole world?', 'October 14, 1962', 1962, 'day', 'The missiles were discovered on October 14, 1962. The crisis was later settled partly through a secret deal that stayed hidden for decades.', 'Span of the 13-day crisis (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Cold War Era', 'Following the resolution of the crisis, the United States and Soviet Union established the Moscow–Washington hotline in 1963 to allow direct, rapid communication between the two governments and reduce the risk of future nuclear miscalculation.', 'The crisis was resolved partly through a secret side agreement in which the United States pledged to quietly withdraw its Jupiter nuclear missiles from Turkey in exchange for the Soviet withdrawal from Cuba, a deal not publicly disclosed for decades.', 'The confrontation directly led to the 1963 Partial Nuclear Test Ban Treaty, the first arms-control agreement between the two Cold War superpowers, signed by the United States, Soviet Union, and United Kingdom.', 'Cuba (crisis diplomacy centered on the United States and Soviet Union)', 'North America', 'Review - nuclear brinkmanship; present factually without alarmist framing', 'Wikipedia ''Cuban Missile Crisis'' confirmed U-2 discovery 14 Oct 1962; naval quarantine announced 22 Oct 1962; resolution 28 Oct 1962; lasted 13 days.'),
  ('dce6a924-8aff-52cb-bd91-6d0e043e503b', 'E034', 'event', 'assassination-of-martin-luther-king-jr', 'Assassination of Martin Luther King Jr.', 'Civil rights leader Martin Luther King Jr. was fatally shot on a motel balcony in Memphis, Tennessee, sparking nationwide unrest and mourning.', 'draft', 'Where was this civil rights leader shot while standing on a balcony?', 35.13, -90.0572, 'Lorraine Motel, Memphis, Tennessee, USA', 'This happened at the Lorraine Motel, in Memphis, Tennessee. Martin Luther King Jr. was shot here while standing on the motel balcony.', 'King is shot on the motel balcony', 'When was this civil rights leader shot while standing on a balcony?', 'April 4, 1968', 1968, 'day', 'He was shot on April 4, 1968. His death led to unrest and riots in more than 100 cities across the country in the days that followed.', 'Span of the single-day event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Civil Rights Movement', 'Following King''s assassination, President Lyndon B. Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act of 1968 (the Fair Housing Act) into law just one week later, on April 11, 1968, banning discrimination in the sale and rental of housing.', 'King''s assassination triggered riots and unrest in more than 100 American cities over the following days, among the most widespread waves of civil disturbance in U.S. history.', 'James Earl Ray pleaded guilty to the assassination in March 1969 and was sentenced to 99 years in prison, but recanted his confession just three days later and spent the rest of his life unsuccessfully seeking a trial before dying in prison in 1998.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - assassination; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Assassination of Martin Luther King Jr.'' confirmed shot 4 Apr 1968 at the Lorraine Motel, Memphis TN; shooter identified as James Earl Ray; King pronounced dead at St. Joseph''s Hospital.'),
  ('6ed2f832-c2b2-56b2-80c9-2686a8c59889', 'E035', 'event', 'the-march-on-washington', 'The March on Washington', 'Over 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial in a landmark civil rights demonstration where Dr. King delivered his ''I Have a Dream'' speech.', 'draft', 'Where did a huge crowd gather to hear a famous speech about equality?', 38.8893, -77.0502, 'Lincoln Memorial, Washington, D.C., USA', 'This happened at the Lincoln Memorial, in Washington, D.C. Over 250,000 people gathered here to hear Dr. King give his ''I Have a Dream'' speech.', 'Dr. King delivers the ''I Have a Dream'' speech', 'When did a huge crowd gather to hear a famous speech about equality?', 'August 28, 1963', 1963, 'day', 'This happened on August 28, 1963. Organizers planned the huge event for about 250,000 people in less than two months.', 'Span of the single-day march (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Civil Rights Movement', 'Following the March on Washington, Congress passed the Civil Rights Act of 1964, outlawing discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, or national origin.', 'The march was chiefly organized by labor leader A. Philip Randolph and civil rights strategist Bayard Rustin, who coordinated logistics for the roughly 250,000 attendees in under two months.', 'The event''s official name was the ''March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom,'' reflecting its dual focus on both civil rights legislation and economic demands, such as a higher minimum wage and an end to employment discrimination.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom'' confirmed date 28 Aug 1963; over 250,000 attendees; King''s ''I Have a Dream'' speech delivered at the Lincoln Memorial.'),
  ('73bed912-be47-5e92-8782-8f82f0ea3c15', 'E036', 'event', 'signing-of-the-u-s-constitution', 'Signing of the U.S. Constitution', 'Delegates to the Constitutional Convention signed the U.S. Constitution in Philadelphia, establishing the framework of American government.', 'draft', 'Where did delegates sign a document that set up a brand-new government?', 39.9489, -75.15, 'Independence Hall, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, USA', 'This happened at Independence Hall, in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. Delegates signed a new plan for government here that still guides the United States today.', 'Delegates sign the Constitution', 'When did delegates sign a document that set up a brand-new government?', 'September 17, 1787', 1787, 'day', 'This happened on September 17, 1787. Only 39 of the 55 delegates who attended the meeting actually signed the final document.', 'Span of the Constitutional Convention (May-Sept 1787) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Founding Era (Early American Republic)', 'Following the Constitution''s signing, it was sent to the states for ratification, and it formally took effect after New Hampshire became the ninth state to ratify it on June 21, 1788.', 'Of the 55 delegates who attended the Constitutional Convention, only 39 signed the final document; several, including George Mason and Edmund Randolph, refused because it lacked a bill of rights.', 'Rhode Island, which had boycotted the Constitutional Convention entirely, was the last of the original thirteen states to ratify the Constitution, doing so in 1790 only after facing a threatened trade embargo from its neighbors.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''United States Constitution'' confirmed Convention ran 25 May-17 Sept 1787 in Philadelphia; signed 17 Sept 1787; ratified by required states by 1788.'),
  ('5bc710f7-4960-5def-a8d8-81e12bbe5a7a', 'E037', 'event', 'the-louisiana-purchase', 'The Louisiana Purchase', 'The United States purchased vast western territory from France, roughly doubling the nation''s size at a cost of about $15 million.', 'draft', 'Where did the United States agree to buy a huge stretch of new land?', 29.9511, -90.0715, 'New Orleans, Louisiana, USA (treaty signed in Paris, France)', 'This land deal centered on New Orleans, Louisiana, though the treaty itself was signed in Paris, France. The United States bought this huge territory from France and nearly doubled in size.', 'The Louisiana Purchase treaty is signed', 'When did the United States agree to buy a huge stretch of land from France?', 'April 30, 1803', 1803, 'day', 'This treaty was signed on April 30, 1803. The land cost less than three cents an acre and made the country almost twice as big overnight.', 'Span of the negotiation-to-ratification events (1803) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Early American Republic', 'Following the Louisiana Purchase, President Jefferson commissioned the Lewis and Clark Expedition (1804–1806) to explore, map, and document the vast newly acquired territory.', 'At roughly $15 million for about 828,000 square miles, the purchase cost the United States less than three cents an acre and nearly doubled the size of the country overnight.', 'The formal transfer ceremony took place at the Cabildo in New Orleans on December 20, 1803, when France handed over sovereignty of the territory to the United States, having only received it back from Spain weeks earlier.', 'United States (territory acquired from France)', 'North America', 'Review - territory transfer displaced Native American nations already living there; note factually without omission', 'Wikipedia ''Louisiana Purchase'' confirmed treaty signed 30 Apr 1803 Paris; ~828,000 sq mi for ~$15 million; formally transferred to the US Dec 1803; land was already inhabited by numerous Native American nations.'),
  ('5ab225e3-b372-5c34-b7eb-4ac1f3127536', 'E038', 'event', 'the-california-gold-rush', 'The California Gold Rush', 'The discovery of gold at Sutter''s Mill triggered a massive migration of prospectors to California, transforming the American West.', 'draft', 'Where was gold discovered that started a huge rush of people heading west?', 38.8016, -120.8894, 'Sutter''s Mill, Coloma, California, USA', 'Gold was found at Sutter''s Mill, in Coloma, California. This discovery sent huge numbers of people west, all hoping to strike it rich.', 'Gold is discovered at Sutter''s Mill', 'When was gold discovered that started a huge rush of people heading west?', 'January 24, 1848', 1848, 'day', 'Gold was found on January 24, 1848. California''s population then exploded from about 15,000 people in 1848 to over 300,000 by 1855.', 'Span of the rush''s peak years (1848-1855) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Westward Expansion', 'Following the surge of population and wealth brought by the Gold Rush, California was admitted to the Union as the 31st state on September 9, 1850, unusually skipping the normal territorial stage.', 'California''s non-Native population exploded from roughly 15,000 in 1848 to over 300,000 by 1855, as prospectors known as ''49ers'' poured in from across the United States and around the world.', 'The rush had a devastating impact on California''s Native American population, which was decimated by violence, disease, and displacement as settlers and miners flooded into their traditional lands.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - Gold Rush caused significant displacement and violence against Native Californians; note factually without omission', 'Wikipedia ''California Gold Rush'' confirmed gold discovered 24 Jan 1848 by James W. Marshall at Sutter''s Mill, Coloma CA; triggered mass migration 1848-1855; caused severe harm to Native Californian populations through displacement, disease, and violence.'),
  ('791375d7-8ffe-5d9b-b948-1b5a6238ad22', 'E039', 'event', 'the-wall-street-crash-of-1929', 'The Wall Street Crash of 1929', 'A catastrophic stock market crash on Wall Street wiped out fortunes and triggered the Great Depression, the worst economic downturn of the 20th century.', 'draft', 'Where did this huge stock market crash happen?', 40.7069, -74.0113, 'Wall Street, New York City, USA', 'This crash happened on Wall Street, in New York City. Stock prices fell so fast that fortunes were wiped out, and it helped start the Great Depression.', 'Stock prices collapse on ''Black Tuesday''', 'When did stock prices collapse on a day people called ''Black Tuesday''?', 'October 29, 1929', 1929, 'day', 'Prices crashed on October 29, 1929, a day known as ''Black Tuesday.'' The panic had actually started five days earlier, on ''Black Thursday.''', 'Span of the crash week (Oct 24-29, 1929) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Roaring Twenties / Great Depression', 'Following the crash, the U.S. economy spiraled into the Great Depression, with unemployment eventually reaching roughly 25 percent by 1933 before New Deal recovery programs began.', 'The crash unfolded over several days, beginning with panic selling on ''Black Thursday,'' October 24, before the more severe collapse on ''Black Tuesday,'' October 29, when the market lost billions of dollars in value in a single session.', 'In response to the crash and the Depression that followed, Congress created the Securities and Exchange Commission in 1934 to regulate stock trading and restore public confidence in financial markets.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - economic catastrophe leading to widespread hardship; present factually', 'Wikipedia ''Wall Street Crash of 1929'' confirmed ''Black Thursday'' 24 Oct 1929, ''Black Tuesday'' 29 Oct 1929 the most severe crash day; triggered the Great Depression.'),
  ('45efee2b-38ad-54bd-9f6e-9faf2ed18353', 'E040', 'event', 'the-hindenburg-disaster', 'The Hindenburg Disaster', 'The German passenger airship Hindenburg caught fire and was destroyed while attempting to dock in New Jersey, killing 36 people.', 'draft', 'Where did this giant airship catch fire while landing?', 40.0335, -74.3254, 'Naval Air Station Lakehurst, New Jersey, USA', 'The airship caught fire at Naval Air Station Lakehurst, in New Jersey. It was a German passenger airship called the Hindenburg, and 36 people died in the fire.', 'The Hindenburg catches fire while landing', 'When did this famous airship catch fire as it tried to land?', 'May 6, 1937', 1937, 'day', 'It happened on May 6, 1937. Of the 97 people aboard, most survived by jumping to safety as the airship burned.', 'Span of the single-day disaster (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Interwar Period', 'Following the Hindenburg disaster, public confidence in hydrogen-filled passenger airships collapsed, effectively ending the era of transatlantic airship travel as airlines shifted toward faster, safer fixed-wing aircraft.', 'Of the 97 people aboard, 35 died along with one member of the ground crew, but 62 passengers and crew survived, many by jumping from the burning airship as it neared its mooring mast.', 'Radio announcer Herbert Morrison''s emotional live broadcast of the disaster, including his famous cry of ''Oh, the humanity!'', was captured on recording and became one of the most iconic pieces of American radio journalism.', 'United States (German-built LZ 129 Hindenburg)', 'North America', 'Review - disaster with fatalities; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Hindenburg disaster'' confirmed date 6 May 1937, Lakehurst NJ; airship caught fire while attempting to dock; 36 of 97 people aboard died, plus one ground crew member.'),
  ('1a6fb784-8766-5935-b73d-a6f1e235ceda', 'E041', 'event', 'the-atomic-bombing-of-hiroshima', 'The Atomic Bombing of Hiroshima', 'The United States dropped the first atomic bomb used in warfare on the Japanese city of Hiroshima, killing tens of thousands instantly and leading toward the end of World War II.', 'draft', 'Where did the first atomic bomb ever used in war fall?', 34.3853, 132.4553, 'Hiroshima, Japan', 'The bomb fell on Hiroshima, Japan. It killed tens of thousands of people right away and helped bring World War II closer to its end.', 'The atomic bomb ''Little Boy'' is dropped on Hiroshima', 'When was the first atomic bomb used in warfare dropped?', 'August 6, 1945', 1945, 'day', 'It was dropped on August 6, 1945. The bomb, nicknamed ''Little Boy,'' was dropped from a plane called the Enola Gay.', 'Span of the single-day event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'World War II', 'Following the bombing of Hiroshima, the United States dropped a second atomic bomb on Nagasaki on August 9, 1945, and Japan announced its surrender on August 15, 1945, bringing World War II to an end.', 'The bomb, nicknamed ''Little Boy,'' used about 64 kilograms of uranium-235 and was dropped from the American B-29 bomber Enola Gay, named by its pilot after his mother.', 'Modern estimates suggest that between 90,000 and 166,000 people died in Hiroshima by the end of 1945 from the blast, burns, and radiation, with the toll continuing to climb in later years from radiation-related illness.', 'Japan', 'Asia', 'Review - mass-casualty wartime bombing; present factually, respectfully, without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki'' confirmed Hiroshima bombed 6 Aug 1945 by the B-29 Enola Gay; bomb named ''Little Boy''; estimated 70,000-135,000 deaths by end of 1945.'),
  ('94e7b960-5bf4-5c78-b821-e825df7da4d1', 'E042', 'event', 'the-treaty-of-versailles', 'The Treaty of Versailles', 'The peace treaty that officially ended World War I, imposing harsh terms on Germany and reshaping the map of Europe.', 'draft', 'Where was the treaty signed that officially ended World War I?', 48.8049, 2.1204, 'Palace of Versailles, France', 'The treaty was signed at the Palace of Versailles, in France. It set harsh rules for Germany and changed the map of Europe.', 'The Treaty of Versailles is signed', 'When was the treaty signed that officially ended World War I?', 'June 28, 1919', 1919, 'day', 'The Treaty of Versailles was signed on June 28, 1919. It took away land from Germany and shrank its army to just 100,000 men.', 'Span of the peace conference (Jan-June 1919) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Aftermath of World War I', 'Following the Treaty of Versailles, the harsh reparations and ''war guilt'' clause imposed on Germany contributed to the economic instability and political resentment that helped fuel the rise of Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party in the following decades.', 'The treaty stripped Germany of roughly 65,000 square kilometers of territory and about 7 million people, and capped its army at 100,000 men while banning it from having submarines, tanks, or an air force.', 'Although President Woodrow Wilson helped negotiate the treaty and championed the League of Nations it created, the U.S. Senate refused to ratify it, and the United States never joined the League it had helped establish.', 'France', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Treaty of Versailles'' confirmed signed 28 June 1919 at the Palace of Versailles; Paris Peace Conference began Jan 1919; officially ended WWI.'),
  ('292de9da-da4b-5cd6-b6a8-3590ef4f54f6', 'E043', 'event', 'the-1906-san-francisco-earthquake', 'The 1906 San Francisco Earthquake', 'A massive earthquake and the resulting fires devastated San Francisco, killing thousands and destroying much of the city.', 'draft', 'Where did this earthquake and the fires after it destroy much of a city?', 37.7749, -122.4194, 'San Francisco, California, USA', 'The earthquake struck San Francisco, California. It was so powerful that it cracked the ground for almost 300 miles along the San Andreas Fault.', 'The earthquake strikes San Francisco', 'When did the ground shake so hard it destroyed much of this city?', 'April 18, 1906', 1906, 'day', 'The earthquake hit on April 18, 1906. It broke almost 300 miles of the San Andreas Fault, one of the longest cracks ever recorded there.', 'Span of the earthquake-and-fire event (days) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Progressive Era', 'Following the earthquake and fires, San Francisco undertook a massive rebuilding effort, and the disaster spurred major advances in seismology, including research by Andrew Lawson that helped establish the elastic rebound theory of earthquakes.', 'The earthquake, estimated at magnitude 7.9, ruptured roughly 296 miles of the San Andreas Fault, one of the longest fault ruptures ever recorded in California.', 'More than 80 percent of San Francisco was destroyed by the combined earthquake and resulting fires, which burned for four days and leveled an estimated 25,000 buildings across about 490 city blocks.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - natural disaster with significant loss of life; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''1906 San Francisco earthquake'' confirmed date 18 Apr 1906, magnitude ~7.9; fires burned for days afterward; estimated 3,000+ deaths; about 80% of the city destroyed.'),
  ('5af4e7a6-d9ae-5e0d-936b-b56971d468b1', 'E044', 'event', 'the-eruption-of-mount-st-helens', 'The Eruption of Mount St. Helens', 'A powerful volcanic eruption in Washington State caused the largest landslide in recorded history and killed 57 people.', 'draft', 'Where did this volcano erupt and cause a giant landslide?', 46.1912, -122.1944, 'Mount St. Helens, Washington State, USA', 'The eruption happened at Mount St. Helens, in Washington State. The blast was so strong it blew off part of the mountain''s top and killed 57 people.', 'Mount St. Helens erupts catastrophically', 'When did this volcano erupt with enough force to reshape the mountain?', 'May 18, 1980', 1980, 'day', 'It erupted on May 18, 1980. The blast knocked about 1,300 feet off the mountain''s summit and caused the largest landslide ever recorded.', 'Span of the single-day eruption event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Late 20th Century', 'Following the 1980 eruption, the U.S. government designated the area the Mount St. Helens National Volcanic Monument in 1982, and the volcano has continued to show periodic activity, including a dome-building eruptive phase from 2004 to 2008.', 'The eruption removed about 1,300 feet from the mountain''s summit and triggered the largest landslide in recorded history, a debris avalanche covering roughly 23 square miles.', 'Among the 57 people killed was volcanologist David A. Johnston, who was monitoring the volcano from an observation post about 6 miles away and radioed ''Vancouver! Vancouver! This is it!'' moments before he was killed.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - natural disaster with fatalities; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Mount St. Helens'' confirmed eruption date 18 May 1980; triggered by a 5.1 magnitude earthquake; caused the largest debris avalanche in recorded history; killed 57 people.'),
  ('7c9fa9b9-47bf-5cf8-b507-12b0ff9fe25c', 'E045', 'event', 'the-2004-indian-ocean-tsunami', 'The 2004 Indian Ocean Tsunami', 'A massive undersea earthquake triggered a devastating tsunami across the Indian Ocean, killing over 225,000 people in 14 countries.', 'draft', 'Where did the earthquake happen that caused this deadly tsunami?', 3.316, 95.854, 'Off the coast of Sumatra, Indonesia', 'The earthquake struck under the ocean near Sumatra, Indonesia. It sent huge waves across the Indian Ocean, reaching 14 countries.', 'The undersea earthquake triggers the tsunami', 'When did the earthquake strike that triggered this giant tsunami?', 'December 26, 2004', 2004, 'day', 'It happened on December 26, 2004. The earthquake shook for at least ten minutes, the longest ever recorded.', 'Span of the single-day disaster''s wave propagation (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', '21st Century', 'Following the massive loss of life, the international community established the Indian Ocean Tsunami Warning and Mitigation System in 2006 to detect future undersea earthquakes and issue faster alerts to coastal nations.', 'The undersea earthquake that triggered the tsunami, estimated at magnitude 9.1 to 9.3, ruptured a fault roughly 1,200 to 1,300 kilometers long and lasted at least ten minutes, the longest duration of faulting ever recorded.', 'The disaster was so powerful that it measurably shortened the length of Earth''s day by a few microseconds and caused detectable ground vibrations worldwide, in addition to killing an estimated 227,000 people across 14 countries.', 'Indonesia (Sumatra)', 'Asia', 'Review - natural disaster with massive loss of life; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami'' confirmed date 26 Dec 2004; magnitude 9.1-9.3 earthquake off Sumatra; over 225,000 deaths across 14 countries; one of the deadliest natural disasters in recorded history.'),
  ('b054fb4d-ff02-51f5-977c-1ca5c362896b', 'E046', 'event', 'hurricane-katrina', 'Hurricane Katrina', 'One of the deadliest and costliest hurricanes in U.S. history struck the Gulf Coast, devastating New Orleans after levee failures caused catastrophic flooding.', 'draft', 'Where did this powerful hurricane cause the worst flooding?', 29.9511, -90.0715, 'New Orleans, Louisiana, USA', 'The worst flooding hit New Orleans, Louisiana. The storm made the city''s levees fail in more than 50 places, and water poured in.', 'Hurricane Katrina makes landfall on the Gulf Coast', 'When did this powerful hurricane hit the Gulf Coast?', 'August 29, 2005', 2005, 'day', 'Hurricane Katrina made landfall on August 29, 2005. Later studies found the flooding was mostly caused by poorly built levees, not just the storm itself.', 'Span of the storm''s landfall-to-flooding timeline (days) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', '21st Century', 'Following Hurricane Katrina, the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers undertook a multibillion-dollar rebuilding of New Orleans'' flood-protection system, completing a new roughly $14.5 billion levee and floodwall network by 2011.', 'Later engineering investigations found that the catastrophic flooding resulted primarily from design and construction flaws in the federally built levee system, which failed in more than 50 places, rather than simply from the storm''s force alone.', 'Katrina caused an estimated $125 billion in damage, making it the costliest tropical cyclone in U.S. history at the time, and displaced hundreds of thousands of Gulf Coast residents, many permanently.', 'United States', 'North America', 'Review - natural disaster with significant loss of life and displacement; present factually without graphic detail', 'Wikipedia ''Hurricane Katrina'' confirmed landfall 29 Aug 2005 near Buras-Triumph, Louisiana; levee failures flooded ~80% of New Orleans; at least 1,392 deaths; among costliest hurricanes in U.S. history.'),
  ('00ab02b9-6064-5497-b406-2ce06ad4f64f', 'E047', 'event', 'discovery-of-tutankhamun-s-tomb', 'Discovery of Tutankhamun''s Tomb', 'British archaeologist Howard Carter discovered the nearly intact tomb of the young pharaoh Tutankhamun, one of the greatest archaeological finds ever made.', 'draft', 'Where was this famous ancient tomb discovered?', 25.7404, 32.6014, 'Valley of the Kings, Egypt', 'The tomb was found in the Valley of the Kings, in Egypt. British archaeologist Howard Carter uncovered the almost untouched burial place of the young pharaoh Tutankhamun.', 'Howard Carter discovers the tomb''s entrance steps', 'When were the first steps to this hidden tomb finally uncovered?', 'November 4, 1922', 1922, 'day', 'Howard Carter found the tomb''s entrance steps on November 4, 1922. His search had been paid for by Lord Carnarvon since 1914.', 'Span of the discovery-to-opening events (Nov 1922) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Golden Age of Egyptology', 'Following the tomb''s discovery, Carter and his team spent nearly a decade cataloguing and clearing its roughly 5,000 artifacts, finishing the full excavation only in 1932.', 'The excavation was financed by George Herbert, 5th Earl of Carnarvon, who had backed Carter''s search in the Valley of the Kings since 1914 and was present when the burial chamber''s sealed doorway was first breached.', 'The tomb had already been entered by robbers at least twice in antiquity shortly after the burial, but ancient officials resealed it and later construction debris covered its entrance, which helped it survive largely intact into the 20th century.', 'Kingdom of Egypt (modern Egypt)', 'Africa', null, 'Wikipedia ''Tomb of Tutankhamun'' confirmed Howard Carter discovered entrance steps 4 Nov 1922; funded by Lord Carnarvon; burial chamber opened Feb 1923; tomb found remarkably intact.'),
  ('c7b4a5e6-3b01-5f9d-9245-831e8ddb36a3', 'E048', 'event', 'the-first-crusade', 'The First Crusade', 'A major Western European military campaign to reclaim Jerusalem and the Holy Land from Muslim rule, launched at the call of Pope Urban II.', 'draft', 'Where did this army capture a city considered holy by many faiths?', 31.7683, 35.2137, 'Jerusalem (campaign launched from Clermont, France)', 'The army marched all the way from Clermont, France, to capture Jerusalem. Pope Urban II had called for the campaign to take the Holy Land from Muslim rule.', 'Crusader forces capture Jerusalem', 'When did this army finally capture the holy city it marched toward?', 'July 15, 1099', 1099, 'day', 'Crusader forces captured Jerusalem on July 15, 1099. Their journey began after the pope''s call for action back in 1095.', 'Span of the campaign (1095-1099) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Crusades / High Middle Ages', 'Following the city''s capture, Godfrey of Bouillon was elected its first ruler on July 22, 1099, and after defeating a Fatimid relief army at the Battle of Ascalon that August, the conquered territory was consolidated into the new Kingdom of Jerusalem.', 'The Crusade was launched after Pope Urban II''s appeal at the Council of Clermont in November 1095, made partly in response to a request for military aid from Byzantine Emperor Alexios I Komnenos against the Seljuk Turks.', 'After the roughly five-week siege, Crusader forces killed a large number of the city''s Muslim and Jewish inhabitants in a massacre that contemporary chroniclers described in graphic, though likely exaggerated, terms.', 'Kingdom of Jerusalem (Levant, modern Israel/Palestine)', 'Asia', 'Review - religious war with significant historical violence and complex religious framing; present factually, neutrally, without endorsing either side', 'Wikipedia ''First Crusade'' confirmed Pope Urban II''s call to arms at the Council of Clermont Nov 1095; Jerusalem captured by Crusader forces 15 July 1099; resulted in the establishment of Crusader states.'),
  ('bc5ea7eb-8ecf-59d2-a661-8a49516ac1ba', 'E049', 'event', 'the-siege-battle-of-yorktown', 'The Siege/Battle of Yorktown', 'The decisive siege in which American and French forces trapped and forced the surrender of British General Cornwallis, effectively ending the American Revolutionary War.', 'draft', 'Where did British forces surrender to help end a long war?', 37.2388, -76.5083, 'Yorktown, Virginia, USA', 'The surrender happened at Yorktown, Virginia. American and French forces trapped the British army there while the French navy blocked their escape by sea.', 'British General Cornwallis surrenders', 'When did a British general surrender, all but ending this long war?', 'October 19, 1781', 1781, 'day', 'General Cornwallis surrendered on October 19, 1781. This American victory helped end the war and win the colonies their freedom.', 'Span of the siege (Sept 28-Oct 19, 1781) is under 15 years; tap-to-sequence order used.', 'American Revolutionary War', 'Although Cornwallis''s surrender effectively ended major fighting in North America, the war continued at sea and abroad for nearly two more years until the Treaty of Paris was signed on September 3, 1783, formally recognizing American independence.', 'The siege was a coordinated Franco-American operation in which the French navy under the Comte de Grasse blockaded the Chesapeake Bay, cutting off British evacuation or resupply by sea and trapping Cornwallis''s army.', 'Cornwallis did not personally attend the surrender ceremony, citing illness, and instead sent his second-in-command, General Charles O''Hara, to hand over his sword on his behalf.', 'United States (Virginia)', 'North America', null, 'Wikipedia ''Siege of Yorktown'' confirmed siege began 28 Sept 1781; Cornwallis surrendered 19 Oct 1781; combined American-French forces under Washington and Rochambeau; effectively ended major fighting in the American Revolution.'),
  ('7f2c4ad8-9c38-542e-94bb-59a5caec66de', 'E050', 'event', 'the-coronation-of-charlemagne', 'The Coronation of Charlemagne', 'Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne as Holy Roman Emperor in Rome, reviving the concept of a unified Christian empire in Western Europe.', 'draft', 'Where was a powerful king crowned emperor by the pope?', 41.9022, 12.4539, 'Old St. Peter''s Basilica, Rome, Italy', 'The crowning took place at Old St. Peter''s Basilica, in Rome, Italy. Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne as Holy Roman Emperor there.', 'Charlemagne is crowned Emperor by Pope Leo III', 'When was this king crowned emperor by the pope in Rome?', 'December 25, 800 CE', 800, 'day', 'Charlemagne was crowned on December 25, 800 CE. He later claimed to be surprised by the crowning, though many historians doubt that story.', 'Span of the coronation''s single-day event (<15 years); tap-to-sequence order used.', 'Carolingian Empire', 'Charlemagne was succeeded as emperor by his son Louis the Pious in 814, after the deaths of his other legitimate sons left Louis as his sole surviving heir.', 'According to his biographer Einhard, Charlemagne was displeased or surprised by Pope Leo III''s coronation, claiming he would not have entered St. Peter''s Basilica that day had he known the pope intended to crown him, though many modern historians doubt this account.', 'The coronation revived the title of Roman Emperor in Western Europe for the first time since the deposition of Romulus Augustulus in 476, a precedent that later evolved into the Holy Roman Empire.', 'Carolingian Empire (Francia; crowned in Rome, modern Italy)', 'Europe', null, 'Wikipedia ''Charlemagne'' confirmed crowned Holy Roman Emperor by Pope Leo III on 25 Dec 800 CE at Old St. Peter''s Basilica, Rome; marked the revival of the title of Emperor in Western Europe.'),
  ('ba59af19-d7a9-51f4-99bd-0be38a4f7a03', 'L001', 'location', 'constantinople-istanbul', 'Constantinople (Istanbul)', 'Nicknamed "Basileuousa" ("Queen of Cities") and simply "i Polis" ("the City") by its own residents — the likely source of the modern name Istanbul.', 'draft', 'Can you find this famous city that once ruled a great empire?', 41.0086, 28.9802, 'Constantinople (Istanbul)', 'This city is Constantinople, known today as Istanbul. Its own people called it simply ''the City,'' which may be where the name Istanbul comes from.', 'Roman Emperor Constantine I dedicates the city as "New Rome," the new capital of the Roman Empire.', 'When was this city named the new capital of a great empire?', '330 AD', 330, 'year', 'Roman Emperor Constantine I dedicated the city as ''New Rome'' in 330 AD, making it the new capital of the Roman Empire.', '4 items span 330-1453 AD (1123 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Byzantine Empire (Eastern Roman Empire)', 'Ottoman Empire — Sultan Mehmed II conquers the city on May 29, 1453, ending the Byzantine Empire.', 'Nicknamed "Basileuousa" ("Queen of Cities") and simply "i Polis" ("the City") by its own residents — the likely source of the modern name Istanbul.', 'The Hagia Sophia''s massive dome, completed in 537 AD, was the largest interior dome in the world for nearly a thousand years.', 'Turkey', 'Europe/Asia (Transcontinental)', 'Review - siege and conquest (Fourth Crusade sack, Ottoman conquest); present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('92db2938-32b4-5652-9ef3-2318e821a7b6', 'L002', 'location', 'babylon', 'Babylon', 'The city''s name (Akkadian "Bab-ilim") means "Gate of the Gods." Its legendary Hanging Gardens were counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, though their exact existence and location are still debated by historians.', 'draft', 'Where in the world was this legendary ancient city?', 32.5425, 44.4211, 'Babylon', 'This city is Babylon, located in what is now Iraq. Its name means ''Gate of the Gods,'' and it was famous for its legendary Hanging Gardens.', 'Nebuchadnezzar II ascends the throne, beginning Babylon''s golden age as capital of the Neo-Babylonian Empire.', 'When did a king take the throne who began this city''s golden age?', '605 BC', -605, 'year', 'Nebuchadnezzar II became king in 605 BC. Under his rule, Babylon became the capital of a powerful empire.', '4 items span 605-539 BC (66 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Neo-Babylonian Empire', 'Achaemenid Persian Empire — Cyrus the Great conquers Babylon in 539 BC.', 'The city''s name (Akkadian "Bab-ilim") means "Gate of the Gods." Its legendary Hanging Gardens were counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, though their exact existence and location are still debated by historians.', 'A full-scale reconstruction of the Ishtar Gate, built from bricks excavated at the site, stands today in Berlin''s Pergamon Museum.', 'Iraq', 'Asia', 'Review - conquest and destruction of Jerusalem''s Temple; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('6a73e44c-bad0-5e7f-a68c-39181b84a65d', 'L003', 'location', 'angkor-wat', 'Angkor Wat', 'Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world by land area. It was originally built as a Hindu temple dedicated to the god Vishnu before gradually becoming a Buddhist site from the late 13th century onward.', 'draft', 'Where is this giant ancient temple located?', 13.4125, 103.8667, 'Angkor Wat', 'This is Angkor Wat, in Cambodia. It is the largest religious monument in the world by land area, and it started out as a Hindu temple.', 'King Suryavarman II ascends the throne and begins construction of Angkor Wat as his state temple and eventual mausoleum.', 'When did the king who built this huge temple take the throne?', '1113 AD', 1113, 'year', 'King Suryavarman II became king in 1113 AD and began building Angkor Wat as his temple and future tomb.', '4 items span 1113-1431 AD (318 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Khmer Empire', 'Ayutthaya Kingdom — Siamese (Thai) forces sack Angkor in 1431, prompting the Khmer court to relocate south toward present-day Phnom Penh.', 'Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world by land area. It was originally built as a Hindu temple dedicated to the god Vishnu before gradually becoming a Buddhist site from the late 13th century onward.', 'Angkor Wat''s five central towers are aligned so that the sunrise on the spring equinox appears directly over the main tower when viewed from the western entrance causeway.', 'Cambodia', 'Asia', 'Review - military sacks of Angkor by Cham and Ayutthaya forces; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('5cc40268-a284-59af-9ad2-a9b06e3b7a20', 'L004', 'location', 'kyoto-heian-kyo', 'Kyoto (Heian-kyo)', 'Unlike Chang''an, the Chinese capital it was modeled after, Heian-kyo was built without defensive walls, relying instead on its inland, mountain-ringed location for security.', 'draft', 'Can you find this old capital city on the map?', 35.0116, 135.7681, 'Kyoto (Heian-kyo)', 'This city is Kyoto, once called Heian-kyo, in Japan. Unlike the Chinese city it copied, it was built with no walls, protected by mountains instead.', 'Emperor Kanmu establishes Heian-kyo (Kyoto) as the imperial capital of Japan.', 'When was this city made the imperial capital of Japan?', '794 AD', 794, 'year', 'Emperor Kanmu made Heian-kyo the capital in 794 AD. Today we know this city as Kyoto.', '4 items span 794-1868 AD (1074 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Heian Period', 'Meiji Restoration — the imperial capital moves to Edo (renamed Tokyo) in 1868, ending Kyoto''s 1,000+ year reign as Japan''s capital.', 'Unlike Chang''an, the Chinese capital it was modeled after, Heian-kyo was built without defensive walls, relying instead on its inland, mountain-ringed location for security.', 'Kyoto was removed from the list of potential atomic bomb targets in World War II specifically because of its immense historical and cultural significance.', 'Japan', 'Asia', 'Review - the Onin War''s devastation of Kyoto; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('110ad030-1b13-5395-ba89-b67854da88a8', 'L005', 'location', 'xi-an-terracotta-army', 'Xi''an (Terracotta Army)', 'Qin Shi Huang ordered construction of his terracotta army beginning when he was just 13 years old, eventually conscripting an estimated 700,000 workers over decades.', 'draft', 'Where is the city famous for its buried army of clay soldiers?', 34.385, 109.2731, 'Xi''an (Terracotta Army)', 'This city is Xi''an, in China, home of the Terracotta Army. Emperor Qin Shi Huang started building the clay army when he was only 13 years old.', 'Qin Shi Huang unifies China and establishes the Qin Dynasty, making the Xi''an region (Xianyang) his imperial capital.', 'When did the emperor who built this famous clay army come to power?', '221 BC', -221, 'year', 'Qin Shi Huang unified China and made this region his capital in 221 BC. His huge clay army took decades and hundreds of thousands of workers to finish.', '4 items span 221-206 BC (15 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Qin Dynasty', 'Han Dynasty — founded in 206 BC after Qin''s collapse; the Han later built their own capital, Chang''an, near the same site.', 'Qin Shi Huang ordered construction of his terracotta army beginning when he was just 13 years old, eventually conscripting an estimated 700,000 workers over decades.', 'No two Terracotta Army faces are identical — sculptors mixed and matched from roughly ten base facial molds, then hand-customized details on each figure.', 'China', 'Asia', 'Review - Qin military campaigns against the Xiongnu; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('83644752-df08-54bb-863f-95e7d2f0f2f0', 'L006', 'location', 'petra', 'Petra', 'Carved directly into rose-colored sandstone cliffs, Petra earned the nickname "the Rose City." Its Greek-derived name, Petra, simply means "rock."', 'draft', 'Where is this city carved into pink rock cliffs?', 30.3285, 35.4444, 'Petra', 'This is Petra, in Jordan. Its buildings are carved right into rose-colored cliffs, which is why people call it the ''Rose City.''', 'First historical record of the Nabataeans at Petra (Diodorus Siculus), already thriving as their capital.', 'When was this rock city first written about in history?', '312 BC', -312, 'year', 'The first known record of Petra comes from 312 BC. By then it was already a busy capital city for the Nabataean people.', '4 items span 312 BC-106 AD (418 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Nabataean Kingdom', 'Roman Empire — annexed peacefully in 106 AD and reorganized as the province of Arabia Petraea.', 'Carved directly into rose-colored sandstone cliffs, Petra earned the nickname "the Rose City." Its Greek-derived name, Petra, simply means "rock."', 'Despite its name ("The Treasury"), Al-Khazneh likely served as a royal tomb, possibly for the Nabataean King Aretas IV.', 'Jordan', 'Asia', 'Review - Roman siege of Petra; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('fe8f6bbb-6c54-5c53-bf5f-8078d40d4170', 'L007', 'location', 'jerusalem-old-city', 'Jerusalem (Old City)', 'Jerusalem is considered one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, with settlement near the Gihon Spring dating back roughly 5,000 years.', 'draft', 'Where is this ancient city that people have lived in for thousands of years?', 31.7767, 35.2345, 'Jerusalem (Old City)', 'This is the Old City of Jerusalem. People have lived here for about 5,000 years, making it one of the oldest cities in the world.', 'King David captures the city and establishes it as the capital of the united Kingdom of Israel.', 'When did a famous king capture this city and make it his capital?', '1000 BC', -1000, 'year', 'King David captured the city around 1000 BC and made it the capital of the united Kingdom of Israel.', '4 items span 1000-587 BC (413 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Kingdom of Israel and Judah (Iron Age)', 'Neo-Babylonian Empire — Nebuchadnezzar II conquers Jerusalem and destroys Solomon''s Temple in 586 BC.', 'Jerusalem is considered one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, with settlement near the Gihon Spring dating back roughly 5,000 years.', 'The Dome of the Rock, one of the site''s most iconic structures, was completed around 691-692 AD under the Umayyad Caliph Abd al-Malik.', 'Israel / Palestinian Territories (contested status)', 'Asia', 'Review - sieges and destruction of the Temple; present factually and respectfully of ongoing religious/political significance.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('0d0e7e75-6898-5027-adee-5d46b6cad65c', 'L008', 'location', 'rome', 'Rome', 'Rome earned the nickname "The Eternal City" (Urbs Aeterna), a term first used by the Roman poet Tibullus in the 1st century BC.', 'draft', 'Where was this legendary city said to have been founded?', 41.9028, 12.4964, 'Rome', 'This is Rome, in Italy. People later gave it the nickname ''The Eternal City.''', 'Traditional founding date of Rome, according to Roman legend, by Romulus.', 'When was this city said to have been founded, according to legend?', '753 BC', -753, 'year', 'Legend says Rome was founded in 753 BC by a man named Romulus. That story is part of why people still call it ''The Eternal City'' today.', '4 items span 753 BC-476 AD (1229 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Roman Empire', 'Fall of the Western Roman Empire — the Germanic chieftain Odoacer deposes the last emperor, Romulus Augustulus, on September 4, 476 AD.', 'Rome earned the nickname "The Eternal City" (Urbs Aeterna), a term first used by the Roman poet Tibullus in the 1st century BC.', 'The Colosseum could hold an estimated 50,000-80,000 spectators and featured a complex system of underground chambers and elevators to lift animals and gladiators up to the arena floor.', 'Italy', 'Europe', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('0749378f-92cb-5dac-a741-39a18cba22d8', 'L009', 'location', 'athens-acropolis', 'Athens (Acropolis)', 'Athens is named for Athena, goddess of wisdom, who — according to myth — won a contest against Poseidon for patronage of the city by gifting it an olive tree.', 'draft', 'Where is the hilltop famous for its ancient temples?', 37.9715, 23.7267, 'Athens (Acropolis)', 'This is Athens, in Greece, home of the Acropolis. The city is named after Athena, the goddess of wisdom.', 'Cleisthenes'' reforms establish democracy in Athens, launching its Classical "Golden Age."', 'When did new reforms bring democracy to this city?', '508 BC', -508, 'year', 'Cleisthenes brought democracy to Athens in 508 BC. This began the city''s Golden Age.', '4 items span 508-338 BC (170 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Classical Greece (Athenian Democracy / Golden Age of Athens)', 'Kingdom of Macedon — Philip II defeats the Athenian-led coalition at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BC, ending Athenian political independence.', 'Athens is named for Athena, goddess of wisdom, who — according to myth — won a contest against Poseidon for patronage of the city by gifting it an olive tree.', 'The Parthenon was severely damaged in 1687 when a Venetian bombardment ignited Ottoman gunpowder that was being stored inside it.', 'Greece', 'Europe', 'Review - Persian sack of the Acropolis and Macedonian military conquest; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('309def64-899d-5fd6-a834-db6226608f23', 'L010', 'location', 'pompeii', 'Pompeii', 'The layers of ash that killed Pompeii''s residents also perfectly preserved the city, including bread left in ovens, graffiti on walls, and hollow voids left by decomposed bodies.', 'draft', 'Where is the ancient city that a volcano buried in ash?', 40.75, 14.49, 'Pompeii', 'This is Pompeii, in Italy. Ash from Mount Vesuvius buried the city and kept it frozen in time, even preserving bread left in ovens.', 'Mount Vesuvius erupts, burying Pompeii under volcanic ash and pumice in a single catastrophic day.', 'When did a volcano erupt and bury this city in ash?', '79 AD', 79, 'year', 'Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 AD. The ash buried Pompeii so completely that it preserved the city for thousands of years.', '4 items span 740 BC-1748 AD (~2,488 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Roman Empire', 'None politically — the buried city lay forgotten for over 1,600 years until its rediscovery in 1748 helped spark the birth of modern archaeology.', 'The layers of ash that killed Pompeii''s residents also perfectly preserved the city, including bread left in ovens, graffiti on walls, and hollow voids left by decomposed bodies.', 'Archaeologist Giuseppe Fiorelli developed a technique in the 1860s of pouring plaster into the voids left by decomposed bodies, creating haunting casts of victims in their final moments.', 'Italy', 'Europe', 'Review - volcanic disaster with mass casualties; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('d1f28efe-3fd1-5b30-b4a6-ce5f9dc7d1bf', 'L011', 'location', 'stonehenge', 'Stonehenge', 'Stonehenge''s smaller "bluestones" were transported roughly 150 miles from the Preseli Hills in Wales — an extraordinary feat for Neolithic builders without wheeled vehicles.', 'draft', 'Can you find this ancient circle of giant stones on the map?', 51.1789, -1.8262, 'Stonehenge', 'This is Stonehenge, in England. Long ago, people dragged its smaller stones about 150 miles from Wales without any wheels to help them.', 'Approximate date the massive sarsen stones were erected in their current circular arrangement.', 'When were the huge stones raised into their circle shape?', '2500 BC', -2500, 'year', 'The giant stones at Stonehenge were set in their circle around 2500 BC. That is over 4,000 years ago, in the Stone Age.', '4 items span 3100 BC-1986 AD (~5,086 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Neolithic and Bronze Age Britain', 'None (predates recorded empires) — the surrounding region was later absorbed into Roman Britain following the invasion of 43 AD.', 'Stonehenge''s smaller "bluestones" were transported roughly 150 miles from the Preseli Hills in Wales — an extraordinary feat for Neolithic builders without wheeled vehicles.', 'Stonehenge is precisely aligned with the sunrise on the summer solstice and the sunset on the winter solstice, suggesting deep astronomical knowledge among its Neolithic builders.', 'England, United Kingdom', 'Europe', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('3c9e049c-768b-5efa-81c2-7f2feee43f58', 'L012', 'location', 'venice', 'Venice', 'Nicknamed "La Serenissima" (The Most Serene) and "Queen of the Adriatic," Venice was built atop 126 small islands connected by more than 400 bridges.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city built on more than 100 small islands?', 45.4408, 12.3155, 'Venice', 'This is Venice, Italy. People call it ''The Most Serene'' because it sits on 126 islands linked by over 400 bridges.', 'Traditional date of the election of Venice''s first Doge, marking the founding of the Republic of Venice.', 'When was this island city''s first leader chosen, marking the start of its republic?', '697 AD', 697, 'year', 'Venice picked its first Doge, or leader, in 697 AD. That moment marked the birth of the Republic of Venice.', '4 items span 697-1797 AD (1,100 yrs) > 15-yr threshold; place is the more differentiated field -> Match by place, not Order.', 'Republic of Venice', 'French First Republic — Napoleon Bonaparte conquers Venice on May 12, 1797, ending over 1,100 years of independence.', 'Nicknamed "La Serenissima" (The Most Serene) and "Queen of the Adriatic," Venice was built atop 126 small islands connected by more than 400 bridges.', 'Venice remains Europe''s largest car-free urban area — all transportation has historically moved entirely by boat or on foot.', 'Italy', 'Europe', 'Review - war and conquest (Fourth Crusade, War of Chioggia, Napoleonic conquest); present factually.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('75412f89-d8e0-56c0-8538-df9bb88ad4a4', 'L013', 'location', 'moscow-kremlin', 'Moscow (Kremlin)', 'During World War II, the Kremlin''s golden domes and towers were camouflaged with paint and fake canvas "roofs" to disguise the complex from German bomber pilots.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city with a famous fortress at its heart?', 55.752, 37.6175, 'Moscow (Kremlin)', 'This is Moscow, Russia, home to the Kremlin. During World War II, workers painted its golden domes and covered them with fake roofs to hide them from enemy bombers.', 'First chronicle mention of Moscow, traditionally credited as its founding by Prince Yuri Dolgoruky.', 'When was this city first written about in history books, marking what people call its founding?', '1147 AD', 1147, 'year', 'Moscow was first mentioned in old records in 1147 AD. People say Prince Yuri Dolgoruky founded the city.', '4 items span 1147-1712 AD (565 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Grand Duchy of Moscow / Tsardom of Russia', 'Russian Empire — Peter the Great moves the capital to his newly built city of St. Petersburg in 1712.', 'During World War II, the Kremlin''s golden domes and towers were camouflaged with paint and fake canvas "roofs" to disguise the complex from German bomber pilots.', 'The Kremlin''s Tsar Bell, cast in the 18th century, is the largest bell in the world — but it has never been rung; a large chunk broke off during a fire before it could ever be hung.', 'Russia', 'Europe', 'Review - Mongol siege and burning of Moscow; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('d02fa808-6e9e-50c7-98fc-831b66b4abe3', 'L014', 'location', 'giza-great-pyramid', 'Giza (Great Pyramid)', 'The Great Pyramid is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still largely intact, and it held the record for tallest man-made structure on Earth for over 3,800 years.', 'draft', 'Can you find this giant ancient tomb that is still standing today?', 29.9792, 31.1342, 'Giza (Great Pyramid)', 'This is the Great Pyramid at Giza, Egypt. It is the only one of the ancient world''s Seven Wonders still mostly whole, and it was the tallest building on Earth for over 3,800 years.', 'Approximate completion of the Great Pyramid, built as a tomb for Pharaoh Khufu.', 'When was this giant stone tomb finished being built?', '2560 BC', -2560, 'year', 'The Great Pyramid was finished around 2560 BC. Workers built it as a tomb for the Pharaoh Khufu.', '4 items span 2560 BC-450 BC (2,110 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Old Kingdom Egypt (Fourth Dynasty)', 'First Intermediate Period — centralized pharaonic authority collapses around 2181 BC, ending the Old Kingdom.', 'The Great Pyramid is the only one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still largely intact, and it held the record for tallest man-made structure on Earth for over 3,800 years.', 'The Great Pyramid was originally covered in polished white limestone casing stones that would have made it gleam brilliantly in the sun; most of this casing was later stripped away for other construction projects.', 'Egypt', 'Africa', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('d9169274-a87d-58ff-a1d5-14418229442f', 'L015', 'location', 'timbuktu', 'Timbuktu', 'A 2006 survey found that a third of young British respondents believed Timbuktu was a mythical place rather than a real medieval center of Islamic scholarship.', 'draft', 'Can you find this old desert city that some people once thought was made up?', 16.7759, -3.0093, 'Timbuktu', 'This is Timbuktu, in Mali. A 2006 survey found that many young people in Britain thought it was a made-up place, not a real center of learning.', 'Mali Emperor Mansa Musa, ruler over Timbuktu, passes through Cairo on his legendary pilgrimage to Mecca, cementing the city''s fame across the medieval world.', 'When did a famous ruler''s trip make this city known across the world?', '1324 AD', 1324, 'year', 'In 1324 AD, Mali''s ruler Mansa Musa passed through Cairo on his famous trip to Mecca. His journey made Timbuktu famous across the medieval world.', '4 items span 1100-1591 AD (491 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Mali Empire', 'Saadi Dynasty of Morocco — a Moroccan army conquers Timbuktu in 1591, ending Songhai control of the city.', 'A 2006 survey found that a third of young British respondents believed Timbuktu was a mythical place rather than a real medieval center of Islamic scholarship.', 'Timbuktu''s Sankore Madrasah once housed hundreds of thousands of manuscripts on subjects from astronomy to law, many of which survive today in private family libraries.', 'Mali', 'Africa', 'Review - Moroccan conquest of Timbuktu; present factually.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('68d97020-4305-5d5e-9056-fcfb0510293b', 'L016', 'location', 'great-zimbabwe', 'Great Zimbabwe', 'The name "Zimbabwe" derives from the Shona phrase for "houses of stone" — a fitting name for the largest stone structure in precolonial sub-Saharan Africa, built entirely without mortar.', 'draft', 'Can you find these huge stone ruins built without any mortar?', -20.267, 30.9333, 'Great Zimbabwe', 'This is Great Zimbabwe. Its name comes from a Shona phrase meaning ''houses of stone,'' and it is the largest stone structure built in that part of Africa before Europeans arrived.', 'Approximate date Great Zimbabwe reaches its peak population and regional influence, shortly before being abandoned.', 'When did this stone city reach its largest size, not long before people left it?', '1450 AD', 1450, 'year', 'Great Zimbabwe reached its peak around 1450 AD, shortly before people left the city. Its stone walls still stand today.', '4 items span 1100-1871 AD (771 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Kingdom of Zimbabwe (Shona civilization)', 'Kingdom of Mutapa — after Great Zimbabwe''s decline, political and trade power shifts north to the Mutapa Empire in the 15th century.', 'The name "Zimbabwe" derives from the Shona phrase for "houses of stone" — a fitting name for the largest stone structure in precolonial sub-Saharan Africa, built entirely without mortar.', 'European colonizers initially refused to believe Great Zimbabwe was built by Africans, wrongly attributing it to Phoenicians or other outsiders — a myth long since disproven by archaeology.', 'Zimbabwe', 'Africa', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('78555006-d121-58de-9446-1024a913b68c', 'L017', 'location', 'carthage', 'Carthage', 'At its height, Carthage was one of the wealthiest cities in the ancient Mediterranean; after the Romans rebuilt it, it grew into the second-largest city in the Western Roman Empire.', 'draft', 'Can you find this ancient city that once rivaled Rome?', 36.8528, 10.3233, 'Carthage', 'This is Carthage, in modern Tunisia. It was one of the richest cities in the ancient world, and after the Romans rebuilt it, it grew into the second-largest city in their empire.', 'Traditional founding date of Carthage by Phoenician settlers, led according to legend by Queen Dido.', 'When was this city founded, according to an old legend about a queen?', '814 BC', -814, 'year', 'Carthage was said to be founded in 814 BC. Legend says Queen Dido led the Phoenician settlers who built it.', '4 items span 814-46 BC (768 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Carthaginian (Punic) Empire', 'Roman Republic — Rome besieges and utterly destroys Carthage in 146 BC at the end of the Third Punic War.', 'At its height, Carthage was one of the wealthiest cities in the ancient Mediterranean; after the Romans rebuilt it, it grew into the second-largest city in the Western Roman Empire.', 'Carthage''s most famous general, Hannibal Barca, marched an army — including war elephants — over the Alps to invade Italy during the Second Punic War.', 'Tunisia', 'Africa', 'Review - Punic Wars and Rome''s destruction of Carthage; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('18a23328-bc5f-5958-bdbe-406c7933ce65', 'L018', 'location', 'chichen-itza', 'Chichen Itza', 'Chichen Itza''s name means "at the mouth of the well of the Itza" in Yucatec Maya, referring to the sacred sinkholes (cenotes) that supplied the city''s water and hosted ritual offerings.', 'draft', 'Can you find this ancient city named after a sacred well?', 20.6829, -88.5686, 'Chichen Itza', 'This is Chichen Itza, in Mexico. Its name means ''at the mouth of the well of the Itza,'' after the sacred sinkholes that gave the city its water.', 'Chichen Itza rises to become a dominant regional capital of the northern Yucatan peninsula.', 'When did this city become a powerful capital in its region?', '900 AD', 900, 'year', 'Chichen Itza rose to become a major capital around 900 AD. It ruled over the northern part of the Yucatan peninsula.', '4 items span 900-1221 AD (321 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Maya Civilization (Terminal Classic / Early Postclassic Period)', 'Mayapan — after Chichen Itza''s decline around 1100 AD, the city-state of Mayapan rises as the region''s new dominant power.', 'Chichen Itza''s name means "at the mouth of the well of the Itza" in Yucatec Maya, referring to the sacred sinkholes (cenotes) that supplied the city''s water and hosted ritual offerings.', 'In 2015, scientists discovered a previously unknown natural cenote hidden directly beneath El Castillo, confirming the pyramid was deliberately built over a sacred underground water source.', 'Mexico', 'North America', 'Review - political revolt against Chichen Itza''s rulers; present factually.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('291b2313-2f10-50a2-9b84-0d42b602562f', 'L019', 'location', 'teotihuacan', 'Teotihuacan', 'At its peak, Teotihuacan was one of the largest cities in the world, home to an estimated 100,000-200,000 people, yet its inhabitants left no known written language identifying who they were.', 'draft', 'Can you find this huge ancient city whose builders remain a mystery?', 19.6925, -98.8439, 'Teotihuacan', 'This is Teotihuacan, in Mexico. It was once one of the biggest cities in the world, with up to 200,000 people, but no one knows for sure who built it, since they left no writing behind.', 'Founding and early growth of Teotihuacan begins in the Basin of Mexico.', 'When did this huge city begin to grow in what is now Mexico?', '100 BC', -100, 'year', 'Teotihuacan began growing around 100 BC. It later became one of the largest cities in the ancient world.', '4 items span 100 BC-550 AD (650 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Teotihuacan Civilization (Classic Mesoamerica)', 'None politically continuous — the city collapsed around 550 AD amid internal upheaval; centuries later the Aztecs revered its ruins as the sacred birthplace of the gods.', 'At its peak, Teotihuacan was one of the largest cities in the world, home to an estimated 100,000-200,000 people, yet its inhabitants left no known written language identifying who they were.', 'The name "Teotihuacan," meaning "the place where the gods were created," was given to the already-ancient ruins centuries later by the Aztecs — the original name used by the city''s builders remains unknown.', 'Mexico', 'North America', 'Review - burning of the city''s ceremonial core amid collapse; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('081f98d6-9852-5677-9700-2d0f27008d3e', 'L020', 'location', 'tenochtitlan-mexico-city', 'Tenochtitlan (Mexico City)', 'At its height, Tenochtitlan may have housed up to 200,000 people, rivaling or surpassing the largest cities in Europe, and it awed the invading Spanish with its causeways, canals, and floating gardens (chinampas).', 'draft', 'Can you find this island city with canals and floating gardens?', 19.433, -99.133, 'Tenochtitlan (Mexico City)', 'This is Tenochtitlan, now Mexico City. It may have held up to 200,000 people, and its canals and floating gardens amazed the Spanish when they first arrived.', 'The Mexica (Aztec) people found Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco, according to legend at the site where they saw an eagle perched on a cactus.', 'When did a legend about an eagle on a cactus mark the start of this city?', '1325 AD', 1325, 'year', 'The Mexica people founded Tenochtitlan in 1325 AD. Legend says they built it where they saw an eagle sitting on a cactus.', '4 items span 1325-1521 AD (196 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Aztec Empire', 'Spanish Empire — Hernan Cortes and his Tlaxcalan allies conquer the city in 1521, founding Mexico City atop its ruins.', 'At its height, Tenochtitlan may have housed up to 200,000 people, rivaling or surpassing the largest cities in Europe, and it awed the invading Spanish with its causeways, canals, and floating gardens (chinampas).', 'In 1978, utility workers accidentally unearthed a massive carved stone disc depicting the dismembered moon goddess Coyolxauhqui, leading to major excavations of the Templo Mayor beneath modern Mexico City.', 'Mexico', 'North America', 'Review - Spanish conquest ending the Aztec Empire; present factually and respectfully of Indigenous history.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('bda1cfec-5295-587c-aed5-96d086d8d39b', 'L021', 'location', 'tikal', 'Tikal', 'LiDAR laser-mapping surveys published in 2018 revealed over 60,000 previously unknown structures hidden beneath the Guatemalan jungle canopy around Tikal, suggesting the Classic Maya population was far larger than once believed.', 'draft', 'Can you find this jungle city hiding thousands of buried buildings?', 17.2219, -89.6236, 'Tikal', 'This is Tikal, in Guatemala. In 2018, laser scans found over 60,000 hidden buildings under the jungle, showing that far more people once lived there than anyone thought.', 'A Teotihuacan-linked warlord named Siyaj K''ak'' conquers Tikal and installs a new ruling dynasty, reshaping the city''s power and alliances.', 'When did a warlord from a faraway city conquer this jungle capital?', '378 AD', 378, 'year', 'In 378 AD, a warlord named Siyaj K''ak'' conquered Tikal and set up a new ruling family. He had ties to the distant and powerful city of Teotihuacan.', '4 items span 378-562 AD (184 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Maya Civilization (Classic Period)', 'Rival city-state Calakmul defeats Tikal in a "Star War" in 562 AD, beginning over a century of decline before Tikal''s later resurgence and eventual abandonment around 900 AD.', 'LiDAR laser-mapping surveys published in 2018 revealed over 60,000 previously unknown structures hidden beneath the Guatemalan jungle canopy around Tikal, suggesting the Classic Maya population was far larger than once believed.', 'Temple I rises 47 meters (154 feet) above Tikal''s Great Plaza; archaeologists discovered the tomb of King Jasaw Chan K''awiil inside it in 1962, filled with jade, pottery, and carved bone artifacts.', 'Guatemala', 'North America (Central America)', 'Review - conquest and the Tikal-Calakmul ''Star War''; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('844ce175-5332-5ae4-8a19-b881f6b71195', 'L022', 'location', 'machu-picchu', 'Machu Picchu', 'Machu Picchu was built directly atop two fault lines, and the Inca deliberately used the fractured, easily quarried stone this created for construction.', 'draft', 'Can you find this mountain estate built on top of cracked rock?', -13.1631, -72.545, 'Machu Picchu', 'This is Machu Picchu, in Peru. It sits right on top of two fault lines, and its builders used the broken rock there to help construct it.', 'Machu Picchu is built as a royal estate under the Inca emperor Pachacuti.', 'When was this mountain estate built for a powerful ruler?', '1450 AD', 1450, 'year', 'Machu Picchu was built around 1450 AD. The Inca emperor Pachacuti had it built as his royal estate.', '4 items span 1450-1572 AD (122 yrs) > 15-yr threshold; place traces the Inca collapse across distinct sites -> Match by place, not Order.', 'Inca Empire', 'Spanish Empire — though the Inca Empire fell to Spanish conquest between 1532-1572, Machu Picchu itself was never found by the conquistadors and was simply abandoned.', 'Machu Picchu was built directly atop two fault lines, and the Inca deliberately used the fractured, easily quarried stone this created for construction.', 'Machu Picchu remained essentially unknown to the outside world until American historian Hiram Bingham was guided to it by local farmers in 1911.', 'Peru', 'South America', 'Review - Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire; present factually and respectfully of Indigenous history.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('4b3c9677-15d7-53ff-94ae-d189c7af0f9d', 'L023', 'location', 'cusco', 'Cusco', 'The name Cusco (Qusqu) is thought to derive from a phrase meaning "rock of the owl," tied to the city''s founding legend of the Ayar siblings; some Inca believed the city was laid out in the shape of a sacred puma.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city said to be shaped like a sacred animal?', -13.532, -71.9675, 'Cusco', 'This is Cusco, in Peru. Some Inca believed the city was laid out in the shape of a sacred puma.', 'Manco Capac founds Cusco as the capital of the Kingdom of Cusco, which later grows into the Inca Empire.', 'When was this city founded as the capital of a small, growing kingdom?', '1200 AD', 1200, 'year', 'Cusco was founded around 1200 AD by Manco Capac. It started as the capital of a small kingdom that grew into the huge Inca Empire.', '4 items span 1200-1533 AD (333 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Inca Empire', 'Spanish Empire — Francisco Pizarro''s forces occupy Cusco in 1533, ending Inca imperial rule.', 'The name Cusco (Qusqu) is thought to derive from a phrase meaning "rock of the owl," tied to the city''s founding legend of the Ayar siblings; some Inca believed the city was laid out in the shape of a sacred puma.', 'Cusco''s Coricancha (Temple of the Sun) was once covered in sheets of solid gold representing the sun god Inti — nearly all of it was melted down and looted by the Spanish after the conquest.', 'Peru', 'South America', 'Review - Inca civil war and Spanish conquest; present factually and respectfully of Indigenous history.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('044bad2a-384d-50d5-be06-59dd2beed9e6', 'L024', 'location', 'tiwanaku', 'Tiwanaku', 'A 2016 drone survey revealed previously unknown structures spanning over 400 hectares around Tiwanaku, including a stone temple far larger than researchers had realized.', 'draft', 'Can you find this ancient capital that hid more ruins than anyone knew?', -16.5547, -68.6733, 'Tiwanaku', 'This is Tiwanaku, in Bolivia. A drone survey in 2016 found hidden ruins spread across a huge area, including a giant stone temple no one knew was there before.', 'Tiwanaku reaches its peak as the political and religious capital of the Tiwanaku civilization around Lake Titicaca.', 'When did this lakeside city reach its peak as a powerful capital?', '500 AD', 500, 'year', 'Tiwanaku reached its peak around 500 AD. It was the political and religious capital for people living near Lake Titicaca.', '4 items span 110-1450 AD (1,340 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Tiwanaku Civilization (Pre-Inca Andean)', 'Collapse around 1000 AD, likely due to prolonged drought; the region was later incorporated into the Inca Empire in the 15th century, which regarded Tiwanaku''s ruins as sacred.', 'A 2016 drone survey revealed previously unknown structures spanning over 400 hectares around Tiwanaku, including a stone temple far larger than researchers had realized.', 'The Gate of the Sun was carved from a single massive block of andesite stone, and researchers believe it may not even stand in its originally intended location today.', 'Bolivia', 'South America', 'Review - Inca conquest of the Titicaca Basin; present factually.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('4ca3baaa-65fb-5741-92d8-ffbbabc9c97f', 'L025', 'location', 'rapa-nui-easter-island', 'Rapa Nui (Easter Island)', 'The island''s famous moai statues were carved from volcanic tuff; the largest ever completed weighs about 82 tonnes, but hundreds more lie unfinished in the island''s quarry.', 'draft', 'Can you find this remote island famous for its giant stone heads?', -27.1127, -109.3497, 'Rapa Nui (Easter Island)', 'This is Rapa Nui, also called Easter Island. Its famous moai statues were carved from volcanic rock, and the biggest finished one weighs about 82 tonnes.', 'Polynesian voyagers settle Rapa Nui, the most isolated inhabited island in the world.', 'When did sailors first settle this faraway island?', '1200 AD', 1200, 'year', 'Polynesian sailors settled Rapa Nui around 1200 AD. It is the most isolated inhabited island in the world.', '4 items span 1200-1722 AD (522 yrs) > 15-yr threshold -> Match by year, not Order.', 'Rapa Nui Civilization (Polynesian)', 'European contact and colonization — Dutch explorer Jacob Roggeveen encounters the island on Easter Sunday, 1722; Chile formally annexes it in 1888.', 'The island''s famous moai statues were carved from volcanic tuff; the largest ever completed weighs about 82 tonnes, but hundreds more lie unfinished in the island''s quarry.', 'In the 1860s, Peruvian slave raiders abducted roughly half of Rapa Nui''s population, and subsequent smallpox epidemics were so devastating that only 111 islanders remained by 1877.', 'Chile', 'Oceania', 'Review - European colonization; also note the island''s population/ecological-collapse narrative is actively debated among scholars -- avoid presenting the older ''ecocide'' story as settled fact.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the 25-Location Subject Pack (Bob-provided, web-search-verified per that pack''s own Read Me, delivered 2026-09-07). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('cb331512-9eba-57c6-8caf-972f87f806ff', 'L026', 'location', 'beijing-forbidden-city', 'Beijing (Forbidden City)', 'Its name, Zijincheng (''Purple Forbidden City''), references the North Star''s mythic heavenly abode of the Jade Emperor; it is the largest preserved royal palace complex and the largest collection of ancient wooden structures still standing in the world.', 'draft', 'Can you find this huge palace named after the color purple?', 39.9163, 116.3908, 'Beijing (Forbidden City)', 'This is the Forbidden City in Beijing, China. Its name means ''Purple Forbidden City,'' and it holds the largest group of old wooden buildings anywhere in the world.', 'Construction of the Forbidden City was completed and it became the imperial palace of the Ming dynasty under the Yongle Emperor.', 'When was this giant palace finished for a powerful emperor?', '1420 AD', 1420, 'year', 'The Forbidden City was finished in 1420 AD. It became home to the Ming dynasty''s Yongle Emperor.', '4 items span 1406-1925 AD (519 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Ming and Qing Dynasty Imperial China', 'The Qing dynasty and imperial rule ended when Emperor Puyi abdicated in 1912 (he remained in residence until his 1924 expulsion), and the complex became the Palace Museum in 1925.', 'Its name, Zijincheng (''Purple Forbidden City''), references the North Star''s mythic heavenly abode of the Jade Emperor; it is the largest preserved royal palace complex and the largest collection of ancient wooden structures still standing in the world.', 'The complex covers 72 hectares and contains nearly 1,000 surviving buildings, popularly (if inexactly) said to number 9,999.5 rooms.', 'China', 'Asia', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('0c12e8c8-fbff-5873-82e5-e2b9c721c279', 'L027', 'location', 'isfahan', 'Isfahan', 'Isfahan''s popular epithet ''Nesf-e Jahan'' (''Half of the World'') celebrated its immense wealth, beauty, and array of wonders under Shah Abbas I.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city once nicknamed ''Half of the World''?', 32.6547, 51.673, 'Isfahan', 'This is Isfahan, in Iran. People gave it the nickname ''Half of the World'' because of its great wealth, beauty, and wonders under Shah Abbas I.', 'Shah Abbas I moved the Safavid capital from Qazvin to Isfahan, launching a massive building program centered on Naqsh-e Jahan Square.', 'When did a ruler move his capital here and start a huge building project?', '1598 AD', 1598, 'year', 'Shah Abbas I moved his capital to Isfahan in 1598 AD. He then began a huge building project around its main square.', '4 items span 1598-1722 AD (124 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Safavid Empire', 'Safavid Isfahan''s golden age ended when Afghan (Hotak) invaders besieged and sacked the city in 1722, precipitating the empire''s collapse.', 'Isfahan''s popular epithet ''Nesf-e Jahan'' (''Half of the World'') celebrated its immense wealth, beauty, and array of wonders under Shah Abbas I.', 'Naqsh-e Jahan Square is widely cited as the second-largest city square in the world, after Beijing''s Tiananmen Square.', 'Iran', 'Asia', 'Review - Afghan (Hotak) siege and sack of Isfahan (1722); present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('04d0c856-7089-50b1-9f77-1596b6df518d', 'L028', 'location', 'samarkand', 'Samarkand', 'The city''s name is believed to derive from a Turkic phrase meaning roughly ''fat/prosperous city,'' reflecting its long history as a wealthy Silk Road trade hub.', 'draft', 'Can you find this old trading city along the Silk Road?', 39.6547, 66.9756, 'Samarkand', 'This is Samarkand, in Uzbekistan. Its name may come from words meaning ''prosperous city,'' fitting for a place that grew rich from Silk Road trade.', 'Timur (Tamerlane) made Samarkand the capital of his empire, launching its transformation into a showcase of Timurid architecture.', 'When did a famous conqueror make this city his capital?', '1370 AD', 1370, 'year', 'Timur, also called Tamerlane, made Samarkand his capital in 1370 AD. He filled the city with grand new buildings.', '4 items span 1370-1500 AD (130 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Timurid Empire', 'In 1500-1501, nomadic Uzbek forces under Muhammad Shaybani conquered Samarkand, ending Timurid rule and founding the Bukhara Khanate.', 'The city''s name is believed to derive from a Turkic phrase meaning roughly ''fat/prosperous city,'' reflecting its long history as a wealthy Silk Road trade hub.', 'Samarkand was sacked by Genghis Khan''s Mongol army in 1220, one of the most devastating conquests of the medieval Silk Road, before later rising again as Timur''s capital.', 'Uzbekistan', 'Asia', 'Review - Uzbek conquest of Samarkand under Muhammad Shaybani; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('5652333e-1d16-5698-bbd2-2180a545cf50', 'L029', 'location', 'ayutthaya', 'Ayutthaya', 'By around 1700 AD, Ayutthaya was one of the largest and wealthiest cities in the world, rivaling or surpassing contemporary London, and hosted major international trade and diplomacy, including a Siamese embassy to Louis XIV''s court at Versailles in 1686.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city that once rivaled London in size and wealth?', 14.3572, 100.5677, 'Ayutthaya', 'This is Ayutthaya, in Thailand. By about 1700 AD, it was one of the largest, richest cities in the world and even sent an embassy to the king of France.', 'King Ramathibodi I (U Thong) founded Ayutthaya as the capital of the Ayutthaya Kingdom.', 'When was this city founded as the capital of a new kingdom?', '1351 AD', 1351, 'year', 'King Ramathibodi I founded Ayutthaya in 1351 AD. It became the capital of the Ayutthaya Kingdom.', '4 items span 1351-1767 AD (416 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Ayutthaya Kingdom', 'After a 14-month siege, the Burmese Konbaung army captured and destroyed Ayutthaya in April 1767, ending the 417-year-old kingdom.', 'By around 1700 AD, Ayutthaya was one of the largest and wealthiest cities in the world, rivaling or surpassing contemporary London, and hosted major international trade and diplomacy, including a Siamese embassy to Louis XIV''s court at Versailles in 1686.', 'European traders in the 16th-17th centuries ranked Ayutthaya among the three great powers of Asia, alongside Ming China and Vijayanagara.', 'Thailand', 'Asia', 'Review - Burmese conquest and destruction of Ayutthaya (1569, 1767); present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('bf214174-7dc1-5ca4-9eee-218a7bbbf506', 'L030', 'location', 'mohenjo-daro', 'Mohenjo-daro', 'The modern name ''Mohenjo-daro'' means ''Mound of the Dead Men'' in Sindhi; the city''s original name is unknown since its script remains undeciphered.', 'draft', 'Can you find this ancient city whose true name is still unknown?', 27.3292, 68.1389, 'Mohenjo-daro', 'This is Mohenjo-daro, in Pakistan. Its modern name means ''Mound of the Dead Men,'' since no one has read its old writing to learn the city''s real name.', 'Mohenjo-daro was built as one of the largest cities of the Indus Valley (Harappan) Civilization, with advanced urban planning and sanitation.', 'When was this ancient city built with surprisingly careful planning?', '2500 BC', -2500, 'year', 'Mohenjo-daro was built around 2500 BC. It was one of the largest cities of the Indus Valley civilization, with well-planned streets and sanitation.', '4 items span 2500 BC-1980 AD (4480 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Indus Valley (Harappan) Civilization', 'The city was gradually abandoned by around 1700 BC, likely due to river shifts, flooding, and environmental/resource decline, well before any single conquering power replaced it.', 'The modern name ''Mohenjo-daro'' means ''Mound of the Dead Men'' in Sindhi; the city''s original name is unknown since its script remains undeciphered.', 'Mohenjo-daro had over 700 wells and a citywide covered drainage system, among the most advanced urban sanitation infrastructure of the ancient world.', 'Pakistan', 'Asia', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('eb303ed0-4c3b-5977-8fc2-2137c437be43', 'L031', 'location', 'paris', 'Paris', 'Paris is nicknamed the ''City of Light'' (Ville Lumiere), both for its early adoption of street lighting and its central role in the Age of Enlightenment.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city nicknamed the ''City of Light''?', 48.853, 2.3499, 'Paris', 'This is Paris, France. It earned the nickname ''City of Light'' for being an early user of street lamps and a center of new ideas.', 'Clovis I, king of the Franks, made Paris his capital, beginning its rise as a seat of royal power.', 'When did a king make this city his royal capital?', '508 AD', 508, 'year', 'Clovis I, king of the Franks, made Paris his capital in 508 AD. That began the city''s rise as a center of royal power.', '4 items span 508-1190 AD (682 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Frankish Kingdom / Capetian and later French monarchy', 'Paris became the permanent, stable capital of France under the Capetian dynasty beginning with Hugh Capet''s election in 987 AD, cementing its status through the medieval and modern eras.', 'Paris is nicknamed the ''City of Light'' (Ville Lumiere), both for its early adoption of street lighting and its central role in the Age of Enlightenment.', 'In 1895, the Lumiere brothers held the world''s first public film screening for a paying audience in a Paris cafe, launching the age of cinema.', 'France', 'Europe', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('26d66a8e-9dde-5c68-9fac-d030562a2bc9', 'L032', 'location', 'prague', 'Prague', 'Prague is nicknamed the ''City of a Hundred Spires'' and the ''Golden City'' for its dense skyline of Gothic and Baroque church towers.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city known for its hundreds of tall spires?', 50.091, 14.4016, 'Prague', 'This is Prague, in the Czech Republic. People call it the ''City of a Hundred Spires'' and the ''Golden City'' for its many church towers.', 'Charles IV became King of Bohemia (and later Holy Roman Emperor in 1355), transforming Prague into an imperial capital and center of learning.', 'When did a new king turn this city into an imperial capital?', '1346 AD', 1346, 'year', 'Charles IV became King of Bohemia in 1346 AD. He later turned Prague into an imperial capital and a center of learning.', '4 items span 1346-1526 AD (180 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Kingdom of Bohemia / Holy Roman Empire (Luxembourg dynasty)', 'Bohemia''s independent monarchy ended in 1526 when the Habsburgs assumed the Bohemian crown, and Prague''s political centrality later declined after the 1620 Battle of White Mountain.', 'Prague is nicknamed the ''City of a Hundred Spires'' and the ''Golden City'' for its dense skyline of Gothic and Baroque church towers.', 'Charles University, founded in Prague in 1348, is the oldest university in Central Europe.', 'Czech Republic', 'Europe', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('8af81c77-6340-515f-89f1-0501ea8706fa', 'L033', 'location', 'cordoba', 'Cordoba', 'In the 10th century Cordoba was the second-largest city in Europe and a leading center of learning, home to an estimated 80+ libraries, including a royal library said to hold several hundred thousand volumes.', 'draft', 'Can you find this old city that once had more than 80 libraries?', 37.879, 4.7794, 'Cordoba', 'This is Cordoba, in Spain. In the 900s it was one of the biggest cities in Europe, and it held more than 80 libraries full of books.', 'Abd al-Rahman III declared himself Caliph, making Cordoba the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate of Cordoba at the height of its power.', 'Can you guess the year a ruler here named himself Caliph, making this city the capital of a powerful kingdom?', '929 AD', 929, 'year', 'In 929 AD, Abd al-Rahman III declared himself Caliph. This made Cordoba the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate, right at the height of its power.', '4 items span 756-1236 AD (480 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Umayyad Caliphate of Cordoba (Islamic Spain / al-Andalus)', 'Christian forces under Ferdinand III of Castile captured Cordoba in 1236 during the Reconquista, ending Muslim rule and converting the Great Mosque into a cathedral.', 'In the 10th century Cordoba was the second-largest city in Europe and a leading center of learning, home to an estimated 80+ libraries, including a royal library said to hold several hundred thousand volumes.', 'Cordoba''s era of Muslim, Christian, and Jewish scholars coexisting and translating classical Greek texts helped preserve and transmit ancient knowledge to medieval Europe.', 'Spain', 'Europe', 'Review - Reconquista conquest of Cordoba by Ferdinand III of Castile (1236); present factually and respectfully.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('3a4a7e19-6dae-5a27-abd0-86d727b4ac89', 'L034', 'location', 'knossos', 'Knossos', 'Knossos''s sprawling, maze-like palace layout is believed to have inspired the ancient Greek myth of the Labyrinth built by Daedalus to contain the Minotaur.', 'draft', 'Can you find this place, where a maze-like palace inspired an old Greek myth about a monster?', 35.2981, 25.1631, 'Knossos', 'This is Knossos, on the island of Crete in Greece. Its winding palace halls are thought to have inspired the myth of the Labyrinth, built to trap the Minotaur.', 'Knossos''s ''New Palace'' was built during the Second Palace Period, marking the height of Minoan civilization on Crete.', 'Can you guess the year workers finished a grand new palace here, at the peak of an early island civilization?', '1700 BC', -1700, 'year', 'Around 1700 BC, the "New Palace" at Knossos was built. This marked the height of the Minoan civilization on Crete.', '4 items span 1900 BC-1900 AD (3800 yrs) >= 15-yr threshold -> Match by year.', 'Minoan Civilization', 'The palace at Knossos was destroyed by fire around 1350 BC, after Mycenaean Greeks had taken control of the site, marking the end of Minoan dominance and the rise of Mycenaean power in the Aegean.', 'Knossos''s sprawling, maze-like palace layout is believed to have inspired the ancient Greek myth of the Labyrinth built by Daedalus to contain the Minotaur.', 'The Palace of Knossos featured advanced plumbing, including one of the world''s earliest known flushing-toilet systems and light wells for interior illumination.', 'Greece', 'Europe', 'Review - destruction of the palace by fire following the Mycenaean takeover; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('bad8fd69-a096-571f-80f7-f5a497c59f11', 'L035', 'location', 'novgorod-veliky-novgorod', 'Novgorod (Veliky Novgorod)', 'Novgorod is famous for over 1,000 surviving ''birchbark letters'' (berestyanye gramoty), everyday documents written on birch bark from the 11th-15th centuries, including letters written by children, revealing remarkably widespread medieval literacy.', 'draft', 'Can you find this old city where even kids once wrote letters on tree bark?', 58.5215, 31.2755, 'Novgorod (Veliky Novgorod)', 'This is Novgorod, in Russia. People have found over 1,000 old letters here written on birch bark, some by children, showing that many people could read and write long ago.', 'According to the Primary Chronicle, the Varangian chieftain Rurik was invited by local Slavic and Finnic tribes to rule at Novgorod, an event traditionally marking the founding of the Rurikid dynasty and the Russian state.', 'Can you guess the year a chief was invited to rule this city, an event that helped start a whole new state?', '862 AD', 862, 'year', 'In 862 AD, a chief named Rurik came to rule Novgorod. This event is remembered as the start of the Russian state.', 'Year span 862-1478 = 616 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Novgorod Republic (1136-1478 AD), a major medieval East Slavic city-state and Hanseatic League trading hub', 'Grand Prince Ivan III of Moscow conquered Novgorod in 1478, dissolving the veche and the Republic and absorbing the city into the unified Muscovite state.', 'Novgorod is famous for over 1,000 surviving ''birchbark letters'' (berestyanye gramoty), everyday documents written on birch bark from the 11th-15th centuries, including letters written by children, revealing remarkably widespread medieval literacy.', 'Novgorod was governed by the veche, a public assembly of citizens that elected officials and made major decisions, making it one of medieval Europe''s earliest and longest-running experiments in republican self-governance.', 'Russia', 'Europe', 'Review - Ivan III''s conquest of Novgorod, ending the Republic (1478); present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('fe9792a7-caf6-55c2-abc8-7e8da5c58fa8', 'L036', 'location', 'alexandria', 'Alexandria', 'Alexandria''s Great Library was the largest repository of knowledge in the ancient world, attracting scholars such as Euclid, Eratosthenes, and Hero of Alexandria.', 'draft', 'Can you find this city that once had the biggest library in the ancient world?', 31.2139, 29.8856, 'Alexandria', 'This is Alexandria, in Egypt. Its Great Library held more knowledge than any other place in the ancient world, drawing famous thinkers like Euclid.', 'Alexander the Great founded the city of Alexandria on Egypt''s Mediterranean coast, intending it as a new Greek administrative and cultural capital.', 'Can you guess the year a famous conqueror founded this city on Egypt''s coast?', '331 BC', -331, 'year', 'In 331 BC, Alexander the Great founded Alexandria on the coast of Egypt. He wanted it to be a new Greek capital for learning and government.', 'Year span -331 to -30 = 301 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Ptolemaic Kingdom (305-30 BC), Hellenistic Egypt''s capital and the ancient world''s leading center of Greek learning', 'Rome annexed Egypt in 30 BC after defeating Cleopatra VII and Mark Antony, making Alexandria a provincial capital of the Roman Empire.', 'Alexandria''s Great Library was the largest repository of knowledge in the ancient world, attracting scholars such as Euclid, Eratosthenes, and Hero of Alexandria.', 'Alexandria was laid out on a Hippodamian grid plan by Alexander''s architect Dinocrates, with wide, straight intersecting streets that were unusually organized for a city of its time.', 'Egypt', 'Africa', 'Review - Roman conquest of Egypt following the defeat of Cleopatra VII and Mark Antony; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('71b9feac-e420-5abb-b245-999a022a0bac', 'L037', 'location', 'axum-aksum', 'Axum (Aksum)', 'Aksum was named one of the four great powers of the ancient world, alongside Rome, Persia, and China, by the 3rd-century Persian prophet Mani.', 'draft', 'Can you find this ancient city once ranked among the greatest powers in the world?', 14.1208, 38.7278, 'Axum (Aksum)', 'This is Aksum, in Ethiopia. A writer in the 200s AD named it one of the four great powers of the world, right alongside Rome, Persia, and China.', 'King Ezana of Aksum converted to Christianity, making the Kingdom of Aksum one of the first states in the world to adopt Christianity as its official religion.', 'Can you guess the year a king here chose a new religion for his whole kingdom?', '330 AD', 330, 'year', 'In 330 AD, King Ezana of Aksum became a Christian. This made Aksum one of the first states in the world to adopt Christianity as its official religion.', 'Year span 50-700 = 650 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Kingdom of Aksum (Aksumite Empire), a major Red Sea trading power from roughly the 1st to 8th centuries AD', 'Arab expansion after the 7th century cut Aksum off from its Red Sea trade routes; the political capital shifted south and the empire fragmented, giving way by the 12th century to the Zagwe dynasty.', 'Aksum was named one of the four great powers of the ancient world, alongside Rome, Persia, and China, by the 3rd-century Persian prophet Mani.', 'Aksum was among the earliest sub-Saharan African states to mint its own coinage, issuing gold, silver, and bronze coins from the 3rd century AD onward, some of the first coins in the world to bear a Christian cross.', 'Ethiopia', 'Africa', 'Review - Aksumite military invasion of Himyar under King Kaleb; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('4b191f33-db7e-5162-b588-2f4b328421f9', 'L038', 'location', 'meroe', 'Meroe', 'Meroe contains over 200 steep-sided pyramids, more numerous than all of Egypt''s pyramids combined, built as royal tombs for Kushite kings and queens.', 'draft', 'Can you find this place with more pyramids than all of Egypt combined?', 16.9333, 33.7264, 'Meroe', 'This is Meroe, in Sudan. It has over 200 steep pyramids, built as tombs for the kings and queens of the ancient Kingdom of Kush.', 'King Aspelta moved the royal capital of the Kingdom of Kush from Napata to Meroe, after Napata was sacked by Egyptian forces under Psamtik II.', 'Can you guess the year a king moved his kingdom''s capital to this city?', '591 BC', -591, 'year', 'In 591 BC, King Aspelta moved the capital of Kush to Meroe. He moved it after enemy forces attacked the old capital of Napata.', 'Year span -591 to 350 = 941 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Kingdom of Kush, Meroitic Period (c. 300 BC - 350 AD), a Nubian civilization along the middle Nile', 'Meroe was conquered and destroyed around 350 AD by King Ezana of the Kingdom of Aksum, bringing an end to the Kushite kingdom.', 'Meroe contains over 200 steep-sided pyramids, more numerous than all of Egypt''s pyramids combined, built as royal tombs for Kushite kings and queens.', 'Meroe was a major center of iron smelting in ancient Africa, earning it the 19th-century nickname "the Birmingham of Africa" from archaeologist Archibald Sayce.', 'Sudan', 'Africa', 'Review - Meroitic-Roman War and the destruction of Meroe; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('eaa580a0-19f3-5de0-aafa-312fe36a33a5', 'L039', 'location', 'fez-fes', 'Fez (Fes)', 'Fez gave its name to the red felt "fez" cap, which the city held a manufacturing monopoly on until the late 19th century.', 'draft', 'Can you find the city that gave its name to a famous red hat?', 34.0633, -4.9764, 'Fez (Fes)', 'This is Fez, in Morocco. The city made the red felt "fez" cap for centuries and held the only right to make it until the late 1800s.', 'Idris I founded the city of Fez, establishing it as the capital of the Idrisid dynasty, Morocco''s first independent Islamic state.', 'Can you guess the year a ruler founded this city as the capital of a brand-new Islamic state?', '789 AD', 789, 'year', 'In 789 AD, Idris I founded Fez. It became the capital of the Idrisid dynasty, Morocco''s first independent Islamic state.', 'Year span 789-1912 = 1123 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Marinid dynasty (13th-15th centuries), Fez''s golden age as Morocco''s political and intellectual capital', 'Fez''s status as Morocco''s sole capital waned as later dynasties shifted the seat of power among Fez, Meknes, and Marrakesh; the capital ultimately moved to Rabat in the 20th century under the French protectorate.', 'Fez gave its name to the red felt "fez" cap, which the city held a manufacturing monopoly on until the late 19th century.', 'Fez''s Chouara Tannery, in continuous operation since the medieval period, still uses centuries-old techniques, soaking hides in stone vats of natural dyes and pigeon-dung softener.', 'Morocco', 'Africa', 'Review - French colonization of Morocco (Treaty of Fez, 1912); present factually and respectfully.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('08b2fc9e-8407-5d4f-ae4a-c6e0b6683ced', 'L040', 'location', 'kilwa-kisiwani', 'Kilwa Kisiwani', 'Kilwa controlled the gold trade route from the Great Zimbabwe/Sofala region, making it the wealthiest and most powerful city-state on the Swahili Coast by the 14th century.', 'draft', 'Can you find this island city that grew rich from the gold trade?', -8.9578, 39.5122, 'Kilwa Kisiwani', 'This is Kilwa Kisiwani, in Tanzania. By the 1300s it controlled the gold trade route and became the richest, most powerful city on the Swahili Coast.', 'According to Swahili Coast tradition, the Persian merchant Ali ibn al-Hassan Shirazi founded the Kilwa Sultanate, which grew into the dominant trading power of the East African coast.', 'Can you guess the year a merchant is said to have founded the sultanate that made this city powerful?', '1000 AD', 1000, 'year', 'Around 1000 AD, a merchant named Ali ibn al-Hassan Shirazi is said to have founded the Kilwa Sultanate. It grew into the strongest trading power on the East African coast.', 'Year span 1000-1505 = 505 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Kilwa Sultanate, a Swahili Coast city-state and Indian Ocean trade hub, with its golden age in the 13th-15th centuries', 'Portuguese forces conquered and sacked Kilwa in 1505, ending its independence and beginning a long decline; the town was fully abandoned by the 1840s.', 'Kilwa controlled the gold trade route from the Great Zimbabwe/Sofala region, making it the wealthiest and most powerful city-state on the Swahili Coast by the 14th century.', 'Moroccan traveler Ibn Battuta visited Kilwa in 1331 and described it as one of the most beautiful and well-constructed towns he had seen.', 'Tanzania', 'Africa', 'Review - Portuguese conquest and sack of Kilwa (1505); present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('ee0fcfff-a654-5fc3-8725-e086df9c0786', 'L041', 'location', 'cahokia', 'Cahokia', 'At its peak around 1100 AD, Cahokia''s population is estimated at 10,000-20,000+ people, larger than London at the time and the largest city in North America until Philadelphia surpassed it in the late 18th century.', 'draft', 'Can you find the place that was once the biggest city in North America?', 38.6539, -90.0644, 'Cahokia', 'This is Cahokia, in the United States. Around 1100 AD, as many as 20,000 people lived here, more than lived in London at the time.', 'Cahokia underwent a rapid burst of urbanization and mound-building, transforming it into the largest pre-Columbian settlement north of Mexico.', 'Can you guess the year this settlement grew fast into the largest city north of Mexico?', '1050 AD', 1050, 'year', 'Around 1050 AD, Cahokia grew quickly and built many large mounds. It became the biggest settlement north of Mexico before Europeans arrived.', 'Year span 1050-1350 = 300 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Mississippian culture, Stirling Phase (c. 1050-1200 AD), the peak of Cahokia''s political and ceremonial power', 'Cahokia was gradually depopulated and abandoned by around 1350-1400 AD, likely due to a combination of environmental strain (deforestation, flooding, drought), resource depletion, and social stress; the site was empty well before European contact.', 'At its peak around 1100 AD, Cahokia''s population is estimated at 10,000-20,000+ people, larger than London at the time and the largest city in North America until Philadelphia surpassed it in the late 18th century.', 'Cahokia''s "Woodhenge," a large circle of wooden posts, functioned as a solar calendar, marking solstices and equinoxes for ceremonial and agricultural timing.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('9d8849eb-81ea-56ea-9403-cbfbcc87d01a', 'L042', 'location', 'mesa-verde', 'Mesa Verde', 'Cliff Palace, the largest cliff dwelling in North America, contains about 150 rooms and 23 kivas and is estimated to have housed roughly 100-125 people.', 'draft', 'Can you find the place with the biggest cliff dwelling in North America?', 37.1799, -108.4623, 'Mesa Verde', 'This is Mesa Verde, in the United States. Its Cliff Palace, built into a cliff, has about 150 rooms and once housed 100 to 125 people.', 'Ancestral Puebloans began constructing the large, multi-story cliff dwellings, including Cliff Palace, in the sandstone alcoves of Mesa Verde''s canyon walls.', 'Can you guess the year builders began carving huge homes into this place''s cliff walls?', '1190 AD', 1190, 'year', 'Around 1190 AD, the Ancestral Puebloans began building large, multi-story homes in the sandstone cliffs of Mesa Verde, including Cliff Palace.', 'Year span 550-1888 = 1338 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Ancestral Puebloan culture, Pueblo III period (1150-1300 AD)', 'A prolonged ''Great Drought'' from roughly 1276-1300 AD, combined with resource depletion and social stress, led the Ancestral Puebloans to abandon Mesa Verde entirely by around 1300 AD, migrating south toward the Rio Grande and Little Colorado River regions.', 'Cliff Palace, the largest cliff dwelling in North America, contains about 150 rooms and 23 kivas and is estimated to have housed roughly 100-125 people.', 'Ancestral Puebloans at Mesa Verde built sophisticated reservoirs and check dams, such as the Far View Reservoir, to capture and manage scarce rainwater for their mesa-top farms.', 'United States', 'North America', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('4ccfeeef-5791-5fe0-8634-ec29b2d7ac19', 'L043', 'location', 'quebec-city', 'Quebec City', 'The city''s name comes from the Algonquin word "kebec," meaning "where the river narrows"; Old Quebec is the only walled city north of Mexico with its fortifications still intact.', 'draft', 'Can you find the only walled city north of Mexico with its walls still standing today?', 46.8139, -71.208, 'Quebec City', 'This is Quebec City, in Canada. Its name comes from an Algonquin word meaning "where the river narrows," and its old town still has its full stone walls.', 'Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec City on the Saint Lawrence River as the capital of New France.', 'Can you guess the year an explorer founded this city as the capital of a new French colony?', '1608 AD', 1608, 'year', 'In 1608 AD, Samuel de Champlain founded Quebec City on the Saint Lawrence River. It became the capital of New France.', 'Year span 1608-1763 = 155 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'New France (French colonial era)', 'Britain seized Quebec after the 1759 Battle of the Plains of Abraham, and French rule formally ended with the 1763 Treaty of Paris.', 'The city''s name comes from the Algonquin word "kebec," meaning "where the river narrows"; Old Quebec is the only walled city north of Mexico with its fortifications still intact.', 'The Plains of Abraham, site of the decisive 1759 battle between Britain and France, is now a public park overlooking the St. Lawrence River.', 'Canada', 'North America', 'Review - Battle of the Plains of Abraham and the British conquest of New France; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('b64e0795-f8af-5723-b0fa-97d7e20c4137', 'L044', 'location', 'palenque', 'Palenque', 'The Maya called the city Lakamha, meaning "Big Water," for the many streams running through the site.', 'draft', 'Can you find this Maya city once called "Big Water" for its many streams?', 17.4842, -92.0464, 'Palenque', 'This is Palenque, in Mexico. The Maya called it Lakamha, or "Big Water," because so many streams ran through the city.', 'K''inich Janaab'' Pakal I ascended to the throne and transformed Palenque into one of the most powerful Classic Maya city-states.', 'Can you guess the year a king took the throne and made this city one of the most powerful Maya states?', '615 AD', 615, 'year', 'In 615 AD, K''inich Janaab'' Pakal I became king of Palenque. Under him, it grew into one of the most powerful Classic Maya city-states.', 'Year span 431-1952 = 1521 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Classic Maya civilization (the B''aak'' polity)', 'Palenque went into decline amid the broader Classic Maya collapse and was largely abandoned by around 799 AD.', 'The Maya called the city Lakamha, meaning "Big Water," for the many streams running through the site.', 'Palenque''s engineers built the earliest known pressurized water aqueduct in the Americas, channeling water underground to raise it several meters.', 'Mexico', 'North America', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('4b6941bd-f9be-5bd1-8dc3-e16584c370fb', 'L045', 'location', 'nazca', 'Nazca', 'The Nazca Lines comprise hundreds of geoglyphs - animals, plants, and geometric shapes, some over 300 meters long - made by removing dark surface stones to expose the lighter ground beneath.', 'draft', 'Can you find the desert home of huge animal shapes drawn on the ground?', -14.6975, -75.135, 'Nazca', 'This is Nazca, in Peru. Its people made huge shapes, called the Nazca Lines, by clearing away dark stones to show the lighter ground below.', 'The Nazca culture emerged on Peru''s southern coast and began creating its enormous geoglyphs, the Nazca Lines, in the desert pampa.', 'Can you guess the year a culture here began drawing its giant desert lines?', '100 BC', -100, 'year', 'Around 100 BC, the Nazca culture appeared on Peru''s southern coast and began making the huge Nazca Lines in the desert.', 'Year span -100 to 1994 = 2094 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Nazca culture (Early Intermediate Period, southern Peruvian coast)', 'The Wari (Huari) empire expanded into the region and absorbed the Nazca culture by around 650 AD.', 'The Nazca Lines comprise hundreds of geoglyphs - animals, plants, and geometric shapes, some over 300 meters long - made by removing dark surface stones to expose the lighter ground beneath.', 'Because the Nazca Lines are best seen from above, they went largely unnoticed by outsiders until commercial aircraft began flying over the region in the 1920s-30s.', 'Peru', 'South America', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('3f8416a0-7a8a-5e4f-83d3-9d25eece28ef', 'L046', 'location', 'cartagena', 'Cartagena', 'Cartagena became one of Spain''s wealthiest American ports, shipping Andean silver (including from Potosi) to Spain and, notoriously, bringing enslaved Africans into South America.', 'draft', 'Can you find this fortified port that once shipped silver across the ocean to Spain?', 10.4218, -75.5389, 'Cartagena', 'This is Cartagena, in Colombia. It became one of Spain''s richest ports in the Americas, shipping silver from the Andes back to Spain.', 'Spanish conquistador Pedro de Heredia founded Cartagena de Indias as a fortified colonial port.', 'Can you guess the year a Spanish explorer founded this fortified port city?', '1533 AD', 1533, 'year', 'In 1533 AD, Pedro de Heredia founded Cartagena de Indias. It grew into a strong, walled port for Spanish ships.', 'Year span 1533-1821 = 288 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Spanish colonial era (Viceroyalty of New Granada)', 'Cartagena declared independence from Spain on November 11, 1811, with Spanish colonial rule in the region ending definitively by 1821.', 'Cartagena became one of Spain''s wealthiest American ports, shipping Andean silver (including from Potosi) to Spain and, notoriously, bringing enslaved Africans into South America.', 'After a devastating fire in 1569, Cartagena organized what is considered the first firefighting brigade in the Americas.', 'Colombia', 'South America', 'Review - Battle of Cartagena de Indias and the siege during the independence war; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('b97c137a-8a9e-5b60-92d0-d13bd99163a2', 'L047', 'location', 'potosi', 'Potosi', 'At its height in the early 1600s, Potosi was one of the largest and wealthiest cities in the world, reputedly rivaling London in population, its silver financing the Spanish Empire.', 'draft', 'Can you find this mountain town that once rivaled London in size, thanks to silver?', -19.6047, -65.7539, 'Potosi', 'This is Potosi, in Bolivia. In the early 1600s, its silver mines made Potosi one of the largest, richest cities in the world.', 'Spanish colonists founded Potosi after the discovery of massive silver deposits in the Cerro Rico ("Rich Mountain").', 'Can you guess the year settlers founded this town after finding huge silver deposits?', '1545 AD', 1545, 'year', 'In 1545 AD, Spanish settlers founded Potosi after finding huge amounts of silver in the nearby Cerro Rico, or "Rich Mountain."', 'Year span 1545-1825 = 280 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Spanish colonial silver-mining era (Viceroyalty of Peru)', 'Silver output declined through the 18th century as Mexican mines overtook production, and Bolivia won independence from Spain in 1825.', 'At its height in the early 1600s, Potosi was one of the largest and wealthiest cities in the world, reputedly rivaling London in population, its silver financing the Spanish Empire.', 'Potosi''s silver was extracted largely through the mita forced-labor system, which conscripted thousands of Indigenous men annually and caused enormous loss of life from accidents, mercury poisoning, and altitude.', 'Bolivia', 'South America', 'Review - the mita forced-labor system at Potosi''s silver mines caused enormous Indigenous suffering and loss of life; present factually and respectfully.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('6a2f9f6b-08c5-593a-8cb5-14c64fed57cc', 'L048', 'location', 'nan-madol', 'Nan Madol', 'Nan Madol''s name means "the spaces between," referring to the canals separating its 92 artificial islets; it is often called the "Venice of the Pacific."', 'draft', 'Can you find this island city built on 92 tiny man-made islands?', 6.8419, 158.3322, 'Nan Madol', 'This is Nan Madol, in Micronesia. Builders made 92 small islands linked by canals, which is why people call it the "Venice of the Pacific."', 'The Saudeleur dynasty began monumental megalithic construction of Nan Madol as their ceremonial and political capital on Pohnpei.', 'Can you guess the year rulers began building this city''s giant stone islands?', '1180 AD', 1180, 'year', 'In 1180 AD, the Saudeleur dynasty began building the huge stone structures of Nan Madol on the island of Pohnpei.', 'Year span 1100-2016 = 916 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Saudeleur Dynasty (Pohnpei)', 'Around 1628, the warrior Isokelekel overthrew the Saudeleur dynasty, ending centralized rule from Nan Madol and beginning the Nahnmwarki era of decentralized chiefdoms.', 'Nan Madol''s name means "the spaces between," referring to the canals separating its 92 artificial islets; it is often called the "Venice of the Pacific."', 'The massive basalt columns used to build Nan Madol, some weighing many tons, were quarried elsewhere on Pohnpei and transported to the site, likely by raft.', 'Federated States of Micronesia', 'Oceania', 'Review - Isokelekel''s overthrow of the Saudeleur dynasty; present factually without graphic detail.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('12efced3-255a-5399-85a3-bb6bd2743a90', 'L049', 'location', 'tongatapu', 'Tongatapu', 'At its height (c. 1200-1500), the Tu''i Tonga Empire''s influence extended across much of Western Polynesia, including Samoa, Fiji, and Niue.', 'draft', 'Can you find the island that was once the center of a huge Pacific empire?', -21.1353, -175.0422, 'Tongatapu', 'This is Tongatapu, in Tonga. At its height, the Tu''i Tonga Empire based here reached across much of Western Polynesia, including Samoa and Fiji.', 'Ahoeitu became the first Tu''i Tonga, founding the sacred dynasty that grew into the Tu''i Tonga Empire centered on Tongatapu.', 'Can you guess the year the first ruler of a sacred dynasty took power on this island?', '950 AD', 950, 'year', 'Around 950 AD, a man named Ahoeitu became the first Tu''i Tonga. His line of rulers later grew into the powerful Tu''i Tonga Empire.', 'Year span 950-1875 = 925 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'Tu''i Tonga Empire', 'Political power split with the creation of the Tu''i Ha''atakalaua line and later the Tu''i Kanokupolu dynasty (established 1610); the sacred Tu''i Tonga title was formally abolished in 1875 (the last Tu''i Tonga, Laufilitonga, died in 1865).', 'At its height (c. 1200-1500), the Tu''i Tonga Empire''s influence extended across much of Western Polynesia, including Samoa, Fiji, and Niue.', 'Tonga is the only Pacific island nation never formally colonized by a foreign power, retaining its monarchy continuously to the present day.', 'Tonga', 'Oceania', null, 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('4b3b3709-2358-5512-918b-c5a115852707', 'L050', 'location', 'sydney', 'Sydney', 'Sydney was built on the land of the Eora Nation''s clans, who had lived in the area for at least 30,000 years before British arrival.', 'draft', 'Can you find this harbor city where a British fleet built its first colony in a new land?', -33.8568, 151.2153, 'Sydney', 'This is Sydney, in Australia. The land had belonged to the Eora Nation for thousands of years before the British built a colony there in 1788.', 'Governor Arthur Phillip led the First Fleet to establish a British penal colony at Sydney Cove, the first European settlement in Australia.', 'Can you guess the year a British fleet arrived to start the first European settlement in this land?', '1788 AD', 1788, 'year', 'In 1788 AD, Governor Arthur Phillip led the First Fleet to Sydney Cove. It became the first European settlement in Australia.', 'Year span 1788-1932 = 144 years, far over the 15-year threshold.', 'British colonial era (Colony of New South Wales)', 'The Australian colonies federated on 1 January 1901, forming the Commonwealth of Australia with Sydney as capital of New South Wales.', 'Sydney was built on the land of the Eora Nation''s clans, who had lived in the area for at least 30,000 years before British arrival.', 'The Sydney Harbour Bridge, completed in 1932, is affectionately nicknamed "the Coathanger" for its arched steel shape.', 'Australia', 'Oceania', 'Review - British colonization of Australia and displacement of the Eora Nation; present factually and respectfully of Indigenous history.', 'Base Pin/When/Era/Succession/Know/See/bonus-category data from the second 25-Location Subject Pack (web-search-verified, delivered 2026-09-17). Match/Order items freshly researched and web-search-verified (Wikipedia primary, cross-checked) in this session.'),
  ('383c305c-22db-53a7-82c0-0c68faab9bc2', 'P051', 'person', 'ashoka-the-great', 'Ashoka the Great', 'Emperor of the Maurya Empire who unified most of the Indian subcontinent and became a renowned patron of Buddhism after the bloody Kalinga War.', 'draft', 'Where was the capital of this emperor, who turned to peace after a terrible war?', 25.5941, 85.1376, 'Pataliputra (modern Patna)', 'This is Pataliputra, now called Patna, in India. It was the capital of Ashoka the Great, who ruled the Maurya Empire and became a follower of Buddhism after the bloody Kalinga War.', 'Kalinga War and turn to Buddhism', 'Can you guess the year this emperor fought a bloody war that changed the way he thought forever?', 'c. 260 BCE', -260, 'year', 'In 260 BCE, Ashoka fought the terrible Kalinga War. The huge loss of life shocked him so much that he became a follower of Buddhism.', 'Events span 72 years (304-232 BCE), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Maurya Empire', 'Ashoka was succeeded as Mauryan emperor by Dasharatha, and the empire''s central authority progressively weakened until it finally collapsed around 185 BCE when the last Mauryan ruler was overthrown by his own general, Pushyamitra Shunga.', 'Ashoka had his edicts on moral and administrative principles carved onto rock faces and monolithic sandstone pillars across the subcontinent, some standing over 40 feet tall, making them among the oldest deciphered inscriptions in Indian history.', 'He created a corps of officials called dharma-mahamattas who were sent to tour the empire promoting his ethical policies, and he ordered the construction of rest houses, wells, and hospitals for travelers and animals.', 'Maurya Empire (modern India)', 'Asia', 'Review - depicts the brutal Kalinga War and its heavy casualties', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Ashoka'' (birth c. 304 BCE Pataliputra; coronation c. 268 BCE; Kalinga War in his 8th regnal year, c. 260 BCE; death 232 BCE), cross-checked chronology summary on Britannica.'),
  ('2ddee0cd-d96e-5e4b-9ba3-53fe590de869', 'P052', 'person', 'akbar-the-great', 'Akbar the Great', 'Third Mughal emperor of India, who vastly expanded the empire through conquest while pursuing policies of religious tolerance.', 'draft', 'Can you find the capital city this powerful Indian emperor built for himself?', 27.0945, 77.6679, 'Fatehpur Sikri', 'This is Fatehpur Sikri, in India. Akbar the Great, the Mughal emperor who grew his empire while treating people of many faiths fairly, built it as his capital.', 'Coronation', 'Can you guess the exact date this emperor was crowned as ruler?', '14 February 1556', 1556, 'day', 'Akbar the Great was crowned emperor on February 14, 1556. He never learned to read, but he had books read to him daily and remembered them so well that people were amazed.', 'Events span 63 years (1542-1605), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Mughal Empire', 'Akbar was succeeded by his son Prince Salim, who took the Mughal throne as Emperor Jahangir in 1605.', 'Despite being unable to read or write, possibly due to dyslexia, Akbar had texts read aloud to him daily and was renowned for an exceptional memory that let him engage deeply with literature, history, and theology.', 'Akbar was an avid hunter who reportedly kept thousands of trained cheetahs for the chase and personally took part in training the animals himself.', 'Mughal Empire (modern India/Pakistan)', 'Asia', 'Review - includes military conquest and subjugation of kingdoms (e.g., siege of Chittorgarh)', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Akbar'' (born 15 Oct 1542 Amarkot; crowned 14 Feb 1556 at Kalanaur; conquered Chittorgarh 1568, Gujarat 1572-73; died 27 Oct 1605 at Fatehpur Sikri/Agra).'),
  ('0a2c6706-fb26-58cd-bf66-81a143147fc2', 'P053', 'person', 'qin-shi-huang', 'Qin Shi Huang', 'First emperor of a unified China, founder of the Qin dynasty, who standardized the empire and commissioned the Terracotta Army.', 'draft', 'Can you find the huge underground tomb built for the first emperor of a unified China?', 34.3841, 109.2785, 'Mausoleum of Qin Shi Huang (near Xi''an)', 'This is the great tomb of Qin Shi Huang, near Xi''an in China. He was China''s first emperor, and he had a huge army of clay soldiers, called the Terracotta Army, built to guard his tomb.', 'Unification of China / declared First Emperor', 'Can you guess the year one ruler united all of China''s warring kingdoms under his rule?', '221 BC', -221, 'year', 'In 221 BC, Qin Shi Huang unified China and declared himself its First Emperor. He then made one currency, one set of weights and measures, and one writing script for the whole empire.', 'Events span 49 years (259-210 BC), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Qin Dynasty', 'Qin Shi Huang was succeeded by his younger son, installed as the Second Emperor (Qin Er Shi) through a forged decree by officials Li Si and Zhao Gao that forced the rightful heir Fusu to commit suicide; the Qin dynasty collapsed in rebellion within about four years.', 'He standardized weights, measures, currency (the Ban Liang coin), cart-axle widths, and the Chinese script across his newly unified empire, reforms that helped bind together a state previously divided into competing kingdoms.', 'In his later years he grew obsessed with immortality and reportedly died after ingesting mercury-laced elixirs prescribed by court alchemists who believed the substance would extend his life.', 'Qin Dynasty (modern China)', 'Asia', 'Review - unification involved violent conquest, mass forced labor, and repression (book burning)', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Qin Shi Huang'' (born Feb 259 BC Handan; became King of Qin 247 BC; unified China and took the title emperor 221 BC; died 210 BC at Shaqiu), cross-checked with Britannica biography.'),
  ('75ddb42f-6cb7-5591-999d-f87e03f9a61d', 'P054', 'person', 'kublai-khan', 'Kublai Khan', 'Mongol ruler and grandson of Genghis Khan who completed the conquest of China and founded the Yuan dynasty as its first emperor.', 'draft', 'Can you find the city this Mongol ruler made the capital of his new Chinese dynasty?', 39.9042, 116.4074, 'Khanbaliq (modern Beijing)', 'This is Khanbaliq, known today as Beijing, in China. Kublai Khan, the grandson of Genghis Khan, made it his capital after he finished conquering China and founded the Yuan dynasty.', 'Conquest of the Southern Song, unifying China', 'Can you guess the year this Mongol ruler finished conquering China and united it under his rule?', '1279', 1279, 'year', 'In 1279, Kublai Khan conquered the Southern Song and unified China under Mongol rule. The Venetian traveler Marco Polo later spent about 17 years at his court.', 'Events span 79 years (1215-1294), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Yuan Dynasty (Mongol Empire)', 'Kublai Khan was succeeded by his grandson Temur Khan, who became Yuan emperor and nominal Great Khan of the Mongol Empire in 1294.', 'The Venetian merchant Marco Polo reportedly served in Kublai Khan''s court and traveled through his empire for around 17 years, later producing a widely read account that shaped European knowledge of Asia.', 'Kublai Khan launched two large seaborne invasions of Japan, in 1274 and 1281, both of which failed after storms (later mythologized in Japan as the ''kamikaze,'' or divine wind) destroyed much of his invasion fleets.', 'Yuan Dynasty / Mongol Empire (modern China)', 'Asia', 'Review - depicts violent Mongol military conquest of China and failed invasions of Japan', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Kublai Khan'' (born 23 Sept 1215; enthroned Great Khan 15 April 1260 at Shangdu; proclaimed the Yuan dynasty 1271; completed conquest of the Southern Song 1279 at the Battle of Yamen; died 18 Feb 1294 at Dadu), cross-checked with Britannica biography.'),
  ('47588358-7b9c-572a-a20a-7dd4fc978c5a', 'P055', 'person', 'saladin', 'Saladin', 'Kurdish Muslim sultan who founded the Ayyubid dynasty and led Muslim forces to recapture Jerusalem from the Crusaders.', 'draft', 'This sultan led his army to take back a holy city from crusader knights. Where was that city?', 31.7683, 35.2137, 'Jerusalem', 'The city is Jerusalem. Saladin, a Kurdish general, led Muslim forces to recapture it from the Crusaders in 1187.', 'Recapture of Jerusalem', 'When did this sultan''s army recapture a holy city from the Crusaders?', '2 October 1187', 1187, 'day', 'Saladin recaptured Jerusalem on October 2, 1187. He was a Kurdish leader who went on to found the Ayyubid dynasty.', 'Events span 56 years (1137-1193), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Ayyubid Dynasty', 'After Saladin''s death in 1193, the Ayyubid empire was divided among his relatives rather than passing to a single heir, with his son al-Aziz Uthman taking Egypt and his son al-Afdal taking Damascus.', 'Saladin was of Kurdish descent, born around 1137 in Tikrit (in modern Iraq), making him one of the most celebrated Kurdish figures in history.', 'After retaking Jerusalem in 1187, Saladin largely spared the city''s Christian inhabitants and let many who could not pay ransom go free, a restraint that contrasted sharply with the massacre Crusaders had carried out when they first captured the city in 1099.', 'Ayyubid Sultanate (Egypt, with rule over Syria and the Levant)', 'Africa', 'Review - depicts the Crusades, warfare, and the siege of Jerusalem', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Saladin'' and ''Al-Adid'' (born c. 1137 Tikrit; Fatimid Caliphate ended and Saladin became sole ruler of Egypt 1171; Battle of Hattin 4 July 1187; capture of Jerusalem 2 Oct 1187; died 4 March 1193 Damascus).'),
  ('ee5c8002-e5f3-59a9-bdf7-0538c3ca6365', 'P056', 'person', 'wu-zetian', 'Wu Zetian', 'The only woman to rule China in her own right as emperor, founding the short-lived Zhou dynasty during the Tang era.', 'draft', 'This woman ruled an ancient empire as its only female emperor. Where was her capital city?', 34.6197, 112.454, 'Luoyang', 'Her capital was Luoyang, in China. Wu Zetian ruled from there as the only woman ever to be emperor of China in her own right.', 'Proclaims herself Emperor, founds the Zhou dynasty', 'When did this woman declare herself emperor and start her own dynasty?', '16 October 690', 690, 'day', 'Wu Zetian proclaimed herself emperor on October 16, 690. She founded the short-lived Zhou dynasty during China''s Tang era.', 'Events span 81 years (624-705), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Tang Dynasty (Zhou Interregnum)', 'Wu Zetian was overthrown in a palace coup in early 705 and succeeded by her son Li Xian, restored to the throne as Emperor Zhongzong, ending her Zhou dynasty and restoring the Tang dynasty.', 'Before ruling in her own right, Wu Zetian had first served as a low-ranking concubine to Emperor Taizong, then unusually became a consort and eventually empress to his son, Emperor Gaozong.', 'She significantly expanded China''s imperial examination system, opening government office to a wider pool of candidates from outside the traditional hereditary aristocracy and strengthening merit-based recruitment.', 'Zhou Dynasty / Tang Dynasty (modern China)', 'Asia', 'Review - includes political purges, secret police repression, and palace intrigue', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Wu Zetian'' (born 17 Feb 624 Lizhou/modern Guangyuan; became Empress consort 655; regent/empress dowager 683-690; proclaimed Emperor and founded the Zhou dynasty 16 Oct 690 at Luoyang; died 16 Dec 705 at Luoyang).'),
  ('8521b628-3b01-5128-aed2-6320c74d2e5a', 'P057', 'person', 'ho-chi-minh', 'Ho Chi Minh', 'Vietnamese revolutionary leader and founding president of North Vietnam who led the country''s independence movement against French colonial rule.', 'draft', 'This revolutionary leader announced his country''s independence from a capital city. Where was that city?', 21.0285, 105.8542, 'Hanoi', 'The city is Hanoi, in Vietnam. Ho Chi Minh led Vietnam''s independence movement and became the country''s first president there.', 'Declaration of Vietnamese Independence', 'When did this leader declare his country''s independence from French rule?', '2 September 1945', 1945, 'day', 'Ho Chi Minh declared Vietnam''s independence on September 2, 1945. He had spent about thirty years abroad before returning to lead his country.', 'Events span 79 years (1890-1969), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', '20th-Century Vietnamese Independence Movement', 'Following Ho Chi Minh''s death on September 2, 1969, Ton Duc Thang succeeded him as president of North Vietnam, and the state he had founded went on to defeat South Vietnam and reunify the country in 1975.', 'Before returning to lead the Vietnamese independence movement, Ho Chi Minh spent roughly three decades traveling and working abroad under numerous aliases, including stints as a ship''s galley hand and pastry cook in cities such as London and Paris.', 'He was a founding member of the French Communist Party in 1920 and later helped establish the Indochinese Communist Party in 1930, laying organizational groundwork for the independence movement decades before he declared Vietnam''s independence.', 'Democratic Republic of Vietnam (French Indochina, modern Vietnam)', 'Asia', 'Review - covers colonization, war of independence, and armed conflict', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Ho Chi Minh'' (born 19 May 1890 Kim Lien, Nghe An; founded the Indochinese Communist Party early 1930 in Hong Kong; declared Vietnamese independence 2 Sept 1945 in Hanoi; Dien Bien Phu victory 1954; died 2 Sept 1969 in Hanoi).'),
  ('edf04841-9510-5767-98f8-260ba7370319', 'P058', 'person', 'emperor-meiji', 'Emperor Meiji', 'Emperor of Japan whose reign saw the Meiji Restoration, rapidly transforming Japan into a modern industrial and military power.', 'draft', 'This emperor ruled from a palace in a fast-changing capital city. Where was that palace?', 35.6852, 139.7528, 'Tokyo (Meiji Palace)', 'The palace is in Tokyo, Japan. Emperor Meiji ruled from there as Japan rapidly became a modern industrial power.', 'Meiji Restoration', 'When did this emperor''s reign begin transforming his country into a modern power?', '1868', 1868, 'year', 'The Meiji Restoration began in 1868. Under Emperor Meiji, Japan quickly built modern factories, schools, and a strong military.', 'Events span 60 years (1852-1912), far over the 15-year threshold, so players match each event to its year.', 'Meiji Japan', 'Emperor Meiji was succeeded by his son Yoshihito, who took the throne as Emperor Taisho in 1912.', 'Meiji kept almost no personal diary or correspondence and left only a handful of surviving photographs, leaving historians divided over how much he personally directed state affairs versus the oligarchs who governed in his name.', 'Of his fifteen children by various consorts, only five survived to adulthood, reflecting the high infant and child mortality that affected even Japan''s imperial family during his reign.', 'Empire of Japan', 'Asia', 'Review - includes wars (First Sino-Japanese, Russo-Japanese) and colonial annexation of Korea', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Emperor Meiji'' (born 3 Nov 1852 Kyoto; acceded 3 Feb 1867; Meiji Restoration/era change 1868, capital effectively moved to Tokyo Nov 1868; died 29 July 1912 at the Meiji Palace, Tokyo).'),
  ('40e4aea0-2968-56b5-b383-1572c9d23324', 'P059', 'person', 'mansa-musa', 'Mansa Musa', 'The Mansa (emperor) of the Mali Empire whose 1324 pilgrimage to Mecca made him legendary for his extraordinary wealth.', 'draft', 'This emperor ruled a wealthy African empire from a famous city of scholars. Where was that city?', 16.7666, -3.0026, 'Timbuktu, Mali', 'The city is Timbuktu, in Mali. Mansa Musa grew its university with scholars, turning the city into a center of learning.', 'Pilgrimage to Mecca (Hajj)', 'When did this emperor take a famous trip to Mecca that made him legendary for his wealth?', '1324', 1324, 'year', 'Mansa Musa made his famous pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324. He brought so much gold that people still remember him as one of the richest rulers ever.', 'Events span 25 years (1312-1337), well over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', 'Mali Empire', 'Mansa Musa was succeeded by his son Magha as Mansa of Mali, though the empire''s central authority gradually weakened over the following century under less effective rulers.', 'During his reign, Mansa Musa restaffed and expanded the University of Sankore in Timbuktu with jurists, astronomers, and mathematicians, helping turn the city into a major center of Islamic scholarship.', 'He extended Mali''s control over key trans-Saharan trade cities, including Gao and Timbuktu, strengthening the empire''s grip on West African gold and salt trade routes.', 'Mali Empire (modern Mali)', 'Africa', null, 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Mansa Musa'' (birth c.1280, became Mansa c.1312, hajj 1324, death c.1337, Timbuktu scholarship/Djinguereber Mosque, Cairo gold impact) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Musa I of Mali'' (accession 1307/1312, hajj 1324, death 1332/37 noted as uncertain).'),
  ('d04a5d19-6170-5d80-b54a-388e9876cc88', 'P060', 'person', 'shaka-zulu', 'Shaka Zulu', 'The warrior king who founded and united the Zulu Kingdom through military innovation and conquest in southern Africa.', 'draft', 'This warrior king built and ruled his kingdom from a place in southern Africa. Where was it?', -29.3429, 31.2951, 'KwaDukuza, South Africa', 'The place is KwaDukuza, in South Africa. Shaka Zulu ruled from there after uniting many groups into the powerful Zulu Kingdom.', 'Becomes King of the Zulu', 'When did this warrior become king and begin building a powerful kingdom?', '1816', 1816, 'year', 'Shaka became king of the Zulu in 1816. He changed how his army fought by having them use a short stabbing spear called the iklwa.', 'Events span 41 years (1787-1828), well over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', 'Zulu Kingdom', 'Shaka was assassinated by his half-brothers in 1828, and one of them, Dingane, seized the throne and ruled the Zulu Kingdom in his place.', 'Shaka is credited with popularizing the iklwa, a short stabbing spear designed for close combat, which replaced the longer throwing assegai as the Zulu army''s primary weapon and reshaped its battlefield tactics.', 'When shown European firearms, Shaka reportedly judged them inferior to disciplined spear-and-shield infantry for his army''s style of warfare, reasoning that a shooter''s reload time would leave him exposed to a fast charge.', 'Zulu Kingdom (modern South Africa)', 'Africa', 'Review - military conquest and warfare (the Mfecane/Difaqane) are central to his story', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Shaka'' (born c. July 1787 Mthonjaneni, king 1816-1828, capital kwaBulawayo, iklwa spear and ''bull horn'' formation, assassinated 24 Sept 1828 at KwaDukuza by half-brothers Dingane and Mhlangana) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Shaka'' (birth c.1787, reign 1816-28, death 22 Sept 1828 - Wikipedia''s 24 Sept date used as more specific).'),
  ('b372f55f-71c4-5ab1-8343-6b265a8f5a07', 'P061', 'person', 'sundiata-keita', 'Sundiata Keita', 'The exiled prince who defeated the sorcerer-king Soumaoro Kanté and founded the Mali Empire.', 'draft', 'This exiled prince built his empire''s capital after defeating a rival king. Where was that capital?', 11.3797, -8.3842, 'Niani, Guinea', 'The capital is Niani, in Guinea. Sundiata Keita ruled from there after founding the Mali Empire.', 'Battle of Kirina', 'When did this prince win a famous battle that let him found a great empire?', 'c. 1235', 1235, 'year', 'Sundiata won the Battle of Kirina around 1235. As a child he could not walk, but he later rose to defeat a rival king and found the Mali Empire.', 'Events span 38 years (1217-1255), well over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', 'Mali Empire (Founding Era)', 'After Sundiata Keita''s death around 1255, his son Mansa Wali Keita succeeded him as ruler of the Mali Empire and continued to expand its territory.', 'Sundiata reportedly could not walk as a child due to a physical disability and was mocked for it by his father''s other wives, only gaining the use of his legs years later before rising to lead his people.', 'His reign is associated with the Kouroukan Fouga, an oral charter of governing principles that some scholars consider one of the world''s earliest constitutions and which UNESCO has recognized as intangible cultural heritage.', 'Mali Empire (modern Mali/Guinea)', 'Africa', 'Review - founding narrative centers on a war of conquest (Battle of Kirina) and family persecution/exile', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Sundiata Keita'' (born c.1217 in Dakadjalan, exile in Mema, Battle of Kirina c.1235 defeating Soumaoro Kanté, capital at Niani, adopted title Mansa, died c.1255 drowning in the Sankarani River) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Sundiata Keita'' (confirms death year 1255 and role founding the Mali Empire).'),
  ('18f4826b-cc7b-5efe-8e27-e6b8ddc7b977', 'P062', 'person', 'queen-nzinga', 'Queen Nzinga', 'A warrior queen of Ndongo and Matamba who led decades of resistance against Portuguese colonization and the slave trade in Angola.', 'draft', 'This warrior queen once sat down with a foreign governor in a coastal city. Where was that city?', -8.8368, 13.2343, 'Luanda, Angola', 'The city is Luanda, in Angola. Queen Nzinga negotiated there with the Portuguese governor while resisting colonization for decades.', 'Negotiates with the Portuguese Governor in Luanda', 'When did this queen meet with a Portuguese governor to negotiate for her kingdom?', '1622', 1622, 'year', 'Queen Nzinga held these talks in 1622. She even took a new name, Ana de Sousa, as part of her strategy to protect her people.', 'Events span 41 years (1622-1663), well over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', 'Early Colonial Central Africa', 'Queen Nzinga died in 1663 and was succeeded as ruler of Matamba by her sister Kambu, who took the throne under the name Dona Barbara.', 'Nzinga converted to Roman Catholicism at least twice as a diplomatic strategy, taking the baptismal name Ana de Sousa during her 1622 negotiations with the Portuguese governor of Luanda.', 'In her later years she formed a military alliance with the Dutch, who had seized Luanda from Portugal in 1641, using the partnership to continue her decades-long resistance campaign against Portuguese expansion.', 'Kingdom of Ndongo and Matamba (modern Angola)', 'Africa', 'Review - central story involves resistance to Portuguese colonization, the transatlantic slave trade, and warfare', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Nzinga of Ndongo and Matamba'' (born c.1583 Kabasa, became ruler of Ndongo 1624, conflict with Portugal from 1626, conquered Matamba 1631-35, peace treaty 1656, died 17 Dec 1663 at Kabasa) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Nzinga'' (birth c.1582, death 17 Dec 1663 - consistent).'),
  ('96c6b740-badd-5a33-8fd1-eef097ef6c6f', 'P063', 'person', 'haile-selassie-i', 'Haile Selassie I', 'The last Emperor of Ethiopia, a modernizing monarch revered internationally and as a religious figure in Rastafari.', 'draft', 'This emperor was crowned in his country''s capital city. Where was that city?', 9.0367, 38.7514, 'Addis Ababa, Ethiopia', 'The city is Addis Ababa, in Ethiopia. Haile Selassie ruled from there as Ethiopia''s last emperor and became known around the world.', 'Coronation as Emperor', 'When was this emperor crowned as ruler of his country?', '2 November 1930', 1930, 'day', 'Haile Selassie was crowned emperor on November 2, 1930. A few years earlier, he had helped get Ethiopia into the League of Nations by promising to end slavery.', 'Events span 82 years (1892-1974), well over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', 'Ethiopian Empire, Solomonic Dynasty', 'Haile Selassie was deposed in the 1974 military coup and succeeded in power by the Derg junta, which abolished the monarchy in 1975 and established a Marxist-Leninist state.', 'In 1923 Haile Selassie secured Ethiopia''s admission to the League of Nations by pledging to abolish slavery, which still affected an estimated two million people in the country.', 'During a 1924 European tour he adopted forty Armenian orphans who had survived the Armenian Genocide, giving them musical training so they could form his imperial brass band.', 'Ethiopian Empire (Ethiopia)', 'Africa', 'Review - story includes the Italian invasion/occupation of Ethiopia (1936-41) and his violent overthrow/death under the Derg military junta', 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Haile Selassie'' (born 23 July 1892 Ejersa Goro, crowned 2 Nov 1930 at St. George''s Cathedral Addis Ababa, exiled 1936-1941 after Italian invasion, returned 5 May 1941, deposed 12 Sept 1974, died 27 Aug 1975) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Haile Selassie I'' and Wikipedia ''St. George''s Cathedral, Addis Ababa'' for coronation site coordinates.'),
  ('a3239383-0b1c-560d-9b1c-5f1efb29b15f', 'P064', 'person', 'ibn-battuta', 'Ibn Battuta', 'A Moroccan Islamic scholar and explorer whose roughly 30-year journeys across Africa, Asia, and Europe made him one of history''s greatest travelers.', 'draft', 'This traveler set out on his famous journeys from his hometown. Where was that hometown?', 35.7767, -5.8039, 'Tangier, Morocco', 'The hometown is Tangier, in Morocco. Ibn Battuta set out from there and eventually traveled farther than any other explorer of his time.', 'Departs Tangier on His Travels', 'When did this traveler leave home to begin decades of journeys across the world?', '14 June 1325', 1325, 'day', 'Ibn Battuta left Tangier on June 14, 1325. Over the years that followed, he traveled about 117,000 kilometers, more than any other traveler of his era.', 'Events span 65 years (1304-1369), well over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', 'Marinid Sultanate', 'After Ibn Battuta''s death around 1368-1369, the Marinid Sultanate he had served went into a prolonged decline, weakened by succession disputes and territorial losses until its eventual collapse in the 15th century.', 'Ibn Battuta is estimated to have traveled roughly 117,000 km (about 73,000 miles) over his journeys, more than any other explorer of the pre-modern era, including Marco Polo.', 'He served as a qadi (Islamic judge), appointed by the Delhi Sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq and later serving as chief judge in the Maldive Islands.', 'Marinid Sultanate (Morocco)', 'Africa', null, 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Ibn Battuta'' (born 24 Feb 1304 Tangier, departed 14 June 1325, reached Mecca Nov 1325, travels through 1354 including trans-Saharan journey to Mali 1352-54, wrote the Rihla, died 1368 or 1369) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Ibn Battuta'' (confirms birth date and death 1368/69, notes place of death recorded only as ''Morocco'' - some sources say Fez, others Marrakesh; exact city uncertain).'),
  ('f1fac717-de06-5025-964d-96f1aea62288', 'P065', 'person', 'hatshepsut', 'Hatshepsut', 'One of ancient Egypt''s few female pharaohs, known for a prosperous reign of monumental building and expanded trade.', 'draft', 'This female pharaoh built a grand temple in ancient Egypt. Where was it located?', 25.7363, 32.6047, 'Deir el-Bahari, Egypt', 'The temple is at Deir el-Bahari, in Egypt. Hatshepsut built it during her prosperous reign as one of Egypt''s few female pharaohs.', 'Declares Herself Pharaoh', 'When did this woman declare herself pharaoh of ancient Egypt?', 'c. 1473 BC', -1473, 'year', 'Hatshepsut declared herself pharaoh around 1473 BC. She later sent a trading trip that brought back live myrrh trees for Egypt.', 'Events span 21 years (1479-1458 BC), over the 15-year threshold, so mode is subject_to_year.', '18th Dynasty, New Kingdom', 'Hatshepsut was succeeded by her stepson and nephew Thutmose III, who became sole ruler after her death around 1458 BC and later ordered much of her imagery erased from official monuments.', 'She sponsored a major trading expedition to the Land of Punt that returned with 31 live myrrh trees, one of the earliest recorded attempts to transplant foreign trees into Egypt.', 'Later in Thutmose III''s reign, her cartouches and images were chiseled off temple walls and her statues smashed, an act of erasure that nearly removed her from the historical record.', 'New Kingdom of Egypt (Egypt)', 'Africa', null, 'Verified via Wikipedia ''Hatshepsut'' (regent from c.1479 BC, declared herself pharaoh by ~Year 7 of Thutmose III''s reign i.e. c.1473-72 BC, Punt expedition by Year 9/c.1471-70 BC, mortuary temple at Deir el-Bahari, died c.1458/57 BC, tomb KV20) and cross-checked with Britannica ''Hatshepsut'' (reign given as circa 1479-58 BCE, consistent).'),
  ('57c6725f-0c17-59c2-9ab5-965e85b83439', 'P066', 'person', 'atahualpa', 'Atahualpa', 'The last sovereign emperor of the Inca Empire, captured and executed by Spanish conquistadors.', 'draft', 'This emperor was captured by foreign soldiers in a mountain town. Where did this happen?', -7.15, -78.5167, 'Cajamarca', 'It happened in Cajamarca, Peru. Atahualpa, the last emperor of the Inca Empire, was captured there by Spanish soldiers.', 'Capture at Cajamarca', 'When was this emperor captured by Spanish soldiers?', '16 November 1532', 1532, 'day', 'Atahualpa was captured on November 16, 1532. He later offered to fill a large room with gold and silver to win his freedom.', 'Span of 31 years (1502-1533) is well over the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Inca Empire (Tawantinsuyu)', 'Atahualpa was succeeded as a Spanish-installed puppet Sapa Inca by his brother Tupac Huallpa, and after Tupac Huallpa''s death soon after, by another brother, Manco Inca.', 'To secure his release, Atahualpa offered to fill a room roughly 22 by 17 feet to a height of 8 feet once with gold and twice with silver within two months.', 'Although initially sentenced to be burned alive, he converted to Catholicism shortly before his death and was executed by garrote strangulation on 26 July 1533 instead.', 'Inca Empire / Tawantinsuyu (Peru)', 'South America', 'Review - depicts violent Spanish conquest, betrayal, and Atahualpa''s later execution', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atahualpa and en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cajamarca; capture date confirmed as 16 Nov 1532 (Spanish force arrived 15 Nov, ambush occurred 16 Nov). Execution date 26 July 1533 cross-checked on same page.'),
  ('ae2aee63-41a3-5dc5-972e-09a4db0796b1', 'P067', 'person', 'pachacuti', 'Pachacuti', 'The Inca emperor who transformed a small Andean chiefdom into the expansive Inca Empire (Tawantinsuyu).', 'draft', 'This emperor rebuilt his people''s small city into a great imperial capital. Where was that city?', -13.532, -71.9675, 'Cusco', 'The city is Cusco, in Peru. Pachacuti rebuilt it as the capital of the growing Inca Empire.', 'Accession as Sapa Inca', 'When did this ruler become emperor and begin turning his small kingdom into an empire?', '1438', 1438, 'year', 'Pachacuti became Sapa Inca in 1438. He turned a small Andean kingdom into the vast Inca Empire, known as Tawantinsuyu.', 'Span of 33 years (1418-1471) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Founding of the Inca Empire', 'Pachacuti was succeeded as Sapa Inca by his son, Topa Inca Yupanqui, who continued the empire''s rapid territorial expansion.', 'He rebuilt Cusco as an imperial capital, redesigning the city so each of the empire''s four suyu (quarters) had its own sector centered on the road leading to that province.', 'Most archaeologists credit Pachacuti with commissioning the royal estate of Machu Picchu and establishing the Qhapaq Nan road network stretching from Quito to Chile.', 'Inca Empire / Tawantinsuyu (Peru)', 'South America', 'Review - reign built on military conquest of neighboring peoples and forced resettlement (mitma) policies', 'Verified via britannica.com/biography/Pachacuti-Inca-Yupanqui and en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachacuti. Birth year disputed (c.1391-1418, Cusicancha Palace, Cusco); reign 1438-1471 and death 1471 (per Rowe) confirmed on both sources.'),
  ('799beae9-132b-56b5-8f28-5b8cb979a14f', 'P068', 'person', 'tupac-amaru-ii', 'Tupac Amaru II', 'An indigenous kuraka (chief) who led a major rebellion against Spanish colonial rule in Peru.', 'draft', 'This rebel leader was executed in a city''s main plaza. Where was that city?', -13.5164, -71.9782, 'Cusco', 'The city is Cusco, in Peru. Tupac Amaru II was executed there in the Plaza de Armas after leading a rebellion against Spanish rule.', 'Execution in Cusco''s Plaza de Armas', 'When was this rebel leader executed after his uprising against Spanish rule?', '18 May 1781', 1781, 'day', 'Tupac Amaru II was executed on May 18, 1781. He had taken his name to claim descent from the last Inca ruler and lead his people''s fight.', 'Span of 39 years (1742-1781) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Spanish Colonial Peru, Bourbon Era', 'After Tupac Amaru II''s execution in 1781, leadership of the uprising passed to his cousin Diego Cristobal Tupac Amaru, who continued fighting until Spanish forces crushed the remaining rebels by early 1782.', 'Born Jose Gabriel Condorcanqui, he adopted the name Tupac Amaru II to claim descent from the last Inca ruler and legitimize his leadership of the rebellion.', 'On 16 November 1780 he decreed the abolition of slavery for Black people in the territory under his control, one of the first such decrees issued in Spanish America.', 'Viceroyalty of Peru (Peru)', 'South America', 'Review - depicts an anti-colonial uprising and his brutal public execution (drawn and quartered) by Spanish authorities', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T%C3%BApac_Amaru_II and en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rebellion_of_T%C3%BApac_Amaru_II. Birth name José Gabriel Condorcanqui, born c.1742 in Surimana; rebellion began 4 Nov 1780 at Tungasuca; captured 6 April 1781; executed 18 May 1781 in Cusco.'),
  ('3a9d71b3-6db1-5979-9c80-b8edc29fdcf8', 'P069', 'person', 'jose-de-san-martin', 'José de San Martín', 'A South American general and statesman who led independence campaigns in Argentina, Chile, and Peru.', 'draft', 'This general helped declare independence for a South American country from its capital. Where was that capital?', -12.0464, -77.0428, 'Lima', 'The capital is Lima, in Peru. Jose de San Martin declared Peru''s independence there after leading campaigns across South America.', 'Declaration of Peruvian Independence', 'When did this general declare independence for Peru?', '28 July 1821', 1821, 'day', 'San Martin declared Peruvian independence on July 28, 1821. Years earlier, he had led his army across the Andes Mountains in six separate columns to surprise his enemies.', 'Span of 72 years (1778-1850) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Age of Revolutions in South America', 'After resigning as Protector of Peru following his 1822 Guayaquil meeting with Simon Bolivar, San Martin left South America and Bolivar took over leadership of the campaign, completing Peru''s liberation.', 'He engineered the 1817 Crossing of the Andes, splitting his army into six columns over separate mountain passes to surprise royalist forces defending Chile.', 'Before joining the independence movement, San Martin spent over two decades as an officer in the Spanish army, fighting in North Africa and the Peninsular War against Napoleon.', 'Viceroyalty of the Rio de la Plata (Argentina)', 'South America', 'Review - career involved multiple wars of independence with combat casualties (Battles of Chacabuco and Maipú)', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_de_San_Mart%C3%ADn. Born 25 Feb 1778 Yapeyú; Battle of Chacabuco 12 Feb 1817; Peruvian independence declared 28 July 1821 in Lima; died 17 Aug 1850 in Boulogne-sur-Mer, France.'),
  ('a7e54d1e-0c81-53b0-b0d3-0ccde462ee40', 'P070', 'person', 'pedro-ii-of-brazil', 'Pedro II of Brazil', 'The second and last Emperor of Brazil, who presided over decades of political stability and modernization.', 'draft', 'This emperor ruled his country from a coastal capital city. Where was it?', -22.9068, -43.1729, 'Rio de Janeiro', 'The city is Rio de Janeiro, in Brazil. Pedro II ruled from there as Brazil''s second and last emperor.', 'Coronation', 'When was this emperor crowned to begin his long reign?', '18 July 1841', 1841, 'day', 'Pedro II was crowned on July 18, 1841. He later became an early fan of photography and is seen as Brazil''s first photographer.', 'Span of 64 years (1825-1889) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Empire of Brazil', 'Pedro II was deposed in the 15 November 1889 military coup, which abolished the monarchy and established the Republic of Brazil under its first president, Deodoro da Fonseca.', 'He became an early adopter of photography, acquiring a daguerreotype camera in 1840 and is regarded as Brazil''s first photographer.', 'In 1875 he was elected to the French Academy of Sciences, an honor previously granted only to Peter the Great and Napoleon Bonaparte.', 'Empire of Brazil (Brazil)', 'South America', 'Review - reign included the bloody Paraguayan War (1864-1870) and slavery persisted in Brazil until 1888', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_II_of_Brazil. Born 2 Dec 1825, Rio de Janeiro; crowned 18 July 1841; Lei Áurea (abolition) signed 13 May 1888 by Princess Isabel as regent while Pedro II was in Europe; deposed 15 Nov 1889; died 5 Dec 1891 in Paris.'),
  ('dad230da-2ffa-5063-9f65-ca5f87ab0ddd', 'P071', 'person', 'bernardo-o-higgins', 'Bernardo O''Higgins', 'A military and political leader who secured Chile''s independence and served as its first head of state.', 'draft', 'This leader helped declare his country''s independence from its capital city. Where was that city?', -33.4489, -70.6693, 'Santiago', 'The city is Santiago, in Chile. Bernardo O''Higgins helped declare Chile''s independence there and became its first head of state.', 'Declaration of Chilean Independence', 'When did this leader declare independence for his country?', '12 February 1818', 1818, 'day', 'Chile''s independence was declared on February 12, 1818. O''Higgins did not use his father''s last name in public until after his father died in 1801.', 'Span of 64 years (1778-1842) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Age of Revolutions in South America', 'O''Higgins was forced to abdicate as Supreme Director in January 1823 and was succeeded by his former ally Ramon Freire, amid continuing political instability.', 'He was born out of wedlock to Ambrosio O''Higgins, a Spanish colonial official of Irish descent, and did not publicly use the O''Higgins surname until after his father''s death in 1801.', 'As Supreme Director he abolished titles of nobility in Chile, a reform that alienated the landowning aristocracy and contributed to his eventual overthrow.', 'Captaincy General of Chile (Chile)', 'South America', 'Review - depicts the Chilean War of Independence and associated combat', 'Verified via britannica.com/biography/Bernardo-OHiggins and en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilean_Declaration_of_Independence. Born probably 20 Aug 1778, Chillán; Supreme Director 1817-1823; Battle of Chacabuco 12 Feb 1817; independence declared/approved by O''Higgins 12 Feb 1818 at Talca; died in exile in Peru, October 1842.'),
  ('43fc08ad-9b6c-5c5a-aca0-3620c4612519', 'P072', 'person', 'kamehameha-i', 'Kamehameha I', 'The Hawaiian aliʻi nui who conquered and unified the Hawaiian Islands into a single kingdom.', 'draft', 'This island ruler united a chain of islands into one kingdom from his home town. Where was it?', 19.64, -155.9969, 'Kailua-Kona', 'The town is Kailua-Kona, in Hawaii. Kamehameha I ruled from there after uniting the Hawaiian Islands into one kingdom.', 'Unification of the Hawaiian Islands', 'When did this ruler finish uniting the Hawaiian Islands into one kingdom?', '1810', 1810, 'year', 'Kamehameha I united the islands in 1810. After sparing some fishermen who had attacked him, he made a fair law called the Law of the Splintered Paddle.', 'Span of 61 years (1758-1819) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Kingdom of Hawaii', 'Kamehameha I was succeeded by his son Liholiho, who became Kamehameha II, with his father''s favored wife Kaahumanu serving as a powerful regent (kuhina nui).', 'After being wounded and later sparing fishermen who had attacked him, he issued the Law of the Splintered Paddle, a humane decree still cited in Hawaii''s state constitution.', 'He used Western cannons obtained from the ship Fair American, along with foreign advisors Isaac Davis and John Young, to help defeat rival chiefs and unify the islands.', 'Kingdom of Hawaii (Hawaii, USA)', 'Oceania', 'Review - unification was achieved through warfare, including the Battle of Nu''uanu where hundreds of warriors were driven off the Pali cliffs', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamehameha_I. Born c.1758 (sources vary 1736-1761), Kohala, Hawaii Island; Battle of Mokuʻōhai 1782; Battle of Nuʻuanu (Oahu) 1795; peaceful unification with Kauai completed 1810; died 8 May 1819 at Kamakahonu, Kailua-Kona.'),
  ('0e2b6abc-5ca1-542e-99af-93af79a30ab3', 'P073', 'person', 'queen-liliuokalani', 'Queen Liliuokalani', 'The last reigning monarch of the Kingdom of Hawaii, deposed in an 1893 coup.', 'draft', 'This queen ruled her island kingdom from a royal palace. Where was that palace?', 21.3059, -157.8583, '''Iolani Palace, Honolulu', 'The palace is ''Iolani Palace, in Honolulu, Hawaii. Queen Liliuokalani ruled from there as the last reigning monarch of the Kingdom of Hawaii.', 'Overthrow of the Kingdom of Hawaii', 'When was this queen''s kingdom overthrown in a coup?', '17 January 1893', 1893, 'day', 'The Kingdom of Hawaii was overthrown on January 17, 1893. Before becoming queen, Liliuokalani had written the famous song ''Aloha ''Oe.''', 'Span of 79 years (1838-1917) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Kingdom of Hawaii', 'Following her overthrow, a Provisional Government gave way to the Republic of Hawaii in 1894, which was then annexed by the United States in 1898.', 'Before ascending the throne, she composed the widely known song ''Aloha ''Oe,'' now one of Hawaii''s best-recognized musical compositions.', 'After a failed 1895 attempt to restore her to power, she was imprisoned in ''Iolani Palace, where she wrote her memoir, ''Hawaii''s Story by Hawaii''s Queen,'' published in 1898.', 'Kingdom of Hawaii (Hawaii, USA)', 'Oceania', 'Review - depicts the illegal overthrow of a sovereign nation and Liliuokalani''s subsequent imprisonment', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liliuokalani. Born 2 Sept 1838, Honolulu; became queen 29 Jan 1891; overthrown by the Committee of Safety 17 Jan 1893; imprisoned at ''Iolani Palace 16 Jan 1895 - forced to abdicate 24 Jan 1895; died 11 Nov 1917 at Washington Place, Honolulu.'),
  ('df9e841c-7ae6-56b9-985f-31a91158683b', 'P074', 'person', 'te-rauparaha', 'Te Rauparaha', 'An influential and militarily powerful Maori chief of Ngāti Toa, dubbed "the Napoleon of the South."', 'draft', 'This powerful chief defended a small island stronghold. Where was that island?', -40.85, 174.9333, 'Kapiti Island', 'The island is Kapiti Island, in New Zealand. Te Rauparaha, a Maori chief nicknamed ''the Napoleon of the South,'' held power there.', 'Defense/Conquest of Kapiti Island', 'When did this chief defend his island stronghold from rival forces?', '1824', 1824, 'year', 'Te Rauparaha defended Kapiti Island in 1824. He also composed the famous haka ''Ka Mate'' while hiding from enemies and surviving.', 'Span of 89 years (1760-1849) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Musket Wars Era, New Zealand', 'After his 1846 arrest and exile by the colonial government, leadership influence within Ngati Toa passed largely to his son Tamihana Te Rauparaha, who guided the iwi toward peace with the Crown.', 'He composed the haka ''Ka Mate'' while hiding from pursuing enemies on an island in Lake Rotoaira, celebrating his escape and survival.', 'In 1824 he led Ngati Toa to a decisive victory over a large opposing coalition at the Battle of Waiorua on Kapiti Island, securing his people''s control of the region.', 'Aotearoa (New Zealand)', 'Oceania', 'Review - involved in the violent intertribal Musket Wars, including the 1843 Wairau Affray controversy', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Te_Rauparaha. Born c.1760s, Kāwhia; led the Ngāti Toa migration/conquest south from 1822; won a major battle for Kapiti Island in 1824; imprisoned without trial 1846-1848 by Governor Grey; died 27 November 1849, Ōtaki.'),
  ('536a3dc1-484e-588c-8d91-4d66972f4ac0', 'P075', 'person', 'king-george-tupou-i', 'King George Tupou I', 'The first king of modern, unified Tonga and founder of its constitutional monarchy.', 'draft', 'This king united his island nation and was crowned in its capital. Where was that capital?', -21.1393, -175.2049, 'Nuku''alofa', 'The capital is Nuku''alofa, in Tonga. King George Tupou I was crowned there and became the first king of unified, modern Tonga.', 'Coronation and Promulgation of the Constitution', 'When was this king crowned and his country''s first constitution announced?', '4 November 1875', 1875, 'day', 'George Tupou I was crowned on November 4, 1875, when Tonga''s constitution was announced. Years earlier, in 1852, he had combined the sacred and royal titles of his land into one kingship.', 'Span of 96 years (1797-1893) far exceeds the 15-year threshold, so items match to specific years.', 'Kingdom of Tonga', 'George Tupou I was succeeded upon his death in 1893 by his great-grandson, George Tupou II, after his own son and grandson had predeceased him.', 'In 1852 he merged the sacred Tu''i Tonga title with secular kingship and became head of the Free Church of Tonga, then administered by Reverend Shirley Baker.', 'In 1865 he invited missionary James Egan Moulton to Tonga to found Tupou College, establishing formal Western-style education in the kingdom.', 'Kingdom of Tonga (Tonga)', 'Oceania', 'Review - unification followed decades of Tongan civil war, and his reign involved suppressing rival chiefs', 'Verified via en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Tupou_I and constituteproject.org (Tonga 1875 constitution text). Born 4 Dec 1797 at Tongoleleka, Ha''apai; became Tu''i Kanokupolu/ruler of unified Tonga 4 Dec 1845; formally crowned and promulgated Tonga''s Constitution 4 Nov 1875; died 18 Feb 1893, Nuku''alofa.');
